      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>ALaYs McDOUGALL'S Pategt SELF-RAISING FLOUR T H lETB PS T. The Examiner, Saturday December 21, 1907.</p>
        <p>I improved upon. Reading the must interesting portion 1 of the last issue of the " Examiner," that is,the Voice i of— call it what you like, my faculties for discern- ing and dissecting became engrossed by the confusive 1 effusion of the redoutable ttr. Charles E. Parker. 1 The statement which, if it had never been uttered, ' would have saved Me the trouble of writing 1 this letter, was " but for the country to nationalise the railways pt anything like the labave figures woald probibly mean an enormous loss even if they could run them cheaper, which I gravely doubt." I will deal with the latter portion of the statement first. I can hardly credit Mr. Parker letting such a statement creep in. But let us see what facts teach us. in Britain there are 50 general managers of railways connected with about 250 Boards supervised by 3,000 directors. Under a State system, such as there is• in Germany, there would be an official head of the railways, a minister of the Crown with one central Board of Management, and perhaps a third of the present number of lesser clerical and financial centres. We would save annually 3,000 directors' fees amounting to about £1,500,000, and at least half of the clerical expenses. When, the Prussian Government took over the railways they reduced the clerical centres from 80 to 21 and saved about £1,000,000 per annum by this change alone. The services of the great army of commercial travellers could be dispensed with, for under the State there would be no competitive can- vassing. The sum saved in this way alone would be I very large. Here is an example of what goes on at present. In the town of Middleabrough, the centre of the North of England iron trade, there are seven drummers, with seven offices and seven boys in them. The offices and salaries must cost each company at least £.150 per annum. This means £1,750 is use- lessly spent in that one tons In the town of Leicester, no till some years ago, there was one sh )p for selling railway tickets, that of Thomas Cook and Sons. A new railway, the Great Central went through Leicester and that railway opened a ?shop for the sale o! tickets, putting it beside Cook's. Instantly the Great Northern, the Midland and the London and North-Western opened shops near by. Tkese shops are in the heart of the town, they will cost in rent and taxes from £2OO to £250, and, reckoning the cost of wages at only £l5O each, there is nearly £2,000 thrown away in one town. This same waste goes on in every large town in the IKingdom to much the same extent. Belfast has eight ' shops in the Royal Avenue —the highest rented shops lin the town. Closely connected with this is the waste which competitive advertising entails under our present system. In addition to the official time-tables which give sufficient information to the public, we have endless posters on public hoard- ings, bills far distribution, advertisements in magazines and newspapers, and many forms of the sante thing. Under public ownership all this could be avoided, and therefore the railways run cheaper, I could fill columns of space upon this point, but this will, I think suffice for, the present. I find I have no space left to deal with the first portion of Mr. Parker's statement, but I .must ask the Editor to allow me to discuss in next week's issue,what appears to me the most important of the two points raised by Mr. Parker. —Yours, etc.,</p>
        <p>Committee of Management. namely, the Rev. John Yonge, Mr. T. B. CArter, Mr. J. S. Hill, Mr. R. T Fairclough and Mr. LR. Rowson. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. DAKIN seconded and the motion was carried. The CHAIRM A.N said that death had been busy during the year, and they 1,1:1 lost qnvi-al friends of the institution. Mr. M0n,,,, who c ;he district, had</p>
        <p>been one of the best friends of the institution.</p>
        <p>Meacr,. P. 1. Frio- land ipurod</p>
        <p>W. PM-At. 1-tylands, and J. Lnne:.</p>
        <p>It was decided to add the following names to the list of managers :—The Rev.G. A. Lejeune, and Messrs. F. H. Harris' Lister-7,-n. J. NT-wn Iturray, Arthur Browne, John Upson, A. Nwholls, a tue Mayor, as Mayor of the Borough. Mr. THOMPSON asked for his name to be removed from the list of managers, as he no longer lived in Warrington, but the meeting decided not to accede to his request, the Chairman paying a high tribute to Mr. Thompson's work, and saving that they would be con- tent if he could only come amongst them once a year. They did. not wish to see his name taken off the list. If he could come to ‘Varrington oftener, they would, of course, be all the more pleased. . A vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman, on ' the proposition of Mr. T. S. STEICL, seconded by Mr. THOMPSON.</p>
        <p>LABOR OMNIA VINCIT,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>VOICE OF LABOUR.</head>
        <p>THE VOICE OF LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Edited by John Workman. Readers of this column are invited to assist in. making it interesting and helpful to social workers by contributing themselves. Correspondents must write on one side of the pq per only, and endeavour to make their letters and notes short and to the point. A nom-de-plume may be used, but the rea! name of the writer should be forwarded in confidence to the Editor of the column. M.S.S. must be addressed to John Workman, " Examiner " Office, EVarrington and should be sent in not later than Wednesday in each week.</p>
        <p>JOIIN WOKIA. REPLIES TO MR. P. E PARKER'S LETTER AND CIiALLENU-E.</p>
        <p>" The ethics of Socialistn arts. closz-4y allied to the ethics of _Christianity, if not identical with then."--</p>
        <p>_ " Encyclopvedia Britannica." " 1 believe the things that Socialism stands for. it is God's way out of the wilderness into the Promised Land. It is the very marrow and fatness of Christ's gospel. It is Chriatianity applied. Oh, that I were young again, and it should have my life," —The late Francis E. Willard, President World's Women's Christian Temperance Union. "I seriously believe that Christianity is the only foundation of Socialism, and that a true Socialism is the necessary result of a sbund Christianity."— Frederick Denison Maurice. "Is it any wonder that men like Kiniksley and Maurice, while they toiled for voluntary association and co-operation, should repudiate this soul-deadening, energy-restricting scheme." Mr. Charles E. Parker. Mr. C. E. Parker's previous letters have been like the curate's egg—good in parts ' • his stories have been amusing but his arguments hopelessly befogged. He accuses me of stringing together columns of words and indulging in vauge generalizations and he says I seem to be incapable of giving a direct answer that is free from ambiguity and that a plain man can under- stand. I have tried hard to make my replies as simple as possible, and have even gone so far as to quote other writers to emphasize my remarks. Mr. Parker should take more time over them. I said at the beginning that in the Socialistic state I advocated "each shall give according to his ability,and each shall receive according to his needs" He will see thpt the assumption is that "each shall give according to his ability." That, at any rate, is perfectly clear to any man who can read the English language. Even Mr. Parker must admit that there must be assumptions !rfanything is to be proved. I began with something, for it is impossible to begin with nothing. ThA chances are that nine hundred and ninety-nine ordinary people out of a thousand would see that as clearly as the proverbial pikestaff. Not so Mr. Parker ; he is the odd man. In his reply, wherein I am charged with ambiguity, he states : "Take the first section of the answer, 'The first essential is that each shall . give according to his ability.' This raises the whole difficulty because this condition which John Workman says is essential and takes for granted, will not exist under his Socialistic. scheme." , (The italics are mine.) That is about the richest thing I have ever read. To take the basis of the scheme I advocate and to say that it does not exist under it, either shows an ignorance of the scheme itself, or of the difference in the meaning of an " assumption" and an "answer." Until Mr. Parker has re-read my statement, further argument is futile. I am not content to go on "stringing columns of words" together—pleasant though it may appear to be —just for the fun of it. There are men, John Workman for instance, who are "an impediment in the progress of the nation and race." There ~are others, Mr. Charles E. Parker for instance, " who have a clear idea of not only what is prong but of how it can be put right." That is the burden of the second portion of his letter. lam just a "wee bit" afraid he flatters both of us. . .</p>
        <p>And xhat a challenge!</p>
        <p>Then comes the challenge! Just fancy! Listen ! " Let John Workman and nineteen of his associates make a common pool of their earnings. Let a committee of three or four be appointed by the twenty to manage this common fund and divide it according to the reeds of the members." His friend Alderman Bennett is to make a yearly investigation of the payments in and out, and for every year the Association holds together he is to give £lOO, say £5O to the Warrington Nursing Association, and £5O to the Infirmary. Now, I ask any ordinary individual who has an atom of what is commonly called " commonsense " to refrain from laughing just for a moment and to con- template such a challenge, coming as it does from an educated gentleman who professes to know a great deal about Socialism. Will you walk into my parloUr, said the Spider to the Fly? There are many ways of withdrawing from an argument. Some choose a graceful exit ! . One cannot but recognize that it. is the natural con- sequence of good acts to command approbation. I hope the Warrington Nursing Association and the Warrington Infirmary will not be the poorer by my refusal to accept such a blundering infringement of God's law as Mr. Parker would impose upon twenty of his poor, deluded brethren. The best laws, so we are told, are the interpreters of Nature's intentions, and Nature holds in her own hand the rewards and penalties, whether we bear, or whether we forbear' I personally prefer "letting in the light " ; and there are others who would profit by opening their eyes. A Merry Chrismas to all ! JOHN WORKMAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>TRADES COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>THE TRADES COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Sir, —No doubt "Onlooker" believes he has done a valuable service to the trade unionists of Warrington, in pointing out the shortcomings of our local Trades Council. As a Trade Unionist, I say "Thank you for nothing, 'Onlooker.' It was quite unnecessary for you to point out what is quite obvious to all the intelligent trade unionists in the town." I think I am justified in saying this, for in conversa- tion with trade unionists of all •shades of political belief, Conservative, Liberal and Socialist, I have beard views expressed showing that they regard the Trades Council much in the same light as "Onlooker" does, only they are satisfied with it, and he is not. The majority of the trade unionists tolerate it, as a kind of trade union hobby that is comparatively inexpensive. To many of them it has this redeeming feature, it serves the purpose of shelving those lum- bers who are always displaying their activity by upseteng the complacency of the officials in their respective branches in trying to" speed them up." It is a convehient " House of Lords" in which to place these political agitators who went to commit the trade unions political action, and it must be confessed, it works admirably, for although the Rules permit political action, in a sense, there is something in the "atmosphere" of the Trades Council that stifles enthusiasm</p>
        <p>One point" Onlooker" overlooks. The Warring- ton trade unionists do not want political representa- tion. Whatever else is ambiguous respecting their, aims that is clear. They have allowed the only' organisation that could accomplish that end (the i local Labour Representation C.nnmittee) to dwindlo awAy. So the Warrington Liberals and Conservatives need have no fear that the Trades Council will wrest seats from them, for even imagining the Trade Unionists giving Trmission and funds to fight elections (and this is putting imagination to a severe strain), we should need men of greater ability in managing affairs, and capable of mere strenuous work, than our Trades Council delegates possess, in order to successfully fight the old order. I wonder how many of the delegates think,, as " Onlooker " evidently does, that it is part of the funetions of a Trade Union to tike action in such questions as Housing, Education, Temperance, the Smoke Nuisance, or even the enforcement of the Factory laws. _ _</p>
        <p>Although it may not be clue], it is certainly a use- less proceeding. to " flog a dead horse," and I hope if" Onlooker ' wants exercise for his superfluous energy, he will choose a less gruesome subject on which to expend it. But, if he intends taking in hand the work of transforming the Warrington Trades" Council, I wonld advise him to take it at " time" rats, instead of "piece-work.' If ever he accomplishes it, nay, before then, he will deserve a "long repose." Meanwhile, the majority of the • trade unionists' of Warrington will look on with equanimity at the doings of the Council and its critics, and amongst them I shall be an interested</p>
        <p>" SPECTATOR. '</p>
        <p>THE NATIONALISATION OF RAILWAYS.</p>
        <p>A. few weeks ago the sagacious, if indiscreet, Mr. Parker objected to the appellation applied to the columns known as the " Voice of Labour," " The Voice of Socialiern," in his estimation, would more correctly describe the aforesaid. It seems to me, Mr. Editor, that the present title and the one suggested by Mr. Parker, would be not at all precise. A more appropriate name than the "Voice of John Workman and Mr. Parker" could not, I think, be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
