        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"The Black Bishop."</p>
        <p>"The Black Bishop" is a favourite domestic play which never fails to please and its production at the Royal Court Theatre this week by Mr. G. M. Copping's company has been cordially welcomed. The popularity of the work is not to be wondered at. There are all the elements of an absorbing plot, of which the author —Barrie Williams -has made the best use, the play being very brightly written and abounding in dramatic situations. The" Black Bishop" is not a very ecclesi- astical personage; there is nothing distinctive of the church dignitary about him. In the first act we have him in love with pretty Ruth Oldbury—and an ardent young lover he is too—but he resigns her, though quite unwillingly, because she conceives it to be her duty to marry Simeon Strathcoat, an arch-villian whose intention it is to go through the ceremony to reap monetary advantages and then to cast her aside. As a matter of fact, he is already married and his wire Marian appears on the scene just before the time fixed for the wedding. An angry altercation ensues and in a fit of temper Strathcoat kills her. _ No-one has witnessed the tragedy, which isAfterwards passed off as an accident, and the marriage with Ruth Oldbury proceeds. Strathcoat, however, seeks to evade ita legality'by concocting a story that the dead woman is his wife's twin sister Kate Marleigh, and allowing Kate, an ambitious, heartless woman, to mas.querade as his wife. Poor Ruth is turned adrift but is dis- coiered by the "Black Bishop," who, being a man of spirit, quickly sets to work to redress her wrongs. By applying a little forcible persuasion he extorts a full confession from Strathcoat, and Kate Marleigh is so incensed at the destruction of her schemes that she challenges Strathcoat to a duel and, after an exciting bout, inflicts fatal injuries. The last act witnesses the death of the libertine, the full vindication of Ruth's position, the downfall of Kate Marleigh,and the beginning of an era of true happiness for Ruth and the Bishop. We have nothing but praise for the way in which the play is presented, for the company is a highly capable one. Mr. Stephen Langton makes an excellent Black Bishop, young and impulsive, yet ever animated by the loftiest motives,his pourtrayal being powerful and touching to a degree. Miss Constance Medwyn takes the dual role of Marian Strathcoat and Kate Marleigh and one could not wish for a more vigorous and realistic conception of the cynicism and brutality of which Kate is the personification. Miss Ruth Shepherd is a winsome Ruth Oldbury, her version being marked by 'pathos and fitting restraint. Mr. H. C. Lancely is splendid as the blunt Captain Keilcrift, having fully realized the humour of the part, and matters are decidedly lively whenever be is on the stage. Mr. A. Bernhardt Turner has the unpopular part of Simeon Strathcoat, and does full justice to it. Mr. Sidney Rennef, as Frank Bruttlebut, landlord of the Dragon Coffee House, and Miss Annie Brunette, as his wife, are a typical good:hearted old couple, who also contribute a great deal to the laughable gide. Little Hefty King, —who, we believe is Miss Elsie Jones, of King-street, Warrington—does remarkably well as Ruth Oldbury's child. The mounting of the play is on an elaborate and attractive scale.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Patrons of the Theatre will learn with pleasure that the management have secured Mr. Louis Calvert's principal company in " Sunday " as a special attraction next week. A powerful cast, headed by Miss Gertrude Roswell, who has been specially secured from the Haymarket Theatre in the name part, includes such well-known names as Mr. Leonard Pagden in the dual role of Colonel Brinthorpe and his younger brother Arthur ; Mr. James Skea as " Towzer " ; Mr. Aylmer J. Williams as" Davy " ; Mr. Alec Alves as " Jacky ; Mr. James Hussy as " Lively " ; Mr. Ernest Douglas in the part of "Tom Oxley," which he has played since the piece was first produced ; and Miss Rosalind Ross. whose charming " Mrs. Naresby " has won universal approval. The scenery and effects are on a lavish scale, being careful copies of the original West End produc- tion, and will be found quite in keeping with the import- ance of the play.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>WHY ENDURE SORE THROAT?</head>
        <p>WHY ENDURE SORE THROAT?</p>
        <p>- Peps are an Ideal Throat Tonic.</p>
        <p>Are you one of those unfortunate people who suffer. from sore throat whenever you catch cold, or whenever bad weather sets4n ! That is because you have had a sharp attack of sore throat, some time or other, which was never radically cured. It has left a weak- ! ness behind ; your' throat is " touchy " ; the delicate 'is thickened a trifle; your voice is not quite so membrane clear and vigorous. Perhaps it is not so bad as that, but you suffer from a slight huskiness every time the weather changss, or your throat feels weak and relaxed. The best possible treatment is a prompt course of Peps, the wonderful medicine compressed into handy tablet form. Peps are a true thmat tonic: soothing, invigorating, antiseptic, and germ-destroying. ' They are totally different to cheap lozenges awl drug-laden cough-mixtures. Mr. C. Ellis, 19, Lime Grove, Bideford, a member of the 4th V.B. Devon Regimental Band, writes : -" I suffered greatly with a sore and in flamed throat, for which I tried many remedies, but nothing did me any good until I tried Peps, which quickly cured me. A friend who has long been troubled with asthma and had caught 3 bad cold on it, took Peps on my recommenda- tion, and he says that they are the only thing that has given him any real relief. I have recommended Peps to all my chums." - -</p>
        <p>Make-sure you get the genuine article by seeing the short distinctive title— Peps on the wrapper, on the box, and on ever ir tablet. Sold only in handy metal boxes, at Is. lA. and 2s. 9d. of all chemists.</p>
