      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>YEAR'S THRIFT.</head>
        <p>A YEAR'S THRIFT.</p>
        <p>PROSPERITY OF THE WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>SAVINGS BANK.</p>
        <p>Introduction of Home Safes.</p>
        <p>Another successful year's work was under ,review at the annual meeting of the trustees and managers of the Warrington Savings Btnk which was held at the Bank, Buttermarket-street. on Tuesday morning. Mr. William Long presided and there were also present : Revs.C.Harvey Cook and John Yonge; and Messrs. John Geddes, Charles White, William Tinsley, William Richardson, J. Rymer Young, J. W. Hayward, John Taylor, J. S. Hill, George Thompson, Herbert Woods, George F. F. White, T. S. Steel, J. Longland, G. Dakin, C. Jackson Holmes and W. A. Proud (actuary).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>TEIG REPORT.</head>
        <p>TEIG REPORT.</p>
        <p>• In their ninetieth annual report the Committee of management stated :—ln the Ordinary Department 85:1 accounts have been opened or renewed and 523 closed, leaving 15,175 open- accounts; in the Special Invest- ment Department there are 290 open accounts; together a total of 15,465 accounts. In each Department pro- gress has been made, the Head Office and Branches all showing an increase in the number of accounts and in money. The Special Investment, Department has again been the most successful one, X 16,725 Gs. 2d. having been added to its funds. It is highly gratifying to the Committee to find this Department is being so largely used by those depositors whose accounts have reached the limit allowed in the Ordinary Department. '['he total amount now due to the depositors is £107.587 11s. 7d., an increase for the year of £18,005 is. 4d. This is the first time in the history of the Bank that the total funds exceed £400,000. During the year the Committee have purchased a number of home safes. This is a new departure, an effort being made to introduce a system of home saving, so that the smallest sums may be savod and subsequently brought to the Bank. The Committee regrets the loss to the institution of Mr. F. Monks, who has left the district, and also regrets to record the deaths of Messrs. J. C. Ridgway, W.e R. Neil, L. W. Reynolds, and the Rev. E. J. Willoocks.</p>
      </div>
