      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Sacred Concert.</head>
        <p>Sacred Concert.</p>
        <p>Following on the Parr Hall concert of last week the Band of the 2nd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment gave a sacred concert on Sunday in the Hippodrome. kindly plaoed at the disposal of the executive by the proprietor, Mr. Pi. gsonaghten. There was a very large audience, the fine and comfortable theatre being filled from floor to coiling. Although the programme was entirely monopolised by the band the pieces per- formed were so agreeably diversified in character that no feeling of monotony was experienced in a musical evening which was quite evidently enjoyed by one and all. Nor were the pieces by any means entirely sacred. Indeed the finest examples of the band's capacity to inter- pret the works of the great composers were undoubtedly such secular items as the iuiscellineous selection from Mendelssohn's works and the " Tannhauser "overture. i Here the combination known as the" military band" ! did all that was possible in the way of an artistic inter- pretation of such pieces having regard to the strict limitations of the instruments comprised in it. For at : the best it must be admitted that the higher reeds are a very poor substitute for the violins in the florid decora- tion of the climax to which "Wagner. works up the Pilgrim's Chorus. On the other hand the special array of trombones that were requisitioned to emphasise the melody and to give greater solidity to its magnificent harmonies must he acknowledged as both highly effec- tide and ingenious. The " Tannhanser " overture so gr-iatly delightei the monster audience who listened to it with wrapt attention that the concluding part of it was obliged to he repeated in compliance with insistent demands. The " Samson and Delilah " selection deserves also special mention for the opportunity it afforded to a number of soloists of showing exectitant and artistic abilities of a high order. The scent "Softly awakes my heart" in which a melody of gr-iat breadth arid beauty falls to the lot of the cornet soloist with subordinate counterpoints of great 'melodic interest for the euphonium atid other instruments deeply seated in the harmonio centre, was worthy of all praise,inasmuch as it largely realised that fe -.ling of emotional exaltation which is properly an attribute of much of Saint-Saen's work as a com- poser. Several pieces such as the " Angelus " (Massenet), "Shandon Bells " and " Les Cloches " (Ptertridge) gave opportunity of bringing in a chime of ei rht tuoular bells which were very prettily and effectively used in connection with the wind instru- ments. tinder the conductorship of Mr. Andrew Harris the hand seemed quite at home, being ahlv ind safely guided by him thr.ough many tricky musical pith Ils.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>SOUTH LANCASHIRE BAND.</head>
        <p>SOUTH LANCASHIRE BAND.</p>
        <p>Visit to the Parish Church.</p>
        <p>Sunday morning witnessed a Service which will rank</p>
        <p>as memo-rable in the history of the Parish Church. The full band of the 2nd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, which has been visiting this district in the interests of the Regimental Cottage Homes, attended. and, in combination with the choir of St. Elphin's, interpreted the whole of the musical part of the service. The reputation of the band and the great impression created by a similar service at the church•in the earlier part of this year sufficed to attract a crowded congrega- tion' almost every available inch of space being occu- pied. The Mayor and Mayoress- and many prominent townspeople were present. It is due to the band to say that the service gained much in dignity and impressive- ness by the conscientious and efficient work of the instrumentalists. The music was characterised by devo- tional intensity and when in the triumphal passages the instruments exerted their full strength and the stirring</p>
