s. The first was "Triumph and on the Congregational football ground on Saturday 1 Thanksgiving" (Partridge)—a composition vividly when he slipped and fell, 'sod broke two bones. He was i depicting the joys of victory in war, which culminated taken to Dr. Latham's surgery and medically attended. j in a verse of the well-known hymn "Now thank we all</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>COPYRIGIrt</head>
        <p>COPYRIGIrt</p>
        <p>HOW TO LISTEN TO "THE MESSIAH."</p>
        <p>By John Crowdy.</p>
        <p>(FROM " C.A.SSELL'S FAM ELY MAGNZINE.")</p>
        <p>At this season of the year it is a habit of English people—and a most praiseworthy and salutary one—to hear a performance of oratorio, and generally of the greatest of oratorios, Handel's " Messiah." Whether all who go to a performance of this oratorio make the best of the opportunity may perhaps, without persunap- tion, be doubted. To appreciate adequately a great musical work, some little study of its scope and salient features is required, and it is with a view of setting forth these, in such a form that all who know something of music may follow me—and many who know nothing of music, but have good powers of intelligent apprecia- tion—that I pen these few notes. In breadth and interest of subject, there is only one work of man's genius equal with Handel's oratorio, "The Messiah." This is Milton's poem " Paradise Lost " ; and to the great poetical work of Milton, Handel's great musical work is fairly a parallel. The redemption of the human race is its subject ; the Redeemer Hits self its hero.</p>
        <p>Speaking generally, the listener to "The Messiah " may take as his guide to the significance of the music the signification of the words. The music is through- out, and with no exception, word-painting. Not. except rarely, a painting of single words or small phrases treated apart for their context, but a laying on of vivid and truthful musical colour to the sentences so admirably compiled for illustration. Consequently, the first and parvading rule, in listening to a performance of "The Messiah,' is to grasp the meaning of the words. Let us briefly examine some of the principal musical numbers in the light of this rule ; let us see how the musican has used his many and beautiful resources to enhance their meaning.</p>
        <p>The overture is a vigorous and dignified prelude, fitly introducing the work, but the listener need not strain his attention in endeavouring to discover in it more than a general fitness. In No. 2, the recitative" Comfort ye," we feel our- selves at once in a religious atmosphere. Hopeful repose breathes in every phrase. The first entry of the voice, with a simple musical sentence, requiring no exertion in the singer to produce, is itself a stroke of poetry. At "The voice of him that crieth " there is, as the words demand, a slight access of animation.</p>
        <p>, "Every valley," No. 3, is an example of Handel's word-painting; at" The crooked straight," and other points, the literal sense of the words is elegantly hinted at in the configuration of the musical phrases. One of the enjoyments in hearing an oratorio is the first entry of the chorus. The first burst of a mass of voices has an effect which can only be realised once in the same evening ; here it is employed to bring before the hearer the joyous prophecy," And the glory of the Lord shall be revealed." Alto voices announce the theme ; these are followed at once by the whole body of singers, the" subject" first heard from the altos being now assigned to the basses. When the tenor voices have extended the phrases to the words " shall be revealed " the first theme of the chorus is before the hearer. Let us devote a few moments to the analysis of this number ; it is an example of study which will enable the reader to trace the design of others for himself. Three sentences make up the words, and each sentence has its own apropriate treatment. First comes the bright, buoyant "And the glory, the glory of the Lord shall be revealed," propounded and repeated as just noticed; its first simple half is then interlaced with its more donated second portion ("shall be revealed"), and the voices are brought to a period. Nothing is more noteworthy throughout the oratorio than Handel's careful observance of what has been called the logic of pause. No writer of English, no historian, no poet was ever more careful to sectionise, paragraph, and punctu- ate, than the great musician who composed "The Messiah." There is a "full stop" in the music at the word " revealed" • and then comes the second theme "And all flesh shall see it together." Here mark the nature of the musical passages ; there is a new tone in them, an air of confident, reiterated assertion. But mark still more what follows, that grand monotonic passage in firm, asseveratinc, notes, each 9. bar long, "For the mouth of the Lord'hath spoken it." This is the third sentence, verbal and musical, of the chorus, distinct and characteristic; but when it has once been uttered the other two sentences come back and play around it ; the joyous" And the glot y of the Lord," the positive "And all flesh shall see it," interweave themselves with the massive monotonic passage, and the choral effect is raised to a climax, vo ith which this, the first chorus of the oratorio, effectively ends. In "Thus saith the Lord," we have, at " I will shake," and other points, more sense-painting. Still more illustrative, perhaps, is Handel's setting "But who may abide," breaking out from larghetto to a fiery prestissinto at the second sentence, "For he is like a refiner's fire." Wonderfully dramatic is this air, and the spirit of the prestissinto section is carried on into the chorus •‘ And He shall purify." In this latter the hearer will experience, for the first time in the oratorio, the full effect of incessant choral animation ; the four voice detachments are put through a series of active and brilliant manceuvres, such as only a master could devise.</p>
        <p>" 0 Thou that tallest," is a most religious melody, the effect of which will be heightened if rendered in a proper spirit by a contralto singer of skill and rever- ence. In "For behold darkness.,' the listener must mark the suggestiveness of the orchestral accompani- ment, which is, however, more fully de 'eloped in "The people that walked," where Mozart has added parts for the reed instruments, which, creeping about in chromatic passages between theparts written by Handel for the stringed instruments and the voice, produce a</p>
        <p>cold,graping-effect, marvellous in its suggestiveness of "darkness."</p>
        <p>The prophetic section of "The Messiah "comes to an end with that great creation the chorus "For unto us a _ _</p>
        <p>child is born."- So marvellous is this, that attention must again be demanded for a few lines of detail in description. Grace of form, dramatic force, and con- structional development are all alike here exemplified. A few light sentences for the trebles open the musical dialogue; these the tenors repeat, the trebles adding</p>
        <p>, joyous floriated variation; the alt.o voices enter, then the basses; but instead of proceeding to a climax, the voices gradually drop away, in detached phrases, leaving the field for the present clear for a second theme. The tenors propound it in martial rhythm, "And the government shall be upon His shoulder." While this is being echoed bY other parts suddenly there arises a sort of storm in the band parts ; the violins raise a wind of excitement, amidst which the voice parts, hitherto moving separttely, gather together in a mass, and, marching as one. proclaim the decree, "And His name shall be called Wonderful—Counsellor—the Mighty God—the Everlasting Father; " and lastly, in a reposeful cadence whenever it comes, "The Prince of Peace." Now recurs "For unto us," a different voice part propounding it; the other parts join in as before, but in more rapid succession the martial phrase " And the government" again follows, and a second climax upon the titles" Wonderful—Counsellor" is attained—but in a new key. A third time the same recurrence, the same hurrying up of supporting voices, the same proclama- tory shouts ; and a fourth time—and in the fourth recurrence a marvellous effect of accumulation by com- pression. The basses lead the fourth announcement, '• For unto us " ; but in a moment, and not separately as before, but altogether, the other three parts ara hurried up : the for ner material is tumultuously tossed about for a few moments in new directions, the storm among the violins breaks out earlier than before, and a tremendous Passage for the bass voices leads to the fourth proclamation by united shouts of the Messianic titles; the whole subsiding with, as before, a cadence upon the words...," Prince of Peace." There are few things in art more wonderful than the chorus "For unto us " in " The Messiah." At the" Pastoral Symphony," which now comes, we experience one of the most effective contrasts known in</p>
        <p>a work of art. The magnificent elaboration to which the great choras jest described works up is hushed ; and here we have panted. in softest tones, the calmest atmosphere, the deepest and easiest repose. The listener should mark the idiom of pipe-music exemplified in the "Pastoral Symohony," the principal feAtur t of this being holding notes, or "drones," in the bass. The next few numbers describe the birth of Christ by a gradually accumulating series of eff,ects. Nothing could be simpler than the cornmeneernenNf the recita- tive" There wera shepherds." Rut listen, as the second section arrives, to the arpeggio figure in the orchestril accompaniment exciting expectancy; and mark how it is placed in the high part of the compass of the instru- ments to suggest an mrial effect. In the recitative "And the Lgel said," will he noticed increased animaiion, attained by decisive modulation; in the suoceeding nein- ber, " And suddenly." revives the Delia effect or high- placed accompiniment. These features lead up to another remarkable and beautiful chorus, " Glory to God," which, short and simple As it is, employs several elements of a descriptive effect, and forms a complete tone-picture. The base of its descriptiveness is a eontrast between the heavenly and the earthly. For the first sentence, "Glory to God in the highest," the higher voices, and high instrumentation are used ; for the second, the men's vo;ces enter alone, in unison, changing to octaves at the words "on earth." Some alternation and combination of thehigh and low follow; and the chorus comes to a remarkable end by the bass instruments being taken out of the accompanitneet, and the instrumentation gradually raised in pitch and diminished in power, as if to suggest the return to heaven of the angels. Every bar of this chorus has its intention, and fulfils its office in impressing is picture upon the intelligent listener. Not to dwell upon the brilliant joyousness of "Rejoice greatly," or the tender beauty of " He shall feed His flock," let us pass on to some of the features of the second part of "The Messiah." The exquisite grief expressed in the music of "Ele was despised," needs no comment but the words to which it is set: as the number proceeds a tone of indignant animation sets in,the orchestral accompaniment becomes unquiet, and the mind of the devout and intelligent</p>
        <p>listener is worked up to a sense of the bitter, self- reproaching tone of another wonderful chorus," Surely He bath borne our griefs." Liston here to the poignant dissonances at " He huh borne oar griefs " ; look at, and listen for, the A fiat of the basses against a G in the alto at " He was wounded " ; the E flat, further on planted by the treble voices, at " wounded," against the tenor D; and other dissonatioes, enforced in intensity by a violent orchestral accompaniment; and mark how the whole appropriately subsides into a fine fugue at the words, " And with His stripes we are healed." A few words must suffice to hint to the hearer "how to listen "to the next succeeding numbers. Listen. to the short chopping phrases of the taunting chorus," He trusted in God," delivered first by the bass voices ; listen, in "Thy rebuke," for the solemn yearning harmonies of the accompaniment and the short sobbing phrases of the voice part : and study here, as it marches,the ever-incseasing intensity of the modulation. Mark the tone of hope in the noble air, " But Thou didst not leave ;" and note in" Lift up your heads." the same elements of effect as in the already described "Glory to God," ; in "Why do the nations," the rude agitation of the accompaniment, appropriate to the words : in "Let us break," the jerked passage of short detached notes, the snappish reiterated quavers at" and cast away their yokes from us." and the staccato ending ; observe, also, the suggestion of powerful action in the strongly marked accompaniment to "Thou shalt break them."</p>
        <p>We come now to one of the glories of music, the Hallelujah Chorus." ro prompt a hearer "how to listen " to this is a task which can only be successfully accomplished with the exercise of earnest attention on the part of the listener. In a certain sense the " Hallelujah Chorus," like most of Handel's choruses, speaks for itself ; yet there are few things which reveal more beauty on analysis. No prompting can supply in the hearer a want of power to feel its beauty ; but given the" soul "to perceive its majesty, its splendour, and its almost military yet celestial tone, and a few words descriptive of its plan may do much to enhance its effect to the listener.</p>
        <p>After only three bars of bright orchestration the voices enter altogether, scith a group of joyovs phrases born of the word to which they are sung, "Hallelujah." Ever and anon throughout the chorus these phrases reappear, and as they begin it,so they end it. Presently, in unison of all voices and instruments,black on a white ground (so to say), comes the sentence," For the Lord God omnipotent reigneth." Five times the bold sen- tence is repeated, now by one set of voices, now by another, the exclamatory" Hallelujahs "play around it with gradually increasing animation, and presently the first section of the chorus ends in a tumult of instru- mental activity. A few bars of majestic harmony carry the words, "The kingdom of this world," &amp;c., and then the second section of the chorus commences with the imposing phrase, " And He shall reign for ever and ever," assigned to the basses. Taken up in turn by the other voices, it is gradually, like a former subject, sur- rounded with ejaculations to the words " for ever " "and ever," till the voice parts and orchestra again meet in full tumult ; presently comes the last piece of new material, the majestic phrase in monotone, "King of kings, and Lord of Lords." This passsge, announced without cover of any kind, in all its naked sublimity, rises gradually, step by, from D to G above the stave, emphasised by sound of trumpet, and as it slowly rises the previously heard ejsculations of "Hallelujah "sur- round it, their harmony changing as the monotonic phrase shifts its seat upwards, and their utterance only ceasing, after splendid reiteration, to enable all four voice parts to unite, for the first time in the words " Ring of kings, and Lord of lords." Mark then how the already foreshadowed effects accumulate upon the ; the men's voices give out the monotonic phrase, " King of kings," on the key-note,a pitch at which they can exert the greatest strength, while the other parts throw around it the ejaculations, "For ever," " For ever,"" Hallelujah ! " " Hallelujah ! " ; the bold phrase " And Tie shall reign" re-appears ; the companies of the choral forces rally up and begin to move in massive steps together, till at length, completely uniting, they send forth four simultaneous " Hallelujahs," and a sublime moment of silence intervenes before the final, broad, prolonged" Hallelujah " with which, as it began, this truly magnificent creation ends. More ingenious things have been written by musicians than the "Hallelujah Chorus" in "The Messiah," but nothing more masterly. Effectively to prolong a work which would seem already to have exhausted the resources of musical climax was in its turn a victory for the great composer of " The Messiah," in the third and last part of which are still things to be listened to. There are, in truth, two entirely new elements of effect—the use of the unaccompanied vocal quartette in the numbers "Since ' by man," and " For as in Adam," and the trumpet solo. The listener to the two vocal numbers just mentioned will hear a few chords which constitute the essence of what is deeply solemn and ecclesiastical, and will well repay examination by the eye, or at the pianoforte, before attending a p.?rformance of "The Messiah." The bright, thrilling elect of the solo trumpet, introduced at " The trumpet shall sound," needs no prompting to its appreciation. Great, indeed, is the master who can do so much in the first and second part, and reserve so much for the third.</p>
        <p>The final number of "The Messiah," the chorus 'Worthy is the Lamb," and its sequel " Amen," might employ the writer's pen for yet another page, but he must forbear. The first is a magnificent prelude, the second an unparalleled cadence. No reverential listener should leave the peformance while this grand stream of reiterated " Amen " is rolling to its stately close, or relax his attention while the giant Handel plays with that rugged, nervous theme, first led forth by the bass voices. Masterly elaboration,needful for the extension of this number to a scale and dignity worthy of what has pre- ceded, is shown in the progress of this tumult of great " Amens ;" and after a bar of imposing silence, "The Messiah " ends with the simplest expression known to music, the" perfect cadence."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A " v. OLDHAM "A." 'rbst4</p>
        <p>These teams met at Wilderspool before a large orowd on Saturday and an interesting game was witnessed. The early stages of the game went in favour of the local side and a dashing effort by Landor on the left wing was only just checked in time by Dixon. Several times the Oldham custodian saved:his line and after Lees had opened out the game, and the visitors had threatened the home goal, Jordan beat off the attack, and a movement initiated by Daintith, and taken part in by Isherwood and Cartwright, saw Lloyd badly obstructed, and a try was awarded. Oldham attacked after this, Hewlett and Civil each trying hard to break through. Jordan, how- ever, maintained his excellent form, and the " Wire- platers "once more taking up a strong position,a clever effort by Daintith resulted in another try. Preston failed. The Warrington men played a vigorous game, and with the advantage of the wind were nearly always on the attack, an d near the interval Forster landed a penalty goal, so that the home team led at half-time by a goal and two tries (eight points) to Oldham, niL Turning round. Oldham were almost over after five minutes' play, Cartwright effecting a really fine save. At this stage one of the Oldham players was injured and had to !ewe the field. The only score during this half was a penslty goal to the visitors. The feature of the game was the brilliant defence of Jordan. Final score: Warrington, one goal twa tries (eight points) ; Oldham, one goal (two points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WINTER BRINGS MM.</head>
        <p>WINTER BRINGS MM.</p>
        <p>SuffereJ 5-asoni Nur, Cured by Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>Winter-eczema is the worst skin-scourge kn.,wn. Mrs. Frances Wakefield, of 6, Hastings- Place, Stratton, Cirencester, suffered with it for no less thin .19 years. Her case seemed hopeless, but Zam-Bnk again rising superior to all has at last been the memspf ending her misery and restoring to her that priceless b ice -a healthy skin.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Wakefield, who is 5G years .Nf age, was interviewed by a local reporter, and said : " I b^gan to suffer with the dreadful winter eczema whoa I was l 5 years of age. It used to come oa every winter. The joints of my arms and knees, as well as my shins, were covered with a thick white scale, which dropped off and was replaced by other scales periodically. "It bas been hereditary in our family, but I have suffered worse than any of the others. I could not find anything to cure me. I have been under some of the best doctors, and some years ago I had to go to the Infirmary at Gloucester, where I was attended by three head physicians. I stayed for two months, but their ' cure '-was only temporary. For years I used some stuff that eased me a bit, but this winter it did not seem to do it any good. The disease was worse than ever. At last I thought I would try Zam-Buk. When I first put the balm ota I could not detect much improvement, but I kept on with it, and got a second box. By this time I could see that the dreadful complaint was dying away, arid the pain and irritation got less and less. I continued the dressings for several weeks, encouraged all the time by steady improvement, and now I am com- pletely cured." ion cannot afford to neglect the fire bigns of roughness and soreness so ready to appear at this time of the year. Zarn-Buk, the world's most marvellous skin cure, is sold by all chemists at is. 11d. or 2s. 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>.C 650 FOR THE LOSS OP A LEG.</head>
        <p>.C 650 FOR THE LOSS OP A LEG.</p>
        <p>Damages to the amount of £650 were awarded to a telephone fitter in the High Court on Thursday for injuries received through being thrown out of a lift while at work at the Hotel iNfetropole, London. His right leg was so crushed that it had to be amputated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
