      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Royal Christmas Shopping.—Tt is said that Queen Victoria only once entered a shop throughout her long reign. Queen Alexandra is not quite so exclusive, and has lately visited one or two of the leading Christmas bazaars in London, although more for the purpose of seeing the displays, than for making purchases. These with rare exceptions are selected by the King and Queen at, Buckingham Palace. where, before Christmas, several rooms are set apart, in which shopkeepers, hold- ing the Royal appointment, can display a selection of their wares. In all cases the goods have to be marked in plain net cash prices, and if these are at all exces- sive, the articles are not likely to be retained. Jewellery and fancy articles figure largely in the Royal purchases but the Queen also buys large quan- tities of toys and confectionery, and she also has selections of goo&amp; sent. from some of the leading shops in Copenhagen. The King and Queen set the example of keeping Christmas in 'the good old-fashioned way, with all the Yuletide customs, and, in addition to gifts to relatives and friends, there are Christmas trees libr tenants, dependants and servants, all of whom receive presents of some snbstantial and useful kind. The King and his Entertainers.—Th, King usually sends presents at Christmas to those who have enter- tained his Majesty during the year. Those who have had the honour to be his hosts and hostesses are all , remembered. and also many others. including members I of the theatrical Profession. Sir C. Wyndham and Miss Mary Moore who player' in " Still waters run deen "at the recent" comment' "performance at Windsor Castle have both received handsome presents. A" command" to play before the Court is regarded as the greatest honour in the profession, but it is usually fi very , expensive one for the ector-menagers, as only trifling ont-of-pocket expenses are paid by the King's secretary. Once only, during the reign of Queen Victoria was an excuse-offered for not complying with a " command " to perform at, Windsor Castle. That was by the late Mr. E. T. Smith. who afterwards bad the Italian Opera but for many years the Court refused to natronise his theatre after he had declined to take his company to Windsor. The Children's 'Faith in Santa Clans.—Santa Clans is in some countries a genial old bachelor, and in others accompanied by his wife. but, all over 'Europe he is looked for in some or other form, by the iittle folks on Christmas Eve. There are, however, some people with little of the Christmas spirit, who think it wrong to talk to little boys and girls of any such nerson who comes down the chimney on Christmas Eve, and fills their stocking with toys and all good things. They say it would be better to give the children the toys without the deception, as they call it, but if we are to take away the chill's faith in Santa Clans, we must also take away the fairy tales and the story books, and in fact take no small part Of childhood from the children. Christmas Superstitions.—Many are the pretensions to prophesy associated with Christmas. If a hoop falls off a cask or a light is allowed to die out on Christmas Eve, there will be a death in the family ere long. If the fire burns brightly on Christmas morning prosperity may be expected, and this will be encouraged if you shake the tablecloth in the roadway after dinner. A green yule has an evil reputation, perhaps more so than it deserves but there is certainly some wisdom drawn from experience, in the old saying "a warm Christmas a cold Easter." The prophetic almanacs foretell an unusually terrible lot of crisis for 1908, hut there is an ancient aphorism, that "a clear and bright son on Christmas Day foretelleth a peaceable year and plenty." Royalties Abroad.—The Duke and Duchess of Connaught are to leave at the beginning of the New Year for Malta, the headquarter of the Duke's new military command. His duties will extend from Gibralter to Egypt, and the Duke and Duchess will be away for nine months of the year. The Duchess leaves London with much regret, more especially as Princess Patricia will spend most of her time at home, paying only short visits to her parents. Several other members of the Royal Family are wintering on the shores of the sunny Mediterranean. The Princess Royal, with the Duke of Fife, and their daughters, are spending several weeks at Gibralter, residing at Europe Cottage, where they stayed last March. Gibralter has not a great reputation as a sanatorium, but it appears to agree with the Princess Royal, who has not been very well again lately. Princess Henry of Battenberg accompanied by her son Prince Leopold, has gone to Algiers. The Princess is to make a stay of several months in the South, and will visit her daughter, the Queen of Spain, before returning home. The Dangerous Hatpin.—Two recent accidents caused by the point of a hatpin, bring the danger of wearing such accessaries forcibly before us. Upon one occa- sion, a woman in a London theatre, about to thrust a hatpin through her hat, pushed the business end into the eye of another woman who happened to be leaning forward to speak to her neighbour. The latest reports are to the effect-that there is little hope of the eye being saved. The other accident occured in a train, when a woman dropped her purse, and an unfortunate man stooped to pick it up. As generally happens, the woman stooped down also, and the man raising his head quickly, encountered the point of a hatpin, which struck his eye. Less serious results are expected in this letter case, but the man bad the narrowest possible escape. It is difficult to see how this really dangerous practice can be done away with, but at least we can one and all do our best for our fellow creatures in seeing that the points of our hatpins are buried away safely in some portion of our headgear. Reversing the Order of Things.—Many of the smartest evening wraps would give the uninitiated the idea of being worn inside out, for whereas the coverings we have long been accustomed to have shown linings so light, ()I-lighter than the outside fabric, the newest specimens are in white, or very pale tints, and lined with some deep and striking huge. The notion is not an altogether happy one, as, apart from appearances, one runs the risk of • the excessively brown linings leaving traces of colour upon white, cream, or delicately tinted gowns. Arms and Sleeves.—Leading dressmakers are omitt- ing sleeves, when " creating " ball gowns, the idea being that the material draped about the shoulders, and over- hanging the arm, is all-sufficient. Fringes are frequently called into use on this particular purpose, sometithes built of tiny flowers, or foliage, chenille, or beads. The floral fringes are however, perhaps best, and such a mode needs of course a pretty arm. Happily for those who patronize the "no sleeve idea" our arms are usually much more presentable above than below the -elbow, and as the latter portion must be covered with the long glove we are less likely to experience shocks than in the days of the fashionable afternoon elbow sleeve. Concerning Buttons.—Buttons in days gone by were considered from the useful rather than the ornamental point of view, but we have changed all that. and now expend as much time and attention upon the choice of -each set its upon the gown or wrap they are to decorate. Among Christmas and New Year Gifts, sets of buttons</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>BILIOUSNESS IN WINTER.</head>
        <p>BILIOUSNESS IN WINTER.</p>
        <p>Chas. Forde's Bile Beans Bring Lasting Relief to an Ailing Mother.</p>
        <p>The danger of indigestion and biliousness developing into serious winter sickness cannot be over-estimated. Pleurisy, pneumonia, influenza, and other equally dreaded complaints all take root and thrive on weakened stomach, liver, and bowels. To ensure freedom from epidemics and a winter of robust health, you need Chas. Forde's bile beans, the medicine that has no equal for banishing sickness and giving tone and vigour to the entire system. Mrs. H. L. Cator, of Ruse Cottage, Fundenhall, Norwich, said to a " Norwich Mercury "reporter: —" For many years I suffered severely from indigestion and biliousness. After meals I was sick and dizzy and had such violent pains in the stomach that I would lie on the floor, rolling and writhing in agony. The attacks became so had at last that I thought it would be a relief to die,for life was a misery. I was dread- fully weak and thin, and if I walked only a short distance from home it was all I could do to find strength to crawl back. Even in bed my little share of sleep was broken by dreams, and in the morning I woke weary and unrefreshed. "I tried many remedies, but nothing brought me relief until I took Chas. Forde's bile beans. From the first the pain and sickness were less severe, and as I persevered my appetite improved, and my sleep became regular and undisturbed. Finally the bilious attacks ceased altogether, and since then I have not had the least sign of pain." Still Cured Two Years After.</p>
        <p>It is almost two years since Mrs. Cator gave her testimony, and to prove that Chas. Forde's bile beans have effected a complete and lasting cure she has just volunteered this confirmation— "I am still in splendid health, and I cannot recommend Chas. Forde's. bile beans sufficiently."</p>
        <p>What Chas. Forde's bile beans did for Mrs. Cator they will do for you. But when buying be sure you get the genuine "Chas. Forde's"— " the kind you have always bought."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>MESSRS. PETER WALKER &amp; SON, LTD.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. PETER WALKER &amp; SON, LTD.</p>
        <p>Annual Concert.</p>
        <p>IIYPZRCPI4.,I,:7ICMTISAI</p>
        <p>; The Parr Hall was crowded on Monday night, the occasion being the annual concert in connection with the above firm and their band. The event had been greatly looked forward to by the employees of this firm with the hope of the concert being the most successful one, and their anticipations were realised. In the words of an employee, it was" the best one we had ever had." The concert commenced with a march entitled " Fallen Heroes " (Round) by the Brewery Band, which was immediately followed by "Veronica," an overture by Greenwood which was also rendered in good style. After these a trio—" Turn on old time" (Wallace)—was rendered to great advantage by Madame Walker and Messrs. Cottrell and Harris, whilst two songs (a)" Down in the deep" (Hatton) and (b) " Lighterman Tom " (Squire) reflected great credit on Mr. Hamilton Harris, who was loudly and deservedly applauded for his contribution. Mr. Prank Crawford created great merriment with his humorous sketch "The charity bazaar" and gave as an encore another sketch entitled " The small-pox", which was equally amusing. Mr. J. W. Cottrell after- wards interpreted in a very pleasing manner "Ever so far away" (Adams), whilst Madame Annie Walker sang artistically in "It was a dream" (Cowen). A humorous duet "The twins," which was sung by Messrs. Cottrell and Crawford kept the audience in a fit of laughter whilst after the interval the brewery band delighted the company with a selection entitled " Reminiscences of Wales " by Shipley Douglas. " Drum taps" (J. Arlie Dix) was rendered in a manner that won golden opinion whilst Mr. Frank Crawford again delighted the audience with a humorous railway story entitled" The 11.69 express." A duet from " Maritana." entitled "Of fairy wand" (Wallace) was rendered by Madame Annie Walker and Mr. Hamilton Harris whilst Mr. J. W. Cottrell was deservedly encored for his song entitled "A farewell" (TAM' e) and gave in response "My pretty Jane." After Madame Annie Walker had sweetly rendered the song" Daffodils are here" (Landanßonathl), Mr. Frank Crawford gave another capital humorous selection. The brewery band brought the proceedings to a close by the rendering of" Scottish Gems " (Roete) and the National Anthem and the audience went away delighted with the evening's entertainment. Special praise must be given to Mr. Ernest Spafford who ably discharged the duties of accompanist and also to Mr. Winstanley who deserves great credit for the way be conducted the Brewery Band.</p>
        <p>- - --04%,-</p>
        <p>Lancashire Education Committee. 1 The Lancashire Education Committee on Monday at Preston, Sir Henry F. Hibbert presiding, decided to communicate with the county borough authorities in Lancashire inquiring whether they are willing to co-operate with the County Council in the provision of one or more training colleges. Sir Henry said that the Board of Education had said distinctly that they would not contribute more than 75 per cent. of the cost of building, and personally, after looking very carefully into the matter, he thought it would be a very great mistake if they did. There had been two conferences in Manchester on the question, and neither was attended by representatives from all the county boroughs, while Manchester and Liverpool from the beginning had refused to have anything to do with this co-operative movement.</p>
      </div>
