      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>" Messiah " Service at Legh-street.</head>
        <p>" Messiah " Service at Legh-street.</p>
        <p>Selections from" The Messiah " were rendered at the Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church on Sunday evening last to a crowded congregation, in fact, forms had to be placed in the aisles to accommodate the number that came. Miss A. Bridge was very successful in her interpretations of "Come unto Him' " " There were shepherds,"" Then lo ! the angel of the Lord," "And suddenly," and "I know that my Bedeemer liveth," the latter of which was particularly well sung. Miss L. Plinston in her first attempt at" The Messiah" solos achieved immediate success, her pure contralto voice being heard to great advantage in "Behold, a virgin," "0 thou that tellest," "Then shall the eyes," He shall feed His flock," and "He was despised." Mr. J. H. Corrin, who possesses a voice of fine flexi- bility, gave a good account of himself in "Comfort ye," and" Every valley," the difficult florid passages of the latter being extremely well vocalised. He also sang He was cut off " and "Thou didst not leave" in a pleasing manner. Mr. E. H. Pearce, the well-known Warrington bass, was in his element when interpreting "For behold darkness," "The people that walked," and "Why do the nations," and the way these solos were rendered left nothing to be desired. The choir, under the conductorship of Mr. A. Spruce, rendered the following choruses in a way which certainly enhanced their already good reputation :—" And the glory." 0 Thou that tellest, ' "For unto us," "Behold the Lamb of God," "All we like sheep," "Lift up your heads," "Worthy is the Lamb," and the" Hallelujah Chorus "which was taken last, the congregation standing and many who were evidently acqusinted with the work joining in. Special mention must be made of Mr. A. W. Clatworthy. the youthful organist of the church, who interpreted the difficult accompauiments with remarkable correctness and taste. The Rev. J. P. Buglass presided.</p>
      </div>
