      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>BRIDGE FROCKS.</head>
        <p>BRIDGE FROCKS.</p>
        <p>This season several of the best dressmakers are making a speciality of high-necked dinner frocks or elaborate bridge frocks. These are made of bordered chiffons or coarse net dyed to all shades and trimmed with an applique of silk or cloth of the same colour. Lace is also largely used for these toilets—the heavier makes, such as Irish, Italian, or French guipure lace, which are trimmed with very fine laces and embroideries. HAIR ORNAMENTS.</p>
        <p>Classic 'wreaths of beaten gold or silver aro the most becoming wear for the hair that can be imagined, says a Globe writer, and have an curatisite refinement avhich the most ingenious sequin ornament could never possess. -rney are not expensive, and they wear for ever. Gold and silver ribbon bows are favourites among girls this winter. They certainly do look very pretty in the softly-dressed hair, but it is to be hoped that gold and silver grapes, although they may be pretty in themselves, will soon cease to find favour in English ball-rooms, where they give the daintiest debutante a mildly Bacchana- lian air. Coloured flowers and large blossoms are no longer very smart, but tiny wreaths of Pompadour roses, or circlets of rather larger button roses to go right round the head, are very much in favour.</p>
        <p>cOrlar s taCeel Witn caracal, ana me full three-quarter-length sleeves are finished with cuffs to match. With the coat is worn a fur toque. the crown being of soft white fur and the close-fitting brim of black caracal, and it is trimmed at the side with a large blue and green feather mount. A DISAPPEARING TYPE.</p>
        <p>It is with much relief, says a writer in the Tatler, that one sees that the conventional type of lady journalist who wears a penetrating stare, glasses, and singularly ill-fitting coats is becom- ing much more rare. There were only quite a few at a recent tea party, and they evidently felt that their stares and wrinkles did not give them any useful importance among the other be. feathered and velveted and quite fluffy lady journalists. They had mostly buried their great brains beneath enormous feathered hats without a qualm, and like sensible souls had deigned to wear charming and well-fitting clothes.</p>
        <p>makes it impossible for them to live in harmony, and their relations become so strained that finally John threass up the management of her property, and leaving his wife to go her own way seeks employment else- where. The only person who understands how such a crisis has come to pass is Justine Brent, a hospital- trained nurse and an old friend and school-fellow of Bessy. Her character lifts her above her fellows, and is very finely drawn by the , author. Soon after John's departure Bessie falls from her horse and sustains terrible spinal injuries. For I days she suffers exquisite torture, and then, knowing ' she can never recover the use of her limbs, and that she is only being kept alive by the infliction of ever- increasing pain till the body can literally bear no more, Justine yields to Bessy's prayer for release, and administers an overdose of morphia. To Justine herself the deed is justified, but the young surgeon who has been in constant attendance at the bedside knows what she has done, and when, after a lapse of time, Justine and John are happily married, the ignoble Dr. Wyant holds over her, as a means of extorting money, the threat of divulging her secret. Justine has had her own reasons for concealing her act from her husband, but when presently he learns the truth from Wyant, and she sees how he recoils in horror from her, she is aware that her silence was a mistake, and she unshrinkingly pays the full price of her error. Highways and Byways in Kent," by Walter Jerrold, _Macmillan and Co., 6s. „ .</p>
        <p>The newest volume in the popular "Highways and Byways Series," is by Walter Jerrold, who gives a brief but comprehensive history of the county from the coming of Cesar down to the present day, impart- ing at the same time many pleasant items of informa- tion. In his description of the many less-frequented byways of the county with their varied scenery and quaint villages, he is extremely happy. The one hundred and fifty diawings of Mr. Hugh Thomson, by which this volume is illustrated, add greatly to its attractiveness. It also containes a map of the county. " Silas Marner," by George Eliot ; new edition by Macmillan and Co., Gs. _</p>
        <p>George Eliot's popular story, with illustrations by Mr. Hugh Thomson forms the new volume of "The Cranford Series." Twenty-four of the pictures, which are all full-page, are exquisitely printed in colour. There are also twenty-one in black and white, Clark of Edinburgh prints the book, its typography is therefore excellent, and altogether the volume need fear no comparison with the many popular books that have preceded it in the same series. "Cranford," by Mrs. Gaskell, new edition by Macmillan and Co. ss.</p>
        <p>The new edition of "Cranford" has all the illustra- tions by Ar. Hugh Thomson printed in colour, and the effect is very charming. There are over one hundred of these dainty drawings in the volume and they show the artist at his best. A new and graceful design has been prepared for the cover of the new edition of this favourite presentation volume. It is printed in gold on dark blue cloth, and gives the book a most attractive appearance. "The Poetical Works of John Keats," new edition</p>
        <p>by Macmillan and Co., 3s. 6d. An interesting addition is made to the well-known Globe Library ma Mr. William T. Arnold's edition of "The Poetical Works of John Keats," which has long held a high position for its scholarly qualities, and which now finds a fitting place in a series whose first claim is for purity of text. This edition does not contain every poem written, or even every poem pub- lished by Keats, but all which appeared in the three volumes issued in the poet's life-time are here reprinted. It may be added that in the arrangement of the poems the order of Keats has been religiously maintained. A number of his posthumous poems have also been printed for the first time, the Editor's aim being "to omit nothing which contained even a trace of Keats' finest manner."</p>
        <p>"On Stammering, Cleft-palate Speech, Lisping," by Mrs. Emil Behnke, London, Sampson Low, Marston</p>
        <p>and Co., Ltd., Is. nett. _ Mrs. Emil Behnke is carrying on the work, which for many years was so well done by her husband. She believes that staurTnering, stuttering and lisping can all be cured. If yon stutter or stammer, or lisp read it, and follow the directions indicated. "How does it feel to be old," by Edward Marston, Sampson Low and Co., is. This is a collection of papers reprinted from the "Monthly Review," by Edward Marston, F.R.G.S. (" the amateur angler,") and deals chattily and wisely with a subject which must interest all equallp. "The Family Herald Press " publications. From Messrs. William Stephens, Ltd., of the " Family Herald Press " we have received copies of a thrilling story entitled " The Burning of Trevengartla " Published at 3s. 6d. with cloth and gilt cover, and at 6d, in paper cover. The Monthly Magazine of Fiction, 3d, consists of a complete story "The Harvest Time of Love."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>JAPANESE LUNCH.</head>
        <p>A JAPANESE LUNCH.</p>
        <p>The following is the menu of a genuine Japa- nese lunch lately given at Kawaski to an Eng- lish girl who went out as a bride from England. It is a real Japanese "chow," served at a Japa- nese hotel: First course, a kind of relish or hors d'ceuvre, tied round with seaweed. and served with plum-blossom tea. Second, fish soup con- taining mushrooms, prawns, and white fish. Third, baked fish, sweet sauce, preserved plums, and chestnuts. Fourth, crab stewed and served in its shell, mixed with egg. Fifth, clams, sakin, and rice. Sixth, Japanese tea. SATIN-FAOED CLOTH Corr.</p>
        <p>A smart coat of navy-blue satin-faced cloth is illustrated in our sketch. It fits easily to the figure at the back and sides, and is slightly short- waisted. The fronts are bordered with a wide, stitched band of cloth, and it fastens across the chest with large buttons and loops of cord, the lower part being slightly cut away and left open.</p>
