      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>e" N &lt;A I LI!</head>
        <p>e" N &lt;A I LI!</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>PHEASANTS A L'ESPAGNOLE.—Truss the pheas- ants as for boiling, and put them into a sthwpan with a carrot, a piece of celery, and two onions stuck with two cloves each. Add a sprig of pars- ley, two red Spanish sausages, and enough red wine to moisten. Cover the stewpan, and simmer very gently for. two hours. Meanwhile prepare some rice in the following way: Boil eight ounces of Carolina rice for three minutes, drain it, and dry on a sieve. Putt a gill of salad oil into a saute-pan, and when quite hot put in the rice and fry a light brown. Put the rice into a stewpan, add one pint of good consomme, a little cayenne pepper, and a pinch of saffron. Let all simmer very gently for half an hour. Then take out the rice and mix with it one teaspoonful of tomato sauce and a little glaze. Dish up the pheasanta pour some goon gravy rounu, ana garnish with the rice moulded into the shape of ordinary quenelles. With a tablespoon arrange these round the dish and servo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>ANEMIA CURED.</head>
        <p>ANEMIA CURED.</p>
        <p>Anaemia in the broad meaning is a short way of express- ing low vitality : in the nearer sense it, implies impoverished blood. When we understand, however, that "impoverished " means "poisoned," we realize better all that is comprised in the terrible word anaemia. Get the poison out of the blood, and then the work of nerve and tissue building can be undertaken with con- fidence. The secretive and cleansing organs should be strengthened, and the blood supplied with the red corpuscles which form its beneficent garrison against the attacks of disease. Let the sufferer adopt a course of nourishing diet and fresh air with tonic doses of the following mixture after each meal and at bedtime : Concentrated Infusion of Gentian, loz., Nitirax Com - pound, loz., Dandelion Juice, 2ozs., Cinnamon Water, 2ozs. Two teaspoonfuls in a wineglass of water is a dose. Any chemist will dispense this recipe or supply he ingredients.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>LETTER FROM THE REV. W. POLLOCK-HILL</p>
        <p>The following letter appeared in the " Daily Mail' on Tuesday: _ .</p>
        <p>Sir,—Will you allow me, as winner of both the Oxford and inter-Varsity cross-country races in 1887-8-9, and a holder of the record at that time for both the Oxford and Cambridge courses, to endorse most heartily Mr. Edwards' article on the above subject? What a much more robust generation the next would be if the youths of our large factories and workshops, especially in the North. would spend their Saturday afternoons clearing their lungs and exercising their bodies by healthy cross-country running, instead of merely standing still in tens of thousands watching football matches.</p>
        <p>I say nothing against football but one cannot have one's exercise taken from one any me* than one's meals: and this is one of the main causes o-f the physical degeneracy which one hears spoken of to-day. Cross-country running is of the utmost value, both morally and physically; giving to character strength and perseverance, and, if not overdone, to the body an lasting development of its Dowers of endurance. (Rev.) W. PoLLocK-HILL, M.A., Ex-Pres. O.U.A.C. Padgate Vicarage, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>RYLANDS' CHRISTMAS HANDICAP.</head>
        <p>RYLANDS' CHRISTMAS HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>Rylands' Harriers and A.C. held their Christmas handicap on Saturday last. Twenty members turned out,and trail was laid over a very heavy course of about seven miles. The race was a hard fought one C. A. Harding eventually winning by a few yards, H. Higham and W. Latimer secured second and third respectively, P. Wilding (the captain) from scratch ran splendidly and with remarkably good judgment, his being the fastest time. The position of first six was C. A. Harding received 45sec5. start. time 43mins. 50secs. ; 2, Higham received smins. ; 3, W. Latimer received 5 mins. ; 4, S. Carter received 45secs. ; 5, P. Wilding from scratch time 43mins. 45secs. : Mr. J. Wilding officiated as starter and time keeper. Distribution of prizes will take place on Monday next at the headquarters Ring 0 Bells at 8 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>WARRINGTON CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY. -- Revision of Rules.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY. -- Revision of Rules.</p>
        <p>The rules of the above Society have during the past two weeks been altered so as to bring them up-to-date We understand there have been two private meetings of the members, at which the new rules have been gone into and adopted. Prior to these meetings a small Revision Committee, together with the Manager and Secretary, was appointed to frame the rules, and from what we can gather their suggestions were accepted by the members. There are several new features in connection with the Society, one being the formation of a Thrift or Pension Scheme for the employees. The scheme is quite optional for the employees to join, but we think if they are wise they will do so. Several of them have already expressed their approval of the scheme as it is a mutual one-21 per cent. of the employees wages will be paid by themselves into it and this will be augmented by 21 per cent. fr4m the Society. In cue of an employee leaving he *ill be allowed to take out his proportion. The fond of course will be augmented by interest. Another new feature is the adoption of a Building Club Scheme, which will enable the members to borrow money from the Society on liberal terms is sure to be welcomed by the bulk of the members, especially by those who are desirous of becoming their own " land- lords." Most of the now rules are very democratic, and no-one who has a member of his family engaged in the Society will he allowed a seat on the Committee of Management,neither will a member of the Committee be allowed to sit in judgment on any tender in which he is in any way interested. The rules, taken as a whole, will, we believe, be found workable, and help in assist- ing the Management to further the development of the Society, and benefit the members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Local Property Dispute.</head>
        <p>Local Property Dispute.</p>
        <p>• At the Manchester Assize Courts, on Monday, Vice-Chancellor Leigh-Clare had again before him the action brought by the Warrington Investment Build- ing and Advance Co., Ltd., against Robert Hutchinson Pearson and Thrnnas Baxter. Tde dispute was in connection with a number of bou-es in Sharp-street, Warrington, which were conveyed to Mr. Pearson. It was alleged by the Company that in the transaction Mr. Pearson acted as trustee for them, but he had recently set up a claim that he was the owner of the property, and hal asked the rent collectors to pay over to him the sums they received. Under these circumstances the plaintiff moved for an injunction to restrain him from collecting the rents, from interfering with the tenants, and from mortgaging the property. —For the defence it was contended that the Company had given Mr. Pearson an absolute assignment. - Mr. Grant appeared for tyke plaintiff company, and the defendants were represented by Mr. Rutherford. -Mr. Grant said it was a question whether the defendant Pearson was a trustee for the plaintiffs or the beneficiary owner of certain property, and on the last occasion there was a dispute as to what should be done pending the trial of the action. It had now been agreed that a receiver be appointed, who would give security in the ordinary way. -Mr. Rutherford assent- ing, an order was made for the appointment of a receiver.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>HOARSENESS</head>
        <p>HOARSENESS</p>
        <p>and Loss of Voice. A WELCOME REMEDY.</p>
        <p>The lightning rapidly with which Veno's Lightning Cough Cure does its healing work is amply evidenced in the following letter. Mr. F. Wood, of 31, Queen-road, Reighley, writes : "Veno's Lightning Cough Cure has worked some marvellous cures for me in chest and throat troubles of one kind and another. Recently 1 was so hoarse that I could hardly talk, so at night I took a dose of your 'Cure,' and when I got up this morning, the hoarseness was quite gone, and my chest was as loose as though nothing had beea the matter with me." Veno's Lightning Cough Cure is the purest, safest, and most reliable remedy extant for coughs and colds, bronchitis, asthma, catarrh, sore throat, hoarseness, old age coughs, children's coughs, and all chest and lung troubles. Price 91d., is. lid.,and 2s. 9d., of all chemists and drug stores everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>10, ‘- • . Pare:6l – ,•-• • – • ' • 'IV- • " • ' , – .) – L •</head>
        <p>10, ‘- • . Pare:6l - ,•-• • - • ' • 'IV- • " • ' , - .) - L •</p>
        <p>NEW BOOKS, MAGAZINES,</p>
        <p>ETC.</p>
        <p>"A Love in Ancient Days," by T. H. Crosfield; Elkin Mathews, Gs. Since our brief reference last week to Miss Truda iCrosfield's maiden effort in the realms of fiction we have had an opportunity of reading " A Love in Ancient Days,' and can honestly congratulate the : author on having penned a very pleasant and readable : story. For her "local colour" Miss Crosfield has not I gone any further than the South of England, but few I dwellers in this now populous part would recognise at a first glance the rlaces and things of • a permanent character which figure in the romance unless they had some acquaintance with the history of Britain subse- quent to the Roman invasion and during the embryonic tunes when Britons, Saxons, Picts, and Celts were gradually becoming fused into one race—our own dominant nation —by the simple process of unceasing warfare and blood-letting. It might be thought that the life and love of people living in the sixth century would not be of absorbing interest now-a-days, but Miss Crosfield reconstructs those early times with a skill which makes them admirably realistic. She has chosen a period, too, about which most of us know no more than was gleaned at school from the historical primers and which on closer acquaintance proves to be worthy of attention. It cannot have been easy for Miss Crosfield, on such scant data as exists, to have bridged the centuries in the complete and satisfying way she has done, but in truth the narrative might conceivably have fallen straight from the lips of some ancient Briton who had slept through the ages like " Phra the Phonecian " and come fc'`life con- veniently for our local authoress. Mi4s Clrosfield makes no attempt at the construction of an .i itricate story, culminating in the customary sierf-S:lional denouement. On the contrary, the romarieo :s just such a one as might have entered into thoi,'s•ves of any of the men and maidens of early Br4tain; Her heroine has perfectly natural adventinres, and singularly few set-backs in the nature of misunder- standings. Perhaps the latter were no part of life in those primitive days, but are solely the product of a later civilization. Avanwy is the heroine —a young British maiden with Roman blood in her veins, and living in a Roman villa "set in a cup-like hollow of the downs" until the Saxon marauders came and put , the homestead and its defenders to fire and sword. I, Avanwy and her young sister, Patricia, are captured and taken to the pirate camp, where a period of not altogether unpleasant servitude is undergone. In fact it is here that the heroine meets the man she loves in Cerdica Saxon chieftain. The two girls eventually I escape and the scene changes to the British City of Acqum Sulis, then under the governorship of the great and good 'Ambrosius. From the rough and hardy life of the Saxons to the luxury of a Court there is a contrast which the writer depicts with sure touch and copious detail. There is perhaps too much of the latter, and the story shows a tendency to drag until the decisive Battle of Badon where the Saxons are routed and Avanwy rescues the half-dead Cerdic from the midst of a heap of slain. The culminating point of the love-story is now reached and Cerdic finally accepts the Christian faith, sailiag away with his bride to the green isle of lerne. One has to be hypacritical 1 to find much fault with Miss Crosfield's book, for it is most gracefully written ; while the actors move with 1 ease and speak a simple poetic language which in itself is no easy thing to create. The child Patra is a particularly winning little person and plays no small part in the story, while Cerdic must certainly be a better-class type of Saxon pillager. There were, we suppose, qualities of heart and mind even in the sixth century. We can cordially recommend " A love in Ancient Days "to readers old and young. They will spend a profitable and enjoyable hour or two in its perusal. "Under North Star and Southern Cross," by Francis i Sinclair ; Sampson Low and Co., 6s. 1_ __ _ . . _ . .</p>
        <p>The title of Mr. Sinclair's book is to some extent self-explanatory. It is a collection of stories of adven- tures which came to the author's knowledge "whilst wandering on many seas and lands. The men and women introduced in these pages and the incidents recorded, are not altogether fleeting shadows of the imagination ; the people pourtrayed really existed, and the events took place almost exactly as they are set forth." This seems rather a tall order after reading the bock, and its author must certainly not only be a much-travelled man but one to whom has been vouchsafed some unique experiences. The stories cover experiences in the Antarctic, the Pacific, California, Scotland, Algeria and Palestine—in truth a wide field. The main point is, that these traveller's yarns are exceedingly well-told, with a wealth of poetic imagery which makes people and places live again before our eyes. Mr. Sinclair is an excellent raconteur, and the covers of his latest book embrace some attractive reading. "The White Rose Mystery," by Gerald Bias ; Green- ing and Company, 6s. For Christmas fiction nothing appeals !bore to the average taste than a good thrilling mystery, and we have certainly read nothing of this kind for a long time which transcends" A White Rose Mystery " for sheer grip. Mr. Gerald Biss is rapidly making a name for himself as a writer of sensational fiction, and in the opinion of many he has taken up the mantle which fell from the shoulders of the late Guy Boothby. The story under notice tells of a present-day political plot which, while difficult to admit as a possibility, is so well constructed as to carry the reader along cr,m- pletely enveloped by the tide of events. We have the murder of a Prime Minister in the first chapter, and another to follow soon after, but the element of tragedy is not over-done. The plot involves some of the highest people in the land, and aims at nothing less than the rehabilitation of the House of Stuart on the English throne. Improbable as a determined Jacobite rising seems in these prosaic times the author handles the tangled skein of his mystery with fine power, and the reader is loth to leave the book till the last page is turned over. " The Little City of Hope," a Christmas story by F. Marion Crawford; Macmillan and Co., 2s. 6d. Mr. Crawford marks the season by a new departure. He sends abroad into the world a charming Christmas story, which is full of the spirit of good-will, a spirit, he says," which sleeps most of the time, but wakens every year, like a giant of good courage and good cheer, at the sound of the merry bells across the snow, and to the sweet carol under the windows in the frosty night." The story tells of the patient persevering effort of John Henry Overholt to perfect his invention of an air-motor. To carry on this work he had thrown up his profession, and as time passes and still he does not succeed in his endeavour the little family of three is reduced to great straits. Mrs. Overholt then resolves to become a governess, and she leaves her husband and Newton—her thirteen year old son—to earn money for them by travelling with and teaching the daughters of some people who are rich enough to pay her well. Hope gives place to despair, and but for Newton's sensible manly conduct matters would have become worse. Then, but not before undergoing mental suffering, Overholt receives the necessary sum at the last moment from his wife. Then the wheels of the motor turn exultantly, Mrs. Overholt returns, and the Christmas dinner is, in very truth, "a feast of hearts." Even in this simple little story there is much of Mr. Crawford's fascination as a writer, and " The Little City of Hope" is a worthy piece of fiction. "The Fruit of the Tree," by Edith Wharton; Macmillan and Co., 6s. A new novel from the pen of Mrs Wharton is welcome, and "The Fruit of the Tree" will doubtless meet with a good reception. We have vivid recol- lections of that brilliant novel "The House of Mirth" by the same author, but we must own to some disappointment with the latest production of her fertile brain. "The Fruit of the Tree" is a powerful story written with all Mrs. Whar- ton's brilliancy of style, deep insight into character, and intense sympathy with the faults and weaknesses of human nature, but the theme is not one which will appeal to the average reader. A story of mill life, oppressed operatives, and a beneficent, under-manager is somewhat hackneyed. John Amherst the hero or Mrs. Wharton's novel, is an idealist who at the opening.of the story, is the assistant-manager of some mills which are owned by Bessy Westmore, a young widow of fascinating appearance and with a character wholly swayed by impulse. John had been born with "a nassion for machinery," and he loved the work at the mills as much as he hated the conditions under which the operatives had to perform their duties. As the husband of Bessy, he believes he will be able to carry out his projects for the greater happiness and health of the "hands," and for some time after their marriage all goes well. But the different standpoint from which each regards the source of Bessy's wealth</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>INFECTIOUS RASH ON WHOLE FAMILY</head>
        <p>INFECTIOUS RASH ON WHOLE FAMILY</p>
        <p>Father, Mother, and Two Girls Covered with an Ugly, Pimply Rash—Baby Covered from Crown to Sole—Thought She Would Die —Tried Every Kind of Ointment.</p>
        <p>AT LAST COMPLETELY CURED BY CUTICURA</p>
        <p>"Myself and two little children were covered with a red pimply rash, and it was so itchy. I had tried every oint- ment it was possible to get. Until I tried the Cuticura Ointment, it was the first relief we got. I was afraid to go near a doctor for fear of him making a fuss, as of course I knew it must be infectious, for I took it first, then one little one, and then the other, and finally, my husband. I used about twelve or thirteen boxes Cuticura Ointment, but that wasn't muchonsidering all I had to do with it. 1W own arms and be- tween my fingers, on front of shins and between my toes, were completely cov- ered with an ugly, itchy rash, and my youngest little girl was covered from crown to sole. I thought she would have died. She was only a year and two months at the time. My eldest wasn't so bad, but bad enough. Thanks to the Cuticura Ointment, we are com- pletely cured, and I shall recommend the Cuticura Remedies to my friends. Mrs. D. M. Grieve, Donaghmore Co., Tyrone, Feb. 12 and Feb. 19, 1906."</p>
      </div>
