      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 21, 1907.</p>
        <p>WE CORDIALLY WISH ALL OUR READERS A MERRY CHRISTMAS AND A HAPPY NEW YEAR.</p>
        <p>"Methinks they do protest TEMPERANCE too much" might be said,with REFORM. some appropriateness, of the meeting which was held in Manchester the other day to consider in advance forthcoming licensing legislation the character of which is not at present known. One of the resolutions, " repre3enting every branch of the licensing trade, involving an invested capital of over £300,000,000," protested against any licensing legislation founded on unjust and con- fiscatory principles. This is surely superfluous. Nobody proposes to rob the publicans or the brewers. Whatever is proposed by the Govern- ment, it will not be a measure of con- , fiscation. To use beforehand such words as are to be found in this resolution, ' passed unanimously, is to prejudice discus-' sion of a subject of the greatest national interest and importance. The course of reasoning that is pursued is obvious enough. As it is a Liberal Government that is about to legislate, says "The Trade," shaking hands with itself on its penetration, it must be robbery that is intended. We can assure the licensed victuallers' societies— which, by the way, can nowadays hardly consist of licensed victuallers in the old and legal sense, since most licence-holders are merely the servants of the brewers—that they will not improve their case,</p>
        <p>whatever it may be, by overstating it, or by pre- senting it in resolutions which foam at the top like a mug of old October. We conclude from the resolutions adopted at Manchester, all with entire unanimity, that" The Trade "is opposed to even the most moderate kind of licensing reform. Its attitude is one of "Hands off !" There must be no attempt to increase th duties upon licences, either directly or indirectly. An increase of the present ridiculously low licence duty, whatever it may betis, it seems, cuilculated to cripple a legitimate industry. There must be no local veto. Local vb:o it appears, would increase drunkenness! There must be no Sunday closing, as "the public house is as great a necessity to the working man on Sunday as on any other day.' There must be no time limit to licences.That would be unjust and unsatisfsctory. Finally, as we have shown, there must be no unjust and con fiscatory legislation, the assumption being that any Liberal legislation must be unjust and con- fiscatory. Frankly, we are unable to congratu- ate "The Trade" on its statesmanship, if its quality is to be estimated from the Manchester resolutions. There is an all but general admission among the people that a crying need of the time is temperance reform. Legislation on the subject lthere is certain to be, and reforming legislation ; and ftere are the various societies concerned in the liquor traffic meeting together, not to con- sider how to meet the Government proposals, and how to make what must and will be done as tolerable as may be; but to pass resolutions, each of which is of the character of the instruction which King CANIITC in one age, and Mrs. PART - INGTON in another, imputed to the sea.</p>
        <p>Two or three statements that were made at the Manchester Conference deserve a moment's examination. One of these is that the licensing trade involves an invested capital of over £300,000. This may possibly be true, and, at any rate, it is a statement to be remembered ; but remembered along with it should be the fact that the trade is enormously over-capitalised, in consequence of which it appears to produce a much lower profit than it really does produce on the actual value of its goodwill and stock-in-trade. Any burden, however light, laid on an over-capitalised concern may be more than it can bear, whereas it would not be felt at all under healthy and normal conditions of trading. An increabe of the licence duty, says one of the resolutions, would cripple a legitimate industry. Well, why should it? In a small country like Norway the trade in spirits is run by the Samlag societies, which take a profit of 5 per cent., not a bad interest on capital, and have enough left over to make roads, build free libraries m,aintain baths and wash-houses, and perform many other useful public duties, to the great relief of the community. "The Trade "in this country does nothing for the community. How can it seriously contend that it would be crippled by a higher licence duty? Of course it would not be crippled, or even appreciably pinched, on that capital of £300,000,000. A gentleman from Burton-on- Trent, whose daily surroundings certainly tend to induce beliefs like that which he expressed, said ; that there are more people dependent on the liquor traffic than there are adult teetotalers in the kingdom. We do not take his word; but his statement only matters as one of the now common attempts to make it appear that" The Lade" is a great employing agency, the truth being that, in proportion to the amount of money paid over and the extent of the profits made, it employs fewer persons that any other of our important industries as anybody may see for himself by comparing, say, a brewery with a cotton factory or a shipyard.</p>
        <p>Oa another page of this INFANTILE issue we print . some notes</p>
        <p>MORTALITY, and a diag-ram by the Rev. Sidney Pitt, which have a specially significant bearing on the question of infantile mortality in Warrington. Toe diagram makes startlingly plain the denressing records in Warrington, ward by ward. The deaths pr thousand below the age of one year range from 214 in St. John's Ward to 110 in Wnitecross, whereas the average for Huddersfield is only 85. Mr. Pitt urges sympathetic co-operation between the local Health Committee, and an association such as the one originated by Alderman Broadbent in Huddersfield, which has been instrumental in bringing the infantile mortality figures for that town down from 138 to 85 We hope this grave question will be faced with the same determination and public spirit in Warrington.</p>
        <p>Prosperity continues to be THE SAVINGS BA.NK the key note of the work of the Warrington Savings Bank, for although the number of transactions during the past year shows a decrease, there is a gratifying increase in the number of accounts and the money paid in. For the first time in the history of the bank the total funds exceed £400,000. As we have previously emphasised, the Bank is pre-erninently a working- class-institution and its marked progress may fairly be taken as a reflection of the material prosperity of the industrial community. The inauguration of the Special Investment D3partment for the benefit of those whose accounts have reached the limit allowed in the ordinary depart- ment has been fully justified, £16,62.5 6s. 2d. having been added to its funds during the year. A new departure which has aroused great interest is the purchase by the management of a number of home safes which should prove a great encouragement to thrift. The safes have been introduced with great success in Manchester and we have no doubt that in Warrington they will be welcomed by the thrifty working-classea and considerably extend the use. 1 fulness of the Bank.</p>
        <p>All true imperialists as well THE "ALL BLLCIa " as football enthusiasts will VISIT. make a point of seeing the great match at Wilderspool to-day between the Warrington Rugby team and the New Zealanders. These international contests at football, cricket and other sports do as much if not more, to foster unity and solidarity between the Mother Country and the colonies as the eff )rts of all well-guided or mis-guided statesmen put together. Mr. Chamberlain is the greatest of imperialists,but even he never aroused the interest and enthusiasm of two wide-apart peoples to the extent that the English cricketers in Australia and the New Z ialaul footballers in England are doing. This kin-ship in sport is a grand thing for the British race, and of far more value as an imperial asset than all the tariff-reform induce- ments ever contemplated. But apart from the imperial view,the Warrington public will doubtloss give a hearty welcome to the "All Backs" as gports- men and " jolly good fellows." It is 18 years since a similar event in the town, when the original "All Blacks "—the Maoris—played and beat the ' Warrington men at Wilderspool. The present team is merely" All black" by name and not by nature, though one or two of the men are said to have Maori blood in their veins. Triers were two Wynyards in the Maori team, and the two Wynyards of to-day are understood to be nephews, though they have no more " colour" than would be attributable to the Antipodean sun. Warring- ton's guests of to day number 28 and 13 will play lin the match. We can assure them of a warm welcome and a good game, and we hope the best aide will win.</p>
      </div>
