      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <p>Mr. Hearst as the typ3 of MR. HEARST. the American nation may be gratifying to the pro- prietors of the "New York Journal "—the " Choinal " of the B )wery boy—but it is not flittering to the nation as a whole. Yet Mr. Sydney Brooks, who writes in the "Fortnightly Review "on "The Significance of Mr. Hearst, • makes out a very good"' case for regarding him as typical of his country. Mr. Hearst has had a remarkable career. He has been all strenuosity and push. He turned the piper which he owns from a gutter rag into a formidable rival of the ' "New York World," and, having beaten Mr 1 Palitz3c in the contest, made himself and his sew firm in the affections of the new public he has discovered in the contest. It is the journalism of foot-long scare heads and sensations that make the operations of out own halfpecny journals look foolish. Perhaps is, is because we are accustomed to getting o or idea of the American from the Yellow Press that we are apt to judge all Americans by the samej standard. Mr. Hearst may represent a class of 1 his countrymen. He may be the type of alert. ness, of push and vigour, of the determination that disdains mock modesty, and glories in the recital of achievement and the promise of greater victory He certainly is a power to be reckoned with for many years in all probability, despite his last defeat at the hands of Tammany. His appeal is to young America and he is a surprise personally, presenting a complete and striking contrast to his own newspapers. Mr. Brooks seas to regard his future as being probably a "sinister form of political burlesque."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>We ufnderstand that the United Kingdom Alliance is making arrangements to hold a big meeting at Warrington in February. The principil speaker will be Mr. Sloane, Conservative M.P. for Belfast.</p>
        <p>Warrington may take -credit for playing a very worthy part in the Volunteer movement. At the present time it furnishes a lamer contribution to the Volunteers in proportion to its population than any other town or urban district in Lancashire—the premier county in Volunteering. The actual figures are 15 per cent. as compared with -75 per cent. for Great Britain as a whole, so that, as Colonel Edelsten neatly put it, if the latter rate could be taken as the national standard of duty, Warrington has not only performed its duty, but in recent years has performed it doubly. * * *</p>
        <p>. . There were 120 applications for a post as assistant school attendance officer under the Warrington Education Committee at a salary of 255. a week. Whether the job is looked upon as a sinecure we do not know, but we should hardly have thought the salary would have tempted such a host of candidates. A good many of them seem to have indulged in active canvassing for the appointment, with the result that at the meeting on Nlonday there was a chorus of growls from members who had been" badgered "for their vote and influence on behalf of some local applicant or other. It was given out pretty plainly that canvassing would be strongly resented in future.</p>
        <p>The Cobden Club are proceeding with their arrange- ments for an International Free Trade Conference in August next. Several leading men in the political and industrial world in Germany, France, Austria, and Italy have expressed their willingness to take part in such a congress, and it is expected that a strong con- tingent of Americans will attend it. No doubt a British statesman of position and authority, probably either the Chancellor oi the Exchequer or Mr. Lloyd George, will be asked to preside over the conference, whose proceedings will show that most of the intellectuals " of the world are on the side of Free Trade.</p>
        <p>In Manchester on Satu-rday- Mr. Corrie Grant found as keen an audience for his exposition of the Small Holdings Act, which comes into force next year, as in any of the more rural centres in. which he has addressed meetings on the subject. It was stated (says the " Manchester Guardian ") that over 600 applications for land under the Act have already been received from Lancashire —a signiftcant thing in a county where the attractions of urban employment are nearer at hand than in any othsr. Indeed the reception of the Act everywhere has shown that the "land hunger" is as strong, though hitherto latent for lack of opportunity, in England as in France or Ireland, or any distinctively agricultural country. 0 0 _ _ _</p>
        <p>There are notices in all our- teram-ears which are supposed to prohibit spitting, and some time ago a Warrington man was proseduted and fined for com- mitting the offence. Since then the vile habit has spread to such an extent that some of the cars on the Latchford and other routes much frequented by work- men are daily in a state which can only be described as sickening. Any regular frequenter of the upper deck can testify to the freedom and frequency with which a large proportion' of the passengers spit. The notices need enlarging to about six times the present unobtrusive size, and a batc:h of 50 offenders or more haling before the magistrates each week. We hope the Tramways Committee will recollect their respon- sibility in the matter. At present the condition of the trams is a scandal and a disgrace.</p>
        <p>The Manchester Tramways Department is laughing still about the story of a broken window. The other day a driver caught in a block of traffic saw near him a farm waggon loaded with cabbages. "Hi, mate," he called to 'the waggoner, " chuck us a cabbage." The waggoner chucked a big one and directed it with some force at the tram-driver's head. Taken by sur- prise, the driver ducked, and the cabbage went clean through theglass panel of the sliding door behind him. The luckless driver was held responsible for this, and he was not exactly happy when he received a demand for lie., the cost of replacing the broken panel. How- ever, his fellow-workmen started a penny collection, and the money was soon raised. But every time they meet the unlucky driver they greet him with " Hi, Bill ! has ta biled that cabbage yet ?" * *</p>
        <p>Unreserved praise for MrTHaldane's territorial army scheme was forthcoming from Lieutenant-Colonel Edelsten at the Warrington Volunteer prize distri- bution on Monday evening. He did not hesitate to pronounce it to be a masterpiece of thought and emphasised its suitability for a business community. There is every reason to believe that if the scheme has the enthusiastic support of the Volunteers —and there is little doubt on that score—it will prove thoroughly workable and add greatly to the efficiency of our defensive forces. OrgAnisation is the keynote, and the territorial army will be cpmplete with artillery, engineers, cavalry and medical units. As Colonel Edelsten aptly pointed, out the new arrangements will raise the Volunteers in the national estimation and they will thus be encouraged to persevere in their self-imposed and patriotic duties. * * *</p>
        <p>Charity is the sterilised Milk of human kindness.— MUMFORD.</p>
        <p>Our bodies are our gardens ; to which our wills are ardeners.—SIIIKESPEATtE. The age of chivalry is gone; and one of the calcu- lators and economists has succeeded.—BUßKE.</p>
        <p>A woman need not be evil in order to distroy a man's career. Many people have excellent morals, but the most odious ways—Mits. CRAIGIE. Hunger is the best sauce—and the worst boss.— MIZNER.</p>
        <p>Do noble things, not dream them all day long.— KIIGSLEY.</p>
        <p>There are no sufferings which sympathy may not make lighter.—L ixtENNITS. Our world is not a spectacle; it is a field of battle upon which all who in their hearts love justice, beauty and holiness are bound to play their part. —MizzINI.</p>
        <p>* The Warrington Volunteers may be pardoned if they have been entertaining feelings of pride this week. On Monday evening at their prize distribution —an eminently successful function—General Sir C. J. Burnett, Commander of the Western Division, paid a high tribute to the Corps, based on his personal i experience of their value. When they were n camp at Llanberis the General thought he would take them "on the hop" by inspecting them during the first day in their new quarters. Notwithstanding that they were taken at what would no doubt be considered a disadvantage, the General had pleasure in declaring that all he saw of the Volunteers pleased him very much indeed. He added that if all Volunteer Corps ' came up to the same standard, there would be very little reason to grumble at the Volunteer force. This is high praise from one in authority, and must afford gratification to officers and men as well as to the townspeople generally who take a great interest in the battalion. Woods Great Peppermint Care, for Coughs gad Colds; never fails. 1/11. 2/9.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The projected fusion of all sections of the Labour party seems likely to be accomplished. Mr. Ramsay Macdonald declares very confidently that the Labour Party will soon be more united, and if the Miners' Federation decide that their representatives shall join the L%bonr Party, probably some of the other trades unionist members will take the same course. There will, however, still be a section of labour members who will adhere to the Liberals. I doubt (says the London correspondent of the " Liverpool Post and Mercury ") if any vote of the Miners' Federation will drive Mr. Burt and Mr. Fenwick into the Labour Party. The Durham and Northumberland miners have always taken an independent position, and their representatives will take their own course. I feel convinced, also, that neither Mr. Maddison nor Mr. Henry Vivian, who are strong individualists, will pass under the banner of Mr. Keir Hardie. At the same time, the addition of twenty Liberal-Labour members to the existing Labour party would undoubtedly ncrease its authority and power.</p>
        <p>,4• , •</p>
        <p>.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>VOICE OF LABOUR. The above column will this week be found on page 2.</head>
        <p>THE VOICE OF LABOUR. The above column will this week be found on page 2.</p>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETY NEWS.—On Friday last the Penketh United Order of Druids held their last lodge night of this year. A new member was duly initiated and several others proposed to become members; the contributions were also a record. Election of officers was then proceeded with. BIBLE CLASS.—The Penketh Men's Bible Class held their open service on Sunday in the Chapel, the Chair- man being Mr. C. E. Parker, who is president of the class. The Rev. J. F. Matthews gave a most edifying address taking as his subject "The ape, man and God" Miss Balshaw was an ideal soloist. Mr. G. Weaver officiated at the organ._ _</p>
        <p>LECTURE AT TriE MESSIONL—On Tuesday evening the Vicar the Rev. J. R. Jones M.A. deli6red his second lecture in the church mission the title being " The practical side of a truly Christian life." It was illustrated with splendid lantern slides which were exhibited by Mr. Woodward. LEBERA.L CLUE.—On Saturday a selected team from the Penketh Liberal Club journeyed to Runcorn to meet the Runcorn Liberal club team on their annual visit. The company were entertained to a substantial tea at 5 o'clock. Eighteen games were played and after an exciting contest the Runcorn team proved themselves victorious, winning by 264 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S ANGLING</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S ANGLING</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the above Association took place on Thursday evening at the headquarters, Glad- stone Arms, Church-street. gr. J. Osborne presided and a very fair number of members attended. The Secretary in his report congratulated the members on the success of the Club, they having both an increase in membership and also financially. The balance sheet read showed the income to be £59 is., and the expenditure £52 4s. 7d., leaving a balance in hand of .E 6 16s. sd. The Auditors' report having been given, it was unanimously resolved that the report and balance sheet be accepted. It was decided to again have three contests in the coming season, the opening match at Barrow Lock and the Cup match and annual match at Acton Bridge, the dates to be as near last year's as possible. A long discussion followed with respect to fishing for eels and flounder during the close season and it was decided to give two special prizes out of the funds in addition to a prize offered by Mr. Bryers. It was also decided to offer three special sett prizes for roach, perch and gudgeon out of water rented by the Association, the value of each to be 10s. Five shillings is offered for thp largest roach caught in club waters in August; ss. for the largest roach caught in club waters in September.. The election of officers resulted as follows: Presi- dent, Mr. H. Mather; Treasurer, Mr. R. Eyes ; Secretary, Mr. F. Singleton ; Committee, Messrs. J. Warburton, J. Sanson, J. Seddon, R. Harablett, J. Bryers, W. Ellison, J. Gibson. H. Eyes, T. Barry, T. Robinson, H. Barnes, IV. Bayley, T. Isherwood, S. Stewart, H. G atley J. Taylor, and H. Leyland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Services for Sunday.</head>
        <p>Services for Sunday.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church: Morning 10.30, Rev. E. J. Gawne ; evening 6.30, Rev. William Mellor. The choir in the evening will give selections from Handel's "Messiah."</p>
        <p>Bewssy-road Wesleyan Church: Morning 10.30, Mr. E.J. Guest,B.A. ; evening 6.30,R3r. E. J.Gawne. In the evening selections will be given from "The Messiah." Baptist enure!), Golborne-street : Morning 10-45, evening 6.30, Mr. J. Lowe Fenn, from Liscard,Cheshire. Baptist Church, Latchford: Aforning 10.30 and evening 6.0, Rev. J. F. Matthews. Wycliffe Church. Bewsev-street : Nforning 10-30, ROV. Yonge ; evening, 6.30, Christmas choral servicse. selections from "The Messiah." The soloists will be :—Miss M. Houghton, Miss Knowles, Miss Griffiths' Miss Armstrong, Mr. A. Griffiths and Mr. F. Brown• A small orchestra will be in attendance. Mr. E. J. Guest, 8.A., will give an address. Legh-straet Primitive Methodist Church : Morning 10.30, Rev.J. B. Buglass ; evening 6.30, Carol service including selections from "The Messiah." Soloists, Miss A. Bridge, Miss A. Woodland, Mr. J. H. Corrin.</p>
        <p>Messrs. .J. and W. Dutton, Sankey-street, have this year a larger and more varied assortment of Christmas novelties than ever, and a visit to the Toy Bazaar in the basement is one of the events which the children of Warrington and neighbourhood look forward to with as much eagerness as a visit from Santa Claus. Not only in the Toy Department, however, is the enterprise of this firm manifest, as all departments are making special features in their own line for Christmas presents. Choice Silver Goods, hundreds of useful fancy articles of all descriptions, Photo Frames. Cabinets, Writing Cases, Dressing Cases, Leather Goods, Gloves, Handker- chiefs, Umbrellas, Ties, Novelties in Household Linens and Furnishing Goods, in fact, everyone's requirements are anticipated, and for anyone requiring Presents, either Ladles' or Gentlemen's, the only difficulty at this great establishment is to make a choice out of the bewildering assortment on view.</p>
        <p>THIS DA.Y SATURDAY, MC, 2157.</p>
        <p>PitESENTATION WITEI t' THE EXIMINER</p>
