      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Canvassing for Appointments.</p>
        <p>MEMBERS INDIGNANT.</p>
        <p>Connelllor J. Fulton Smith presided at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee which WAS held at the Town Hall on Monday. There were -also present :—The Mayor (Alderman Smethurst), Mr. H. Roberts (deputy ells irman).Alderman Ellison; Coun- cillors Rill, Barton, Burton, Dickenson. Evans, Lord, Nield, Pemberton, Toole and Wilkinson: Revs. N. Wilson end J. Vonce Miss Williams Messrs. C. Sawkins Burgess. T. P. Bradshaw, R. T. Fairclough, A. J. Jolley, C. H. Unsworth, J. Upson, G. Walker; Mr. J. Moore Murray (director), Mr. R. B. Roberts (assistant director). A resolution of sympathy with the relatives of the late Alderman Fairclough was passed in silence. SCHOOL CHILDREN AND THE BATHS. - - •</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee reported that a letter was read from the Sports Committee of the Warrington and District Teachers' Association, sug- gesting the desirability of the facilities afforded during the past season to school boys in regard to the use of the public baths being extended next season to school girls. and it was resolved that the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee be approached in the hope that some such arrangement may be made. In moving the adoption of the minutes, Mr. Linos remarked that the committee would appreciate the efforts the sub-committee were making to extend the bathing and washing facilities to the girls, as they had done to the boys. They anticipated that they would meet with the same readiness from the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee to grant facilities as they did in the case of boys. Councillor WTLKINSON seconded, and the minutes were passed. CANV A SSTNG FOR APPOINTMENTS.</p>
        <p>SUGGESTED EXAMINATION FOR CANDIDATES. The School Attendance Committee resolved that Mr. John H. Knowles he appointed assistant school attend- ance inspector at a eommencing salary at the rate of £65 per annum, rising by annual increments of £6 10s. to a maximum of £lO4. Mr. UPSON said the pnsitiors of school attendance officers were much songht after, and appointments led to a very great deal of canvassing and wire-pulling. BA did not for a moment suggest in this case that the best man bad not. been appointed, hut he would suggest that in future, subject to the presentation of testimonials and medical certificates, the candidates he submitted to an examination and that. the man at the top receive the appointment.. This would relieve the committee from a greet deal of canvassing and the best men would be spnointed. The Rey .Tnhn vosor, : What would the examina- tion be about? Mr. UPSON replied, as to general intelligence, know- ledge of the bylaws relating tn the Sohool Attendance Committee, tactfulness in dealing with parents, &amp;c. At the present time it might be that a man would be appoinied who had the most persevering and disagree- able friends (Laughter.) The Rev. JotIN YosPE said this wasa most difficult matter, and he would like to point out that the greater number of eandida+es whnsenames were in the selected list had occupied positions Ps clerks and so on. As to tactfulness in manging parents how in the world could they put them through an examination in that ? (Langhtet.) If an examination were instituted be did not envy the Director his task. (Laughter.) The Rev. Father WILSON pointed out that there were ahnnt 120 eprriidate., and he thought that the committee had really held an examination of them to some extent. Thev bad their applications in their own handwriting, and by various enestions put to them. they had elicited as far As possible information on the various points raised by 11r. Upson. The MAYOR considered that the committee were deserving of ever,' credit for the tiwe they had spent in selecting an nf Peer nut nf 120 candidates. He was beginning to wonder as education spread. how m-ny candidates there would be soon for a position at 2.15. a week. Mr. PLINsTON remarked that tho.e who did the</p>
        <p>ninst nanva.sing W ere not among theselected ten. lie did not believe in pr his rota to enynne. The CHAIRMAN said that if the dismission served to let the pnbiic know that canvassing was a barrier to apoointment. it would an a great deal of good. The ,canvassing which had taken place bad been the snnrce of very great annoyance to him. Re had received a nrtmber nf letters from pennle in the town who onght to have knnwn 'hatter. Re felt it rather a reflection nnon himself end his ability to indee for himself. That. was one of the reasons why he did not attend the meeting of the committee when the appointment was made. _ .</p>
        <p>—CoUncillor BARTON also r.rotested against the nnmber of letters he had received from friends of candidates. Mr. JoLLEv : I think the lesson to he (Winced from the subject is somewhat different from that which the Chairman has enunciated. It is not that a man must not canvass. hot rather that he Trust be uncommonly careful how be artPS if. (Laughter.) Mr. UPSON said every speaker had afforded great support to his snggestion as to the method of appoint- ment, in future. _ _</p>
        <p>Alderman ELLisoN said there were two things be dis- liked as a member of the committee. One was' making appointments and the other was the raising of salarieq. (Laughter.) They were both diMcnit thinTs with, and he bad TiP,TI " pestered" by people coming to see him. He wished some method enuld be found to avoid this. He had never made a promise to anybody and did not intend to do so. (Hear, hear.) The minutes were adopi ed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Indiwestion His Poe.</head>
        <p>Indiwestion His Poe.</p>
        <p>Newcastle Blacksmith. robbed of all :ftti-Pruffh lw IndiTection. cnmnletely Cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>For forty-two years the anvils have rung and dazzling sparks danced to the "bump and strike" of Isaac Dnxon's hammer in the busy Sycamore Street Smithy, lstetvcastle-on-Tyne. Still busy at his forge, and claim- Inc to be full sixty Years of age. Mr. Drown chatted recently over old experiences with a Newcastle journalist. have had tn work hari all these years," he said, to keep pace with work.</p>
