      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Death of Colonel Langford Brooke. Colonel Langford Brooke, of Mere Hall, Knutsford, died on Tuesday morning at the age of sixty-five. He was formerly major and hon. lieutenant-colonel of the 2nd Battalion Cameron Highlanders, and was a magistrate and deputy-lieutenant of the county of Elgin. Temporary Authority. James Ince applied at the Borough Police Court on Monday for the transfer from Charles Povey to himself of an off-license in Lythgnes-lane. The Chief Constable produced a character of Mr.Tnce which was very satis- factory and the transfer was granted. Musical Successes. Trinity College of Music, Livernool Centre. The following pupils of Miss Alice Griffiths of 37, Folly Lane, passed successfully. Miss Gladys Starkie, Pianoforte playing,Preparatory Division, 77 marks. Miss Adeline King, Preparatory Division, 66 marks. The Priory School. At the recent music examination the following pupils of the Misses William., the Priory School, were successful candidates in pianoforte playing : —Trinity College. London, Senior Division : Bessie Lee, and Mary -Dorothy Hall. The Associated Board of the Royal Academy and Royal College of Music, Higher Division of the School Examination : Margaret Pitt. Christmas Tree. The annual Christmas tree in connection with the Latchford Bantist (Thumb was on view in the school- room, Wash-lane, on Monday afternoon. The room was opened at. four o'clock and was visited by a large number of people. The effort was a great success and the articles for sale were quickly disposed of. 'LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs and Colds. Of proven efficacy. Of Chemists.—Advt. Fire. Shortly before 11 o'clock on Tuesday night a fire broke out in a storeroom behind the Emmanuel Church Institute, Knntsferd-road, but fortunately it was discovered quickly and the flames were extinguished by some of the neighl,ours. The Fire Brigade had been sent for, but on their arrival it wait found that their services were not required. How the fire originated is a mystery. Lord Acton's Son and Heir. A son and heir was born to Lady Acton on Monday at Bordighera, where her ladyship has been staying with her father and mother, Mr. and Mrs. T. H. Lyon, of Appleton Hall, who usually winter at that. Italian resort. Both Lady Acton and infant, we are pleased to announce, are doing well. Lord and Lady Acton have now two daughters and a son. Motorist Fined. At the Lymm Petty Sessions, on Wednesday, Alfred Ellam, chauffeur, of Weaste-lane, Thelwall, was fined £5 and costs for driving a motor-car at a speed dangerous to-the public on November 13. It was stated on the day in question, that John Thomas, a licensee, of Grappenhall, was driving a young horse and trap. When coming out of Weaste-lane he saw the motor- car, driven by defendant, travelling at about 20 wiles an hoer.—Mr. Brown, who defended, contended that when the chauffeur saw Thomas put up his hand he immediately slackened speed. LINSEED COMPOUND' Mr Conehs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles. 91d. &amp; I/li. Of Chumists.—Advt A Neglectful Father. At the Borough Court, on Thursday, before Messrs. Charles Nvbite and j. Rymer Young, Thomas Catterall last from Manchester. pleaded guilty to leaving his wife and two children chargeable to the Union Mr. Basil Shearer, who prosecuted, said the Prisoner's wife and children were admitted on Decem- ber 3rd. they having been deserted. Prisoner could earn £2 a week as a moulder if he cared to work, and for some time he had been in regular emnloyment at Manchester Prisoner bad been previously charged with a similar offence, but the case was withdrawn on account of big statement that he had got work and would look after his family. Prisoner was now sent to gaol f9r three months' hard labour. Death and Funeral of Mr. S. Robert Clough Mitch regret has been occasioned by the death of Mr. S. Robert Clough, of 6. Paul-street in his 61st year. For many rears he had acted in the capacity of fore- man for Mr. -Dolan, builder, but of recent years had had charge of important duties at Messrs. Monks,Hall, and Company's Works. sinee the recent death of his son, who was a member of St. Paul's Choir, his health had beenme somewhat impaired, and following a bronchial attack on Thursday mcrniurr. he died as a result of the failure of the heart's action. The funeral took place on Sunday, the first part of the service being held at. St. Paul s Church. The Rev. W. Brace- camp spoke in high terms of the deceased's good qualities. Besides the members of the family there were present the heads of the various departments at. lareQß,...mraiks,Hall and n,) The service at the graveside .vs conducted hr the Vicar of St. Paul's. Numerous floral tributes were sent. MOUNTArN FT, 4.X (LTNITX CA.TRARTECUM) PILLS An neropqhle aperient Worth a trial. 90.—Adyt. Theft from a Shop door. At the Borough Court on Tuesday before Rymer Young (in the chair) and C. Jackson Holmes, Edward Sance,Rosa and (irown-street.was charged with steeling from outside the shop of Messrs. Scales and Sons, Bridge-street, a pair of men's hoots. valued at Bs. 11d., the prorerty of Messrs. Scales, on Monday night—The Chief Constable said that the prisoner was seen out- side the shop by a girl named Alice Radernft who saw him cnt piece of string which was attached to a pair of boots and run away. The manager,Benjamin Ge,rge Little, was at once imfortned and gave Anse The nrisoner ran un Rose and Crown-street and then dropped one of the hoots and attempted to strike the manager with the other.—P.C. Bennett was informed and arrested the prisoner who when charged denied any knowledge of the theft.—Prisoner said he was drunk at the time. The Chief Constable said the prisoner was a lazy man and would not wark and had been forty-one times previously convicted. -The rihairman said the prisoner would have to go to gaol for two months with hard labour. PUBLIC RENEFTT—rNMeR TB% CLOCK POP FON ROOTS TN LANCASITTrE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Stack Fire at Bewsey. Some excitement was created shortly after ten o'clock on Thursday morning when it became known that a fire had broken out on the farm of Messrs. Hatton and Reid on the Betvsey Old Hall Estate, near Warrington The outbreak was discovered by Levi .Tackson, who was working on a tennis ground, and he quickly sent a messager to Dr. Markers house, with the result that a tolenhonic message was sent tl the Fire Station. The Borough Brigade tnrnsd out under the command of Deputy-superintendent Turner with the steam fire engine"Major."They were followed shortly after by Messrs. Crosfields' brigade, under reean t Gallon, with their chemical engine. On the arrival of the brigade it was found that a large iron Dutch barn, containing three hays of unthreshed oats and one bay of pressed stra tv, was blazing furiously from end to end. It was absolutely impossible to save any of the oats or straw,and the only thing left for the firemen to do was to prevent the flames from spreading to the adjoining farm buildings. A plentiful supply of water was obtained from an adjoining pond, and the firemen were able to keep the flames confined to the barn. The origin of the fire is nnknown. The damage, which will amount to between £5OO and £6OO, is covered by insurance. 'LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs and Colds. Relieves Asthma and difficult breathing.—Advt. Anglers at Dinner. The members of the Bank Way Jolly Anglers' Society held their annual dinner on Saturday evening, at the Bowling Green Inn, Liverpool-road. The affair was ,a great success. Over 40 sat down to dinner, which was provided by the host and hostess, Mr. and Mrs. Campbell. A smoking concert was afterwards held, the President of the society (gr. Frederick T. Bennie) making a few remarks regarding the work of the society. The Secretary (Mr. Henry Kent) presented his report for the past year, and said the club had had a very good season, and he was very pleased to state that the accounts showed a small balance to the good. The toasts of "The King, Queen and Royal Family," "The .T lly Anglers' Society," and "The Visitors," were given. The prizes which the club gave for the best catches during the season were pre- sented by the President to thelollowing : —A.Wood,n. Nurse, W. Melling, W. Boyle, W. Unswori.h, S. Thomas, J. Allcock, H. Leyland and A. Stvarbrick. During an interval in the concert a presentation from the club msnahers to the President, for his valuable services during the past year was made by Mr. A. ICook,of the Anglers' ,Association, and it took the form of a gold-mounted umbrella. Songs were rendered by Messrs. W. White, C. Nurse, J. Gorst, T. Allcock, and J. Jones. Mr. J. Gorst was the accompanist. The whistling solos by Mr.. Whittaker were much enjoyed.</p>
        <p>" ()SRAM " ELECTRIC ri AMPS. —Daubls the light at half the cost. See Windows.—Agent: J. S. GARL AND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Interesting Local Will. The will of Mr. Joseph Flitcroft, of Market Place, Leigh, Llncashire, hoiiery manufactnter, who died on October Ist, has just been proved by his son, Dr. Thomas Edward Fliteroft. of the Union Infirmary, Warrington, and Mr. William Holland, of 45, Schofield-street, Leigh. The amount of the estate is 111,069. Defrauding the Railway Company.</p>
        <p>Alfred Stidston was, at tymm, on Wednesday, fined 20s. and costs for travelling from Manchester to Lynam on OctobAr 20th without having paid his fare aud with intent to avoid payment. When the tickets were examined at Lymm defendant replied, " Contract," whereas it was found he had no contract. Subsequently he give np a ticket which had been pnrchased six days previously.</p>
        <p>PUBLIO BENEFIT.—UNDER TYR CLOCK FOR BBST BOOTS TN T. ANTrI ARM-RM.—Advt. Wycliffe P.S.A. An open meeting of the Wycliffe Society was held on Sunday, when R3V. John Yonge presided. "Mr George Power, of Stocknort gave the address, speak ing upon" The Christmas Season, with its many dangers and temptations." Yet it had,said Mr. Power, great privileges, and opportunit;es for doing. good, helping the needy, and making the season bright in thnse homes ghere the clouds were heavy, and difficulties many. Miss E. Taylor sang sweetly" I know not what awaits me" and" Come untd Me." Mr. Payton presided at the organ, and Mr. Joseph Owen also took Fart in the meeting.</p>
        <p>An Exciting Chase. Police-constables Clough and Clarke had an exciting chase after an egg stealer, named Samuel Warburton, of Crossley-streq, Warrington on Wednesday night. A young man named Sexton, of the firm of Sexton Brothers, ega dealers. Orford-street, was delivering eggs at a shnp in Crnssley-street, when he saw Warburton. whose pockets were bulky, run from the back of his cart. He gave chase, but the man assaulted him and got away. The officers were informed, and after an exciting chase prisoner was captured. in his flight several eggs had been broken and prisoner's pockets were saturated with the sticky cantents.—At the Borough Court on Thursday Warburton was sent to gaol for three months with hard labour for stealing 30 eggs.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND,' Trade Mark of Kay's Compound Essence of Linseed for Coughs &amp;Colds.--Advt. Shorthand Writers Association.</p>
        <p>In the work of this Association the social element is not overlooked, for on Wednesday evening last a large number of members and friends met at the Winmarleigh Cafe and enjoyed the pleasures of a Whist Drive and Social. The arrangements for the function were admirably carried out by Mr. A N. Legge, who also acted as leader. It was unanimously felt. that a very happy and enjoyable evening had been spent, and a hearty appreciation of the work which had been so willingly given by Mr. Legge, and which had such a successful result, was given amid loud applause. The next meeting of the Association will take place on the Bth January, when Mr. H. B. Hewitt, the secretaty of the St. Helens S.W.A. will give a lecture on' Touch Typewriting" with demonstrations on a typewriter. At last week's meeting a very instructive lecture on'. Music as a Hobby" was given by Mr. F. M. Darby, Mus.Bac., F.R.C.O.</p>
        <p>CANDIED LEMON PEEL 40. per lb. at ASHLEY'S.—Advt. Sequel to a Cycling_ Accident.</p>
        <p>Ict the Warrington —County Court, on Thursday, Walter Henry Vaughan' solicitor, Didsbury, sued Joseph Bullock, hawker, 3, Jockey.street, Warrington, for £5 damages for personal injuries. Plaintiff is a member of the Manchester District Association of the Cyclists' Touring Club, by whom be was snnported in the action. He alleged that he was cycling from Warrington to Winwick when owing to the negligence of defendant, who was driving a horse and cart, he was thrown off his machine and sustained injuries. He had arranged with defendant's wife, who appeared for defendant, that he should pay him three guineas as compensation and costs, £1 to be paid within 14 daya, and the remainder of the debt and coati to be paid at the rate of 10s. a rnonth.— Defendant's wife agn to the terms, ard his Honour gave judgment to that effect. REVOLUTION INELECTRIC LIGHTINO.—" OSMM '9 Electric. Lamps reduce your current Bill one half. —'cent: J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, War- rington.— Advt. Dispute About a Piano. At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Shiress Will, K.C., Alice Norman, widow, Wash-lane, Latchford, sued her son, Thomas Herbert Norman, Mitchell-street, Stockton Heath, for £l4 18s., for groceries supplied and money lent, and also ;or the return of a piano.—Mr. W. J. Forsham apneared for the plaintiff, and said his Honour ought not to have been troubled with the ease Ind it would have been settled had it not been for the stupidity and ingratitude of the defendant. That morning defendant had admitted owing his mother Is. 7d, and she had agreed to accept that in settle- ment of the 2,14 18s.. so that the only question to be decided was the return of the piano. Plaintiff's hus- band died on August 30th, having made a will entirely in her favour. At one time the defendant lived at Sylvan Cottage, Latchford, and his father and mother resided at South View, Latchford. It was agreed that they should exchange houses, plaintiff and her husband having formerly lived at Sylvan Cottage. When the defendant's father was going to Sylvan Cottage he asked the defendant if he could leave the piano at Smith View with the defendant, as it was very difficult indeed to get it into Sylvan Cottage. This was agreed to and now when the defendant was asked to return the piano he refused, saying that it was given to him. Plaintiff gave evidence and emphatically denied that the piano was given to the defendant.—Amy Norman, defendant's sister, cor- roborated. -Defendant Still maintained that the piano was a gift to him. It had been in his house nearly six years, but he had had no peace since he got it.— His Honour decided that the piano was left at defend- ant's house for convenience and was the plaintiff's Property. He gave judgment for the return of the instrument within two days, or in the alternative defendant must nay £lO, its value. He ordered defendant to pay the is. 7d. and costs at the rate of 68 per month.—Defendant alma not give up the piano. —His Honour: You shall give it up or pay 'LINSEED COMPOUND 'of 40 vea rs' proven efficacy for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles.--Advt. FOR XMAS FRUIT, ASHLEY'S, Sankey-street.—Advt. English Church Union,</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington branch of the English Chnrch Union was held in the Masonic Hall on Wednesday evening, under the presidency of 11r. John White.—The following officers were elected: Chairman, Ur. John White ; vice-chairman, Rev. Wilfi id Stanton; secretary and treasurer, Mr. C. S. fiurgesq ; committee, Revs. J. H. G. Hollingworth and J. Holden, Messrs. G. Egerton Warburton and H. N. Floughton.—ln his address, the Chairman expressed regret at the absence of Mr. H. N. Houghton who, he said, had recently undergone a severe operation. Alluding to the events of the past year he said the Union had to a certain extent failed in their opposition to the Deceased Wife's Sister Act. Parliament had, however, learnt the lesson that although it might alter the laws of the realm, it could not alter the laws of the Church. He hoped the clergy would have suffi- cient backbone to carry out the law of the Church in its entirety. With regard to education, there was in all probability a harder fight yet to be faced and it was to be hoped that Churchpeople would show their determination to maintain the religious character of the voluntary schools.—The Ven. Archdeacon Barber, of Chester, moved: "That it is,expedient at the prc • sent time to alter our Prayer Book!. He raid it was inconceivable that anyone should propose to submit the Prayer Book to the deliberations of the present House of Commons. Nothing • seemly could result from such a discussion. It would be a dangerous thing to open the floodgates of criticism by any sug- gestion of alteration.—The Rev. J. H. G. Honing- worth-(vicar of St. Barnabas', Warrington), expressed the view that a ,revision would, be highly inexpedient in the present disunited state of the Church, the hostile attitude of Parliament, and the timidity shown in high quarters.—The resolution was unanimously carried.—Votes of thanks to Archdeacon Barber and the Chairman concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
