      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <p>MONEY LENDING TRANSACTION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Claim Against a Warrington Man.</head>
        <p>Claim Against a Warrington Man.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Shiress Will, K.C., Heber Marshall, Alcard-street, Folly-lane, was sued by the executors of the late John Wilding, Buttermarket-street, for £8 12s. 6d. being the balance due under a promissory note. Mr. Browne, who appeared for the plaintiffs, said defendant arranged to pay off the amount at so much per week, and since the case had been entered into court he had paid 25.6d.0n account.He said he only owed £5 odd but had since admitted that he agreed to the £8 12s. 6d. in June 1906. He, however, said he wanted to fight the case out—in other words he wanted to throw way 9s. hearing fee. George Thomas Wilding said he advanced £ll to the defendant under s promissory note in 1901. There was a balance now owing of £8 12s. 6d., less the 2s. 6d. paid on December 2nd.</p>
        <p>Defendant said he had paid £6 of the £ll loan. His honour: How is the £8 12s. 6d. explained? Mr. Wilding : That includes the interest since 1904. Mr. Browne : No interest has been charged from June, 1906, to the present time. His Honour: What is the amount charged for interest?</p>
        <p>Mr. Wilding: About £5. His Honour: M what rate?</p>
        <p>Mr. Wilding: It will run about 20 per cent. Since the arrangement was made in 1906. that defendant should pay £l2 at 2s. 6d. a week, 1 have not charged a halfpenny interest.</p>
        <p>. . Defendant now said he had done his utmost to pay, but he only earned 225. 2d. a week as a railway signal- man, and he had a big family and had had much sickness in the house.</p>
        <p>His Honour: The interest is not quite so easy as in commercial circles generally, but you might have expected pretty heavy interest on a Ivan like this. Are you expecting a rise in wages? Defendant: I have been receiving the paesent amount for eight years. _ _</p>
        <p>His Honour-: It looks as if a good time is com!lg. An order was made for payment of 3 10s. at 3s. per month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS TREE.—The annual Christmas Tree in count :don with St. Thomas' Church was held in the Church Room, Stockton Heath, on Saturday afternoon. The proceeds were in aid of the Society for the Prop .gation of the Gost-31 in Foreign Parts, and the Caurch of England Waifs and Strays Society. The Room was visited by a large number of parishioners and friends during the day, and the proceeds amounted to £6 ss. 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>BIGGEST TWEED SALMON.</head>
        <p>BIGGEST TWEED SALMON.</p>
        <p>Whilst the Tweed bailiffs were removing spawning salmon which had been attacked by disease, they got the biggest salmon ever taken from the river, at lust so far as there is any authentic record. The fish, which was obtained from Lord Po'worth Mertoun stretch, was weighed before being buried, and it turned the scale at 60th. The next biggest salmon got from the Tweed so far as known was one of 57,111 b., caught by an angler on the Duke of Roxburghe's water aboht twenty years ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>VALUABLE \PRESCRIPTION. – Cures Rheumatism, Kidney Trouble and Bladder Troubles, Indigestion and Constipation.</head>
        <p>A VALUABLE \PRESCRIPTION. - Cures Rheumatism, Kidney Trouble and Bladder Troubles, Indigestion and Constipation.</p>
        <p>We know that the readers of this paper appreciate good advice, and we are glad, therefore, to be able to publish the followin,g from a prominent physician. It is astonishing to know the prevalence of kidney trouble and other diseases, such as rheumatism, bladder and liver troubles, which are almost invariably caused by weak kidneys. it is said on, good authority that fully one-third of the people in England who have reached the age of maturity, are more or less afflicted with kidney trouble in some form. The most dangerous feature of this disease is the fact that many people have it without their knowledge, and consequently neglect it until it has reached a dangerous Eace.</p>
        <p>The least symptom of kidney trouble, such as back- ache, must be given prompt attention, and a good reliable treatment should be taken, until every trace of the disease has been eliminated. We are glad to be able to give our readers a prescription which we have seldom known to fail in any case. It contains no harmful ingredients, is pleasant to take, not expen- sive, and can be supplied by any good chemist. It is as follows :</p>
        <p>Tincture G-entian Compound, one ounce ; Syrup Rhubarb, one ounce ; Liquid Barkola Compound, one ounce ; Syrup of Ginger, five ounces. Take a teaspoonful after each meal, and one at bed-time.</p>
        <p>We are glad to publish this prescription, as we know it will help our afflicted readers who give it a fair trial. If taken continuously for a few weeks, it will be found to be an almost absolutely certain cure for Rheumatism, Gout and Lumbago, Sciatica, Kidney trouble, Bright's disease, Diabetes, Urinary and Uric acid disease, Bladder trouble, Gravel, Enlarged Prostate, Indigestion, Constipation, Sick Headache, Backache, and all allied complaints.</p>
      </div>
