      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>MY DEAR CHILD.</head>
        <p>MY DEAR CHILD.</p>
        <p>Arother week gone, and we are nearly into the Christmas holidays. I am afraid many of my members are disappointed at not seeing their letters and contributions in our columns. • Well, I am sorry that I cannot print all, on account of the very limited space I have at my disposal, so you must take the "will for the deed." Several contributors have sent letters and con- tributions with only their names attached, and no addresses. I hope all my members will carefully note this, as I have had several letters saying that the little mirrors have failed to reach them. I have now given away a large number of mirrors, and in future I shall not send any more to contributors unless specially asked to do so'. I shall be very pleased to do this when requested. Will all contributors in the " New Member" Competition send me during this month their full names and addresses on a post-card? _ _</p>
        <p>I must thank Winnie Elliott and Elsie Talbot for some excellent articles for the poor. You will note that I have received a very nice letter from a member living in Canada. I hope to have further -letters from this interesting country. Many thanks to the following members for letters and contributions :—Marion Farmer, Ernest Doney, James Doney, Elsie Holden, Elizabeth Stock, Evan Lane, Alice Ebury, Margaret Press, Milly Talbot, Annie Harring- ton, Doris Talbot, Alice King, and Ada Batley. Will my members kindly note that I should prefer short stories and jokes to any other form of contribution? With love,</p>
        <p>UNCLE NED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>WELCOME TO</head>
        <p>WELCOME TO</p>
        <p>Katherine Seavons, Albert .Thavons, J. C. Codfish, Cissie •Fletcher, Emily Palmer, Lizzie Kirby, Annie Edwards, Edith Edwards, Veronica Moran, Sidney Tompkins, Elsie Holden, May Prin6e, Eva Bennett, Annie Harrington.</p>
        <p>Berlin, Ontario, Canada, November 18th, 1907.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—Just a line about my pet cat called Tom. I found him one day on the road, when a kitten, and he has been very interesting ever since. We would put him out- do6rs, and when he wanted to come in he would make his way through a screen door, which we had to keep out flies. There was a little hole in it, so he worked and worked at it till he could get. his body through. Mother thought she would put a stop to it, so she patched it, and, do you know, he made his way in between the new and old piece. Soon after we moved to Berlin here, but did not bring Torn. We asked the ether minister that was taking father's place to take good care of him, and he did so. Then one day we got a post- card announcing he would drive here and ‘isit us, and would give Tom a ride also. You should just have heard us children shout when we heard-this. Well. here he is yet. He is black and white, and keeps himself clean. He is very fond of toffy or any sweet things, and he is so very disappointed if we don't have potatoes for dinner. But lam making my letter too long. I will try and send you a story very soon. With love to my cousins and your self, I will close. From your niece,</p>
        <p>BARTOL SCHOFIELD.</p>
        <p>AN ODD WAY OF CATCHING THEM,</p>
        <p>When I was a boy at school," said my grandfather, "I was often very idle. Even during lessons I used to play with other boys as idle as myself; Of course, we tried to hide this from the master, but. one day we were fairly caught. Boys,' said he, you must not be idle; you must attend to your books. You do not know what you lose, by being idle now. Youth is the time to learn. Any one of you who sees another boy looking off his book will please come and tell me.' ' Ah,' thought I to myself, 'there is Joe Smith, whom I don't like; I'll watch him, and if I see him 'look off his book, I'll tell.' Not very long afterwards I saw Joe look off his book, and I at once marched up and told the master. 'lndeed,' said he; 'how do you know he was idle?' 'Please sir,' said I, 'I saw him." Oh, you did, did you? and were your eyes on your book when you saw him?' "I was fairly caught; the other boys laughed, and I hung my head, while the master smiled. I never watched for idle boys again." If we watch over dur own conduct, ,and always do our own duty, we shall have no time to watch for faults or idleness in others.</p>
        <p>LIZZIE KIRBY.</p>
        <p>CREW.</p>
        <p>The noble Newfoundland dog has often been the means of saving the life of a drowning man, but here is a story of one who saved the whole crew. A heavy gale was blowing when a vessel was seen drifting towards the coast of Kent. She struck on the beach, and the break- ing waves dashed over her in foam. Eight men were seen holding on to the wreck, but no ordinary boat could go to their aid in such a sea; and in those days there were no life-boats —at least there were none on that part of the coast. The people on shore feared every moment that the poor sailors would be washed off the ship, and drowned, for although the ship was not far from the land it was too far for any one to swim through the foaming breakers. If a rope could be taken from the wreck to the shore, the sailors might be saved. A gentleman who was standing on the beach with a large Newfoundland dog by his side thought he saw how it could be managed. He put a short stick in his dog's mouth, and then pointed to the vessel. The brave dog knew what his master wanted, and, springing into the Sea, he fought his way bravely through the waves. When he reached the ship he tried to climb up its side, but in vain. He was seen. however, by the crew, and they made fast a light rope to another piece of wood. He then turned his head towards the shore to carry it to his master. This time the wind and waves helped him on Iris way; but. he was almost. worn out when he reached the land, and, drag- ging the rope after him, he laid the piece of wood at his master's feet. A stronger rope was then tied to the first one by the sailors, and One end of it was pulled to the shore. Along this rope the sailors made their way one by one to the land, and in this way every man on board was saved through the courage and wisdom of the dog. By next day the storm was over, and the sailors were able to row out. to their ship and save a large part. of the cargo. A week afterwards they got the ship itself off the rof;ks, and took it into the harbour to be repaired. ALBERT EARNSHAW.</p>
        <p>BABY'S TROUBLES I had a little brother,</p>
        <p>Who was so like a girl, He played with dolls and tea-sets, _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>And had his hair in curl. He wore a little frock of blue, And used to look so sweet, With a pretty sash of blue Just reaching to his feet. But now he's grown just like a boy, I feel so sad to say, His pretty yellow curls were all Cut off the other day. He does not like my tea-set now, And seldom plays with me; I loved my little brother more When he was only three. He will not wear a frock like mine, Although he used to do; For now he feels so happy in A navy suit of blue. ,</p>
        <p>VERONICA MORAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>HOW TRUE.</head>
        <p>HOW TRUE.</p>
        <p>A country gentleman, walking up a staircase which had a mirror at the top, bowed to him- self, thinking that his reflected image was an acquaintance. "I wonder," said he to the friend with him, "who that seedy-looking fellow is? I have seen his face somewhere." His friend laughed, but bad not the heart to tell him of his mistake. A man may not know his own face, and may forget also the faces of other people. MAY ANDREWS.</p>
        <p>THE CUCKOO. Hail! beauteous stranger of the grove, Thou messenger of spring, Now heaven repairs thy rural seat, And woods thy welcome ring.</p>
        <p>What time the daisy decks the green Thy certain voice we hear; Rat thou a star to guide thy path Or mark the rolling year? Delightful visitant! with thee I hail the time of flowers, And hear the sound of music sweet From birds among the bowers. The schoolboy wandering through the wood To pluck the primrose gay, Starts, thy curious voice to hear. And imitates thy lay.</p>
        <p>What time the pear puts on the bloom, Thou fliest the vocal vale, An annual guest, in other lands Another spring to hail.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>"WHAT'S SAUCE FOR THE GOOSE-"</head>
        <p>"WHAT'S SAUCE FOR THE GOOSE-"</p>
        <p>A mischievous boy, walking down the street one day, threw a stone at a pigeon, and knocked it over. Tbe bird fell in the mud, but quickly recovered- itself, and flew out of reach Lefore the boy couid manage to capture it. A woman who was passing at the time caught hold of the offender by the ear, and gave him a sound drubbing with her umbrella, accom- panying this exercise by loud cries against the cruelty of his conduct to poor, dumb creatures. "if I was your mother," she said, as she treated him to a parting cuff on the side of his head, "I would whip ou to one inch of your life! and, if I had my way, I would pass a law, inaking it possible to send every boy to prison who threw stones at poor, innocent birds!" and she walked off down the street in a state of virtuous indignation. As she went, a bystander remarked that she had upon her hat a stuffed swallow and the wings of a couple of sea-birds—beautiful creatures, whose lives had been taken because a passing fashion called for the sacrifice; and this philosopher said to him- self that there are some folk who fail to see that "what is sauce for the goose is sauce for the gander." ADA BATLEY.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—My favourite hobby is mice- keeping. I have seven mice, some black and white, and some brown and white. They eat mixed corn, and bread soaked in milk. For a bed I give them wadding and little feathers. A few days ago I bought a new mouse, and put it with the others, and the next day, on going to feed them, I found it dead; its head was bitten right off, and the skin nearly all off its back. I found out this had been done by the other mice, because it was a fresh one. And now, Uncle Ned, I think it time to close my letter, and hope you will accept me as a mem- ber. I hope you will kindly find room in the paper next week for this. SIDNEY TOMPKINS.</p>
        <p>A LITTLE HERO. The patter of feet was on the stair, As the editor turned in his sanctum chair, And said—for weary the day had been— " Don't let another intruder in."</p>
        <p>But scarce had he uttered the words, before A face peered in at the half-closed door, And a child sobbed out, "Sir, mother said I should come and tell you that Dan is dead."</p>
        <p>"And praygwho is ' Dan ' f " The streaming eyes Looked questioning up, with a strange surprise; "Not know him? Why, sir, all day he sold The papers you print, through wet and cold. q The newsboys say that they could not tell The reason his stock went off so well. I knew !.—with his voice so sweet and low, Could anyone bear to say him ' No ? ' "And the money he made, whatever it be, He carried straight home to mother and gie, No matter about his rags, he said, If he only kept us clothed and fed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <p>200 YEARS A SECRET.</p>
        <p>GRANNIE OINTMENT FOR SKIN TROUBLES.</p>
        <p>For 200 Years the Secret of Grannie Ointment has been kept in one Family. Its curative action is wonderful in all cases of WOUNDS. CUTS, BRUISES, BAD LEGS, and all SKIN TROUBLES. It at once soothes and heals, and cannot hurt the most delicate skin, In many cases of very long standing it has quickly cured, even when doctors have despaired. Frae for 14stamps. GRAN NIE COMPANY, NORWICH.</p>
      </div>
