      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS</p>
        <p>" MILK " FROM FISH.</p>
        <p>Many cuttle fish farms—great tanks where the fish are bred for their black milk, which gives us India ink—are to be found on the Chinese coast, observes the Reader. To milk a cuttle fish, the queer creature is driven slowly and gently into a compartment or lock lined with metal. Then the water is gradu- ally drawn off, and the fish in the end finds himself left high and dry. He becomes alarmed, and squirts forth all the sepia in his sack. Then, milked dry, he is returned to his tank again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>FORESTS AND WIND VELOCITY.</head>
        <p>FORESTS AND WIND VELOCITY.</p>
        <p>M. St. Murat, director of the Meteorologi- cal Institute of Roumania, has published the results of his study of the retarding influence of forests on wind velocity in the Annales of the Roumanian Academy. He finds that the greatest effect which a forest can have upon the ' wind consists in diminishing the wind velocity to leeward of the forest. At 164 ft. this decrease in velocity may amount to 4 to 7-4- miles an hour, which means a reduction of the force of the wind by ldeg. on the Beau- fort scale. This decrease is felt within 330 ft. of the forest. After that the velocity in- creases again with increasing distance, and at about 1,6401 t. reaches the force noted before the forest was encountered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>STRONGEST OF ALL WOODS.</head>
        <p>STRONGEST OF ALL WOODS.</p>
        <p>Official tests of the many valuable hard woods native to Western Australia have made known the extraordinary properties of yate, believed to be the .strongest of all known woods. Its average tensile strength is 24,0001 b., to the square inch, equalling that of good cast iron. Many specimens are, how- ever, much stronger, and one was. tested up to seventeen and a-half tons to the square inch, which is equal to the tensile strength of wrought iron. The sawn timber of yate is probably the strongest in the world. The tree grows to a maximum height of 100 ft., and has sometimes a diameter of 24-ft. or even 3ft.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>MOTH AND THE CANDLE.</head>
        <p>THE MOTH AND THE CANDLE.</p>
        <p>What causes a moth to fly into a candle? There are many theories on the subject. Pro- fessor Loeb sees in filo, insect's flight candle- ward only a directive effect of light similar to that; which causes the flower to turn to- wards the sun, while others ascribe the effect purely to the insect's curiosity. One authority notes that only flying and swim- ming creatures are thus affected, and he ex- plains this by reminding us that such crea- tures must rely for their guidance almost wholly on Sight. Hence a bright light in the night attracts them, because it gives rise to the sole external impulse that can act upon them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>LIGHT AND COLORATION.</head>
        <p>LIGHT AND COLORATION.</p>
        <p>An interesting phenomenon discovered Ey Marckwaldt consists of an alteration occur- ring in the colour of a body under the action of light, while the original coloration is re- established in the ,dark. The substances shewing this phenomenon are very few in number, though, according to communication by Professor H. Stobbe at the Congress of German Naturalists and Physicists, the group of fulgides contains a number of substances of this kind. This phenomenon, called photo. trophy, is of especial importance, says the Scientific American, for chemical and physi- cal science, as possibly embodying an immedi- ate conyersion of light waves into chemical energy. It might become extremely' valuable for photography or for any other art utilising radiations of a similar nature.</p>
        <p>POWER OF THE INFINITESIMALL", SMALL.</p>
        <p>The Laifeet gives some striking examples of the importance of little things. We know what infinitesimally small quantities of cer- tain substances will put an end to the great vital processes, and we know also how end- less appears to be the action of the enzymes or ferments which render food assimilable so that the, same vital processes are sustained. A thirtieth part of a grain of aconitine will kill the human organism, one part of an enzymes will transform 100,000 parts of cane sugar into invert sugar, the enzymp of malt will convert a thousand times its weioht of starch into sugar, and so forth. Nor is the enormous action of infinitesimally small quantities confined to the organic or or- ganised world. Even certain materials devoid of life are found to exert a similar action.</p>
        <p>POISONING PLATINUM.</p>
        <p>- - - - Platinum, for example, in the colloidal state is capable of decomposing 1,000,000 :times its weight of hydrogen * peroxide into water and oxygen, and then of :remaining as strong 41 d as active as ever. Perhaps the most remarkable fact in connection with the extraordinary " vitality" of colloidal plati- num is that its energies are at once paralysed by such ordinary animal poisons as prussic acid, corrosive sublimate, or sulphuretted hydrogen. The platinum may thus be said to be poisoned, and such a small quantity as one-millionth of a °O-rain of prussic acid is sufficient to prevent this , great transforming power. To give another example of the de- cided 'effect of minute traces of various sub- stkraces, it has been found that certain water organisms are destroyed in water contained in a copper vessel, • and yet the quantity of copper present is only one part in a thousand .million parts of water. Such effects, the Lancet adds, are impres- sive, and they are calculated to iMpress us still .more when we contemplate the number of processes going on in the human machine •which \are dependent upon the action of small 'things.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>OPTICIANS AND SEA SIGNALLING.</head>
        <p>OPTICIANS AND SEA SIGNALLING.</p>
        <p>'There is probably no other department of life in which the instruments of observation and of precision furnished by opticians are •!greater ckimand than in the. Navy and the .mereantile.. marine.. This fact itself, con- sidered from the optician's point of view, lends considerable interest to the sea-going profession; but there .are other special grbunds also upon which our readers, says the Optician, may profitably turn their atten- tion to the requirements of the shipping world. Although the geeat outward and visi- ble f.ign of the change that is going on. in our trade is the extension and development of sight-testing, yet seeing that this has its root in diffusion of a •</p>
        <p>KNOWLEDGE OF TECHNICAL OPTICS, we believe there are other modihcations of the opticiani,s practiee to be traced directly -to the *same cause.</p>
        <p>For instance., the line of demarcation be- tween the purely marine opticians and others in the seaport towns appears to be losing its ...distinctness. The marine optician may not undertake sight-testing, but he does find that bis customers expect an understanding of the use and application of corrective lenses on his part. Conversely, the different types of nautical apparatus are not only more widely sold but much more generally understood. Marine signrlling, .which is .so vital a mat- er for the • safety of shipping, requires not only telescopes, but various . other instru- nients that are the product of the optician's art; and there is still great scope for the exercise of optical ingenuity, in the ,improve- ment of. methods now in use. The air, ether, and water, have all been turned to account for transmission purposes, the electric wave and</p>
        <p>UNDER-WATER SOUND SIGNAL being developments of the use of lamp and foghorn. Further, it would seem that there are still regions in the spectrum that await being turned to valuable account in this sense, so soon as the necessary optical de- vices can be produced for dealing with them. Thus the immediate infra-red region of the spectrum presents a 'field that, it is thought, may be exploited . for the purpose of signal- ling through heavy mist or fog; and as this form of radiation would i be far more re- than the so-called electric waves, it foltows that the direction from which such signals were received could be determined, ....Inch would be a very great advantage.</p>
        <p>fr</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>VOLUNTEERS.</head>
        <p>THE VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>.4!t!'</p>
        <p>Tit*</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>Warrington made a big effort to check the undefeated Oldham team at Watersheddings on Saturday, but were beaten after a great effort before 12,000 spectators. There was a lot of heavy rain in the morning, but the day improved and there was a fine afternoon for the match, while the ground was in very fair. .order. Both teams were at full strength, and turned out in the following order : Oldham—Thomas, full back ; Tyson' W. Dixon, Llewellyn and Oldershaw, three-quarter bucks; White and Beynon, half backs ,• Ferguson, Avery, Smith, Topham, Longworth, and Wilkinson, forwards. Warrington—Tilley, full back ; Brooks, Taylor, Dickinson, and Fish, three-quarter backs ; S. Lees and Jenkins, half backs; Boardman, Stuxgars, Thomas, Belton,Naylor, and Harmer, forwards. Referee, Mr. F. Renton, Hnnslet ; touch judges, Messrs. J. E. Smith, Rochdale Hornets, and A. Hest ford, Broughton Rangers. Oldham were the first to effectively attack, and after Dixon bad crossed and been ordered back for a forward pass, Ferguson made a series of marks in the visitors' quarter, and at the third attempt kicked a goal. The Warrington me,n replied to this with a spirited burst right from the re-start, and Lees sent the ball to Fish, who bored through the Oldham defence and scored with a couple of opponents hanging on to him. A fairly even game ensued, Oldham having slightly the better of the exchanges, especially in the passing work among their backs, the visitors holding their own forward. It was almost half-time, however, before Oldham drew ahead through a try scored by Ferbmson after a long struggle close on the visitors' line. At the interval Oldham lea by 6 points to 3. This lead was lost two minutes after the re-opening of the game, through the referee awarding Warrington a goal as the result of a drop kick by Taylor, which apparently sent the ball under the bar. Oldham how- ever, soon regained the lead with another scrambling try, Beynon capturing the ball at full length as it rolled from under a struggling heap of opponents. The goal awarded to Warrington had evoked a great outburst of indignation against the referee, but this try appeased them to some extent. Warrington continued to play fine football, and Fish made a great effort to score, but. was stopped near the line. In the closing stages War- rington put on great pressure, and after a fine run by Brooks and Thomas, the former got over at the corner only to be brought back by the touch judge. Final ; Oldham,l. goal 2 tries (8 points) ; Warrington, I goal 1 try (5 points).</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday December 21, 1907.</p>
        <p>. ~ , .. • _ - -_.s , ---, - • ; . ' . k ......, i r-7.-:-...7.1,7= A , -......._______ ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>PRIZE DISTRIBUTION AT THE HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PRIZE DISTRIBUTION AT THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Praise for Mr. Haldane's Scheme</p>
        <p>CRITICISM OF THE PEACESEIErY.</p>
        <p>Great interest centred in the aanuAl Christ fl ts prize distribution in connection with the Ist Volunteer Battalion (P.W.V.) South Lancashire Regiment which took place at the Hippodrome on Monday evening. The function was the most successful of its kind on record,being attended by a very large and representative gathering including the Mayor and Mayoress (Alder- man and Mrs. Smethurst). The members of the Battalion and their friends assembled in strong force and the bnilding, which is admirably adapted for such a meeting, presented an animated scene. The invited guests were accommodated in the circle, while the Volunteers and their fiiends had seats in the pit and gallery. Lord Stanley, Chairman of the West Lanca- shire County Association Territorial Force, had beau announced to present the prizes but to the regret lof all, illness prevented his attendance. His place was taken by General fle C. J. 1 Burnett, Commander of the Western Division, ! Chester. The proceedings opened with the regi- mental march and the overture "Jubilation " (Rimmer) admirably played by the band of the battalion, conducted by Bandmaster Richardson. An , excellent variety entertainment provided by the management of the Hippodrome was then entered upon and proved interesting and enj ,yable. A pleasant , surprise was afforded by the appearance of George ' Robey, the famous comedian, who is this week falallieg an engagement at Birke.ahead and : came down to Warrington specially for the occasion. Nee:Hess to say, he kept the audience in a state of great hilarity during three songs given in his inimitable style. ;i is most amusing turn " was his impersonation of a Mayor, for he presented a ludicrous figure clad in the , official robes, with a huge chain which threatened to ' get entangled with his feet, and cirrying a bundle of imposing•looking documents. At the close the great "star " was applauded to the echo. Other features of the programme included humorous and senti- mental songs, pretty dances, selections by the Palace orchestra, and a fine series of animated pictures. During an interval in the entertainment, the prize distribution took place. The uprising curtain disclosed a table draped with Union Jacks and covered with silver trophies won by successful competitors. At the table were seated General Sir C.J. Burnett,Lieutenant- Colonel Edelsten (commanding the battalion), and a number of officers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>VOLUNTEERS' CREDITABLE R ECOR</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEERS' CREDITABLE R ECOR</p>
        <p>COLONEL EDELSTEN AND TILE PEACE SOCI ET Y. Lieutenant-Colonel EDELSTEN announced that he had received the following telegram from Lord Stanley 'Very sorry owing to my being confined to bed by severe chill am unable to fulfil my engagement to pre- sent prizes to your battalion. Please express to all ranks my most sincere regret and my earnest 'hope that in the formation of the Territorial Army Assocation, over which I have the honour to be Chairman, may have their cordial support and co-operation.—Stanley.', They were, continued Colonel Edelsten, deeply dis- appointed that Lord Stanley was unable to be with them and their disappointment was increased by the fact that illness was the cause. Continuing the Colonel said that as far as 1907 was concerned, it was, to use Mr. Haldane's words, an interim year and it left them iery much as it found them. They wera down a little in strength—some twenty or thirty men—and their recruiting was not up to the usual standard, but be did not think very much importance need be attached to either of those diminutions,. as he attributed them both to the same cause, and that a temporary one— namely, that they were restricted this year to a camp of one week. Those who had been accustomed to go to camp year after year for a fortnight 'began to take their volunteering more seriously and to them a camp of a week was a terrily inaufficient affair, and he was afraid he had heard a week's camp described in very hard and unrepeatable words. (Laughter.) However, h,ppily that was one of the the things which were going to be rectified by</p>
        <p>THE COMING CHANGES</p>
        <p>which were almost the sole topic of Conversation in Volunteer circles. They were saying " Good-night " to the Volunteer movement as this generation had known it. They had been passing through the twilight in this interim year. Some of them had been doing some night work, and ha wanted them to look upon that evening as ushering I in locally the dawn of the territorial day. (Applause.) Warrington had no reason to he ashamed of its part under the old Volunteer system. (Applause.) As to quantity, at the present time Warrington furnished a larger con- tribution to the Volunteers in proportion to its pcpula- tion than any other town or urban district in Lancashire —and Lancashire was acknowledged to be the premier county in, Volunteering. (Applause.) Warrington furhished 1.5 that was 11 inhabitants per 100 of its population to the Volunteers. Great Britain as a whole furnished approximately •75 per cent.—that was fl of a person per 100,and if that could be taken as the national standard of duty, then Warrington not only performed its duty, lbut in recent years had performed it doubly. (Applause.) It was not for him to express any opinion in regard to quality or to confide to them any com- ments he might have heard upon it, but there were one or two indisputable facts to which he would refer. They had only once been called upon to furnish troops for active service—that was in the South African war—and they sent out over 25 per cent, in officers and 25 per cent. in men of their strength at the beginning of that campaign—a percentage which he believed compared very favourably with those of other places. About that time it was decided to select 27 battalions for special training in peace and special duties in war, and the Warrington battalion was one of the 27. Later, under a meditated reform which did not fructify, it was intended to differentiate still further and to select eight battalions out of those 27 for -a special purpose. He had reason to believe that the Warrington corps was to have been ono of the eight. (Applause). Coming to more visible and tangible things —their increased buildings, waggons, harness, cycles, mobilization stores which were just completed—they had managed to provide these without the usual bazaar —(laughter)—with no talisman but what he might venture to cal) clean and careful finance—and so to put Warrington into a positior, should the demand arise, to place not only the largest contingent of Volunteers in the field in proportion to its population, but to place them there as quickly and fully equipped—perhaps a little more quickly than anyone else. (Applause). The nation was about to embark on important changes and he wished them to know</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>HOW WARRINGTON STOOD</head>
        <p>HOW WARRINGTON STOOD</p>
        <p>lat the jumping-off point. After all, historically it was only a repeat. In 1796-7-8 our enemies were making • attempts on the coasts of Wales and Ireland and the ; Loyal Warrington Volunteers since .called the Blue Backs, sprang immediately into being. When a few • years later, just before the battle of Tralfalgar, the , danger of serious invasion bad become imminent and 1500 Warrington Volunteer infantry were called for, 11,300 responded to the call. (Applause.) Warrington bad always been a loyal town and he laid the past before them to ask a question which he was sare Lord Stanley I would have asked :—" Will Warrington embrace and I• support the new territorial scheme with the same spirit that it has acted in the past and in the maintenance of . its traditions ?" Having had opportunities of closely ! studying the scheme during Cie past few months, he might perhaps be allowed to express the parsonal opinion that the scheme was sound, that it was</p>
        <p>A MASTERPIECE OF THOUGHT</p>
        <p>and that it was suitable for a business community. In support of that opinion be would like to say that in his hearing recently the heads of the two chief cities of our County—the Lord Mayors of Manchester and Liverpool—gave their unstinted approval, after con- sideration, to the scheme and while approaching it from totally divergent points of view, they accorded it their recommendation, especially to the employers of labour in the cities. He (Colonel Edelsten) had not the slight- est doubt that if Lord Stanley had been present he would have taken the opportunity of begging the support of the local employers of labour to the scheme, for it was fully realized by all who had anything to do with it, that its success or failure depended upon employers of labour carefully considering the sugges- tion and the alternative that must follow if this failed and deciding whether the scheme was worthy of any sacrifice. With slight sacrifices on their part, it was believed that the scheme could succeed. Without those small sacrifices there must be no course but to compel greater ones. Warrington had had the advantage of much</p>
        <p>MILITARY AND ANTI-MILITARY ORIIDRY Of late. They had been visited by able exponents from what he might call the extreme schools, by those who would have every man drilled and more or less a soldier and by the Peace Society which he thought was a little concerned at the loyal state of Warring- ton. He did not know whether he quite rightly apprehended the tenets of that well meaning Society, but so far as he grasped them, it seemed to him that while recognizinc, the absurdity of urgino: that Great Britain alone sliould dis Arm, they would raduce the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>WHAT PUDDING IS IT TO-DAY?</head>
        <p>WHAT PUDDING IS IT TO-DAY?</p>
        <p>Be ready at least three days a week with the answer: "A pudding with lots of currants in it." The craving which little folks have for fruits and sweetmeats is a natural one, and should be gratified by supplying them with that most healthful and nourishing of all fruits— dried currants.</p>
        <p>Your Grocer will give you free on request a booklet entitled " Currants—A few Tasty. Recipes," which is full of useful recipes suitable for the family dinner.</p>
      </div>
