      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD.—The members of this auild held their annual Chriatmas Social on Monday evening. The room had been prettily decorated by the lady members. I The Rev. W. Nienor presided. 4 musical programme was first given in which songs were well rendered by Mrs. Dutton, Nliss Aason, Miss Ward, and Dr Bennett, after which the v. W. Mellor gave a reading. After refreshments hal beet served the rest of the evening was spent in a winner of amusing competitions and games. A now font:ire o the guild work was decided during the evening, this being the formation of a reading circle. THE VICAR'S IMPENDING DEPARTURE.—Before cOM- mencing his sermon on Sunday evening, the Vicar, the Rev. W. Pollock-Hill, referred to his impending depar- ture to become vicar of St. Thomas', Ashton-in-Maker- field. He said that in Padoto he had wade many friends and for many reasons he regretted the sever- ance of his present associations. The new living had been unsought by him, and before accepting it he had consulted the Bishop of Liverpool, who advised him to accept it. It was a larser parish, having a population of 11,000. and a far wider sphere of labour, but he would have two assistant clergy working with him. It was, however, not very far away and he hoped to see his friends many times. THE NATIONAL SOCIETY.. —0 Sunday morning a sermon w,is preached in Chriat Church, Padgate, on oehalf of this society. by the Rey. .1. Spurrell, organiz- ing secretary for the North of Englind. He said that great attacti.s were being tirade on the iChurch of England, and the attack wts now mainly directed against the schools, but this was only a flank movement against the Church itself. Ile pleaded with all who had the 'religious training of our children at heart, to oppose any effort to re novo the Bible and its teaching from our elementary schools.</p>
      </div>
