      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <p>8</p>
        <p>children who did not recieve the care and attention they ought. He felt that that would be a valuable institution, and he wished the work every success, and had pleasure in declaring the room open.— WIDNES WORKS ACCIDENT. Mr. H. Lee J. Jones (of the Liverpool Food and Better- Edwin Guy, 39, labourer, of Park-road, is detained at ment Association) delivered an address,. and said that the Widnes Accident Hospital suffering from a broken after fifteen years' night and day life In the slums of leg sustained by the falling of a pole at the Widnes Liverpool, he had come to the conclusion that the chief Foundry where he works. Dr. Brebner has set the limb cause of poverty was not misconduct but misfortune. and the patient is doing well. He did not wish to minimise the terrible evil of drink, RAILWAY SMASH AT WIDNES. but it seemed to him there was a greater cause of poverty than drink, and that was the lack of and the On Monday evening three empty passenger coaches irregularity of employment. Dr. Cotton, in moving a left the permanent way just outside Widnes London and vote of thanks to the Mayor and Mr. Lee Jones, said North-Western station. The line was completely the district nurses co-operated with them, and did what blocked for some hours and Liverpool, Warrington and Manchester traffic had to be diverted. , they could to help the children by dressing their wounds. The members of the committee afterwards R.S.P.C.A. CASES AT RUNCORN. held a short conference with Mr. Jones. Amongst those At the Rnncorn Petty Sessions, on Monday, before present at the opening were Miss Gamble, of Windle. Messrs. E. liaison and J. Ockleston, Robert Gatley was hurst, and Miss M'Lean, of the Railway Men's Mission. -summoned for having worked a horse which was in an until condition on the 3rd inst., and Arthur Riley, Bridge-street, was summoned for having allowed it to be worked.—lnspector Nye, of the R.S.P.C.A., said he saw Gatley driving the horse along Halton-road. It was going lame. It walked well, but was unfit to trot. He saw Mr. Riley, who said if he thought it was unfit it should not go out again, but would be shot. He did not regard it as a bad case, and did not press it.— Defendants were ordered to pay the costs, Is. 6cl. Frederick Ratcliffe and Thomas Briscoe, the latter manager of the Church-street shop of Messrs. Lewis and Calvert, were summoned for similar offences.—The Inspector said he saw Ratcliffe driving a horse past the Central Station. It was lame on the near hind leg. It had badly contracted tendons which were very much inflamed. He saw Mr. Briscoe who said the animal was stiff from a chills and was not lame when it turned out in the morning.—The defendants were ordered to pay the coats, is. Gd. PECULIAR WIDNES CASE.—POLICE REBUFFED. At Widnes Police Court on Monday morning, before the Mayor (presiding), Alderman Wareing and Mr. J. B. Aitken, John Lynch, of Victoria-road, was charged on remand with having been drunk and disorderly on the sth December. He was further charged with having assaulted P.C. Thornton and Detective-Sergeant Davidson. He was also charged with having damaged the woodwork of his cell to the extent of 4s. He said he did not remember anything about the drunkenness. He denied the assault and the damage.—P.C. Thornton I said that at 6.15 p.m. on the date in question he saw the prisoner in Lugsdale-road. He was drunk and shouting. Witness requested him to be quiet, but he refused, and began to use threatening and abusive language. Witness took him into custody, and he at once became very violent and kicked witness on the shoulder, legs, and under the chin. He also twisted his thumb, severely spraining it. Witness had been off duty since. Detective-Sergeant Davidson and P.C. Martin, gave corroborative evidence.—Sergeant Blackball said that on visiting the prisoner in his cell he found that he had brokensorne woodwork and thrown the pieces outside through the trap door.—Prisoner said the woodwork was broken before he went into the cell. It was placed so that it would fall to pieces when anybody touched it. It was a trap. The Superin- tendent knew as much about it as anybody.—Mrs. Lynch said that on the date in question she and her husband were going home together. Her husband had had drink, but was perfectly quiet. The policeman came up to him and said, "You will have to go with me." He then got hold of her husband, and the two fell on the floor struggling, the policeman being on top. She asked the policeman to let her hnsband go home and report and summon him, but he would not.—P.C. Thornton, recalled, said he would have let the prisoner .go home but for his• threatening language.—The Chair- man said the Bench had decided to dismiss the case.— The Magistrates' Clerk (Mr. R. W. H. Thomas).—There are four cases.—Alderman Wareing said the Bench pro- posed to dismiss the lot.—The Magistrates' Clerk: He pleads guilty to being drunk.—Alderman Wareing said it was the opinion of the Bench that the prisoner should have been summoned. That was the proper procedure. In his opinion there would have been no .assault had the prisoner not been interfered with.</p>
        <p>LICENSING PROSECUTION AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday, before Messrs. E. Imison and J. Ockleston, Thomas Fraine, licensee of the Rose and Shamrock Inn, Mersey-street, was charged with having permitted drunkenness on his premises on November 30th. He pleaded not guilty. Mr. W. H. Churton, of Chester, appeared to prosecute, and the defendant was represented by Mr. %V. Rudd, of Liverpool.—Mr. Churton stated the facts of the case, and said the Chief Constable felt very strongly that a licensee's plain duty when a man was drunk on his ' premises was to get rid of him at once.—lnspector Deanaaid that be and Sergt. Williams visited defend- Ant's house at ten past ten on the evening of the date in luecticra. In the parlour the defendant was standing, And two men were sitting on either side of the fire. There were three full glasses of beer, two on a -table and the other on the mantelpiece. 'One of the glasses was in front of a man named Higgins, who was asleep. He asked defendant what the man was doing there asleep,and be made no reply. The other man said he was tired, and had had a long walk. Defendant awakened Higgins, and witness could see that be was drank, and said so. He asked defendant about the glasses of beer. He said that one belonged to him, one for the other man, and the third to a man who had gene to the back. They went to the back, but could find no one. They also went into the bar,but no- one said anything about having left beer in the parlour. He took Higgins to the Police Station, arid he was locked up.—Corroborative evidence having been given, Mr. Rudd outlined the defence. He asked the magis- trates to say that defendant had taken all reasonable precautions to prevent drunkenness.—Defendant said he heard the door of the parlour open, and walked there from the bar and saw Higgins. He told him he had hadenough and ordered him out. Higgins asked to be allowed to have a warm, and he replied that he could havez warm or a minute and then must go. He went back to the bar and heard the parlour door open again. He went there and saw the officers entering. Higgins had sat down, but still had his bundle under his arm. He (defendact) explained the three glasses of beer to the inspector. The third belonged to a man named Doyle. They could not find him at the back, but found him in the bar, and he (defendant) told him to go to his beer. He replied that he would go in a minute.— After further evidence, the magistrates retired, and on returning into Court the Chairman said there was no doubt that defendant had committed a technical offence He might have done it out of kindness of heart. They did not take a very serious view of the offence, and he would be ordered to pay the costs, £1 17s. Gd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>MILITARY CONCERT AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance on Saturday night at the St. Helens Volunteer Hall, when the band of the 2nd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment gave a grand military concert in aid of the funds of the regimental cottage homes. Mr. Andrew Harris, L.R.A.M., con- ducted, and the programme was exceptionally interest- ing, including items by famous composers. All the selections were capitally rendered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>GLASSWORKS APPRENTICES' STRIKE.</head>
        <p>GLASSWORKS APPRENTICES' STRIKE.</p>
        <p>At the St. Helens Police Court, on Saturday, the Chief Constable mentioned to the magistrates that men had been apprehended in the town for collecting in the streets, but as they were genuine out-of-works he had released them. A body of the glassworker apprentices who were on strike had approached him and asked per- mission to collect money in the streets. He had inquired if it was not a fact that Messrs. Pilkington Bros. had urged them to come to terms and to re-consider their action with a view to a settlement,and the lads had refused. The leaders of the apprentices admitted that this waa so, and the Chief Constable amid he had told them that they wore breaking the law as apprentices. He had advised them to approach Messrs. Pilkington and endeavour to get their griev- .ancea remedied, but he could not allow them to collect publicly in the streets, and would proceed against them if they did so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>PASSIVE RESISTERS.</head>
        <p>PASSIVE RESISTERS.</p>
        <p>Ten St. Helens passive resisters were summoned at the local police court on Monday for non-payment of rates. The defendents were Dr. T. T, Best, Revs. W. Holroyd (Baptist, W. H. Jeffries (Congregational), J.Bradley (Primitive Methodist), and Norman M`Leod (Baptist), Messrs. T. Jenkinson (a local magistrate), J. E. Anderton, T. Stevenson, W. Barker,and W.Murray. All the defendants admitted that they legally owed the money. Dr. Best said that as that was the fifth time they had appeared before the magistrates, the bench was fully conversant with the reasons which impelled them to protest most strongly against being compelled to contribute to schools where sectarian doctrines were taught, to which they conscientiously objected. As the magistracy were aware, the passive resistance move- ment all over the country had already accomplished some good in that after January 1 next no religious test might he imposed upon any student. entering any training college, and they felt sure that they had the great bulk of public opinion at their back. Promises had been made by the responsible persons that in an education bill to be introduced next year the schools shall be put under popular control, and all religious tests for head teachers abolished. They must, there- fore, continue their protest, as they believed their demands were within measurable distance of being granted.—The usual orders were made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>NEW CHILDREN'S SHELTER.</head>
        <p>NEW CHILDREN'S SHELTER.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon the Mayor of St. Helens (Alderman H. Martin) opened a new children's shelter which has been established in Water-street by the com- mittee of the local Fresh-Air Fund and Pleasant Winter Evenings Association, which was founded in 1903 by Mr. John pennington.—Mr. S. Glover presided, and said they were met to inaugurate a new departure. In carry- ing out the work of their association, it was felt that it would be good if they could keep more closely in touch with the children that had passed through their hands, and have a shelter to which the children could go rather than they should roam about the streets.—The Mayor said he was glad to welcome that extension of what he knew to be a useful work. In large communi- ties like that they had, of course, a large number of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>BURTONWOOD COLLIER'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>Peter Ellison, aged sixty-four, of Fairclough-street, was admitted to the St. Helens Hospital on Monday night and died on Tuesday morning from injuries. Ellison had been employed at the Collins Green Colliery, and while at work in the pit he was struck down by a fall, and had several ribs broken. When bmoved to the hospital it was found that the broken es had penetrated the lungs, and he quickly collapsed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>CHRISTMAS TREE.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS TREE.</p>
        <p>The Christmas Tree, in aid of the funds of St. John's Church Sunday schools was held in the Mission Hall Legh-street, on Saturday. The opening ceremony was performed by Mrs. G. F. Clarke. The teachers in charge of the stalls did excellent service, as did also the Black Diamond Minstrels, with Mr. Greening as their leader, assisted by Mr. H. Foster, of Birkdale, who is always at his best as a minstrel. Captain Haslem, Church Army Evangelist, and Mr. T. Martindale, secretary, were busy from early morning with a band of willing helpers, getting the hall decorated and the tree laden with the good things that delighted the children. The vicar (the Rev. T. R. Matthews) and the Rev. Dr. Devinny were present during the afternoon and evening, and at the close the Vicar announced that the takings amounted to .£32.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>DR. BARNARDO'S HOME-.</head>
        <p>DR. BARNARDO'S HOME-.</p>
        <p>A :Tile of work was held in the Kirkfield School Newton, on behalf of these libmes on Thursday, under the management of Miss Pick and Mrs. Hartley. With the help of the pupils the room was given the appear- ance of a bazaar, stalls were erected and the place decorated and a tea room also provided. Mrs. Airey opened the sale of work, and spoke of the good cause the sale was to benefit which was Dr. Barnardo's Homes. Those institutions had done a great deal of good and were worthy of support, and she hoped a large stun of money would be raised by this effort. Mrs. Ashton, representing the Preston branch of the Homes, moved a vote of thanks to Mrs. Airey for her kindness and also thanked the parents of the pupils for the very great help they had rendered. Some very hard work indeed had been done to get the quantity of beautiful work they had there that afternoon. They had 1,155 crippled, deaf, dumb, and blind children, and it was for those they were asking for help. They also had 1,110 little babies, and others were coming to them every day, and they never sent any away wanting help. _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>-CRIPPLED GIRLS' FLORAL EXHIBITION.</head>
        <p>-CRIPPLED GIRLS' FLORAL EXHIBITION.</p>
        <p>The highly successful floral exhibition held in the Earlestown Town Hall on behalf of the Watercress and Flower Girls' Christian Mission was brought te a close on Saturday. Never before has there been such a dis- play of colour in the hall, while at the same time everything was arranged in excellent taste. The artificial flowers are made by blind and crippled girls connected with a mission founded and superintended by Mr. John Groom as far back as 186 G. Mr. Groom was to have been present at the opening of the exhibition, but his place was taken by Mr. Trott, who sketched the history and explained the work of the institution. The late Earl of Shaftesbury was its patron and warm supporter, its first object having been to promote the welfare, socially, morally, and spiritually, of the thousands of flower girls in the streets of London. From a small local effort the movement has grown till it includes an orphanage for 250 children, a convalescent home, a cottage hospital, a seaside holiday home for blind and crippled girls, and other beneficent agencies. Its principle is to put blind and crippled girls into the way of earning their own liveli- hood, and some of these were at work in the Town Hall exhibition. Mr. Collingwood, J.P., presided at the opening ceremony, and Mrs. Collingwood declared the exhibition open, and was presented with a large and beautiful artificial bouquet. A vote of thanks was then moved by the Rev. J. Ryder, seconded by the Rev. S. C. Hall, and acknowledged by Mr. Collingwood. The exhibition, was very well attended, large quantities of flowers being sold to visitors, who were also entertained by the music of Mr. Sudworth's bijou orchestra. Mr. and Mrs. Flatman and Nurse Wilmot, the managers of the exhibition, were well assisted by lady stallholders representing the various churches of the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>English Church Union,</head>
        <p>English Church Union,</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington branch of the English Chnrch Union was held in the Masonic Hall on Wednesday evening, under the presidency of Mr. John White.— The following officers were elected: Chairman, Mr. John White ; vice-chairman, Rev. Wilfrid Stanton ; secretary and treasurer, Mr. C. S. Burgess; committee, Revs. J. H. G. Hollingworth and J. Holden, Messrs. G. Egerton Warburton and H. N. Houghton.—ln his address the Chairman expressed regret at the absence of M;. H. N. Houghton who, he said, had recently undergone a severe operation. Anticline,. to the events of the past year he said the Union had to a certain extent failed in their opposition to the Deceased Wife's Sister Act. Parliament had, however, learnt the lesson that although it might alter the laws of the realm, it could not alter the laws of the Church. He hoped the clergy would have suffi- cient backbone to carry out the law of the Church in its entirety. With regard to education, there was in all probability a harder fight yet to be faced and it was to be hoped that Churchpeople would show their determination to maintain the religious character of the voluntary schools.—The Ven. Archdeacon Barber, of Chester, moved: "That it is expedient at the pre- sent time to alter our Prayer Book.' He said it was inconceivable that anyone should propose to submit the Prayer Book to the deliberations of the present House of Commons. Nothing seemly could result from such a discussion. It would be a dangerous thing to open the floodgates of criticism by any sug- gestion of alteration.—The Rev. J. H. G. Honing- worth (vicar of St. Banaabas', Warrington), expressed the view that a revision would be highly inexpedient in the present disunited state of the Church, the hostile attitude of Parliament, and the timidity shown in high quarters. -The resolution was unanimously carried.—Votes of thanks to Archdeacon Barber and the Chairman concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>Anglers at Dinner.</head>
        <p>Anglers at Dinner.</p>
        <p>The members of the Bank Quay Jolly Anglers' Society held their annual dinner on Saturday evening, at the Bowling Green Inn, Liverpool-road. The affair was a great success. Over 40 sat down to dit,ner, which was provided by the host and hostess, Mr. and Mrs. Campbell. A. smoking concert was afterwards held, the President of the society (Mr. Frederick T. Behnie) making a few remarks regarding the work of the society. The Secretary (Mr. Henry Kent) presented his report for the past year, and said the club had had a very good season, and he was very pleased to state that the accounts showed a small balance to the good. The toasts of "The King, Queen and Royal Family," "The Jolly Anglers' Society," and "The Visitors," were given. The prizes which the club gave for the best catches during the season were pre- sented by the President to the following :—A.Wood,C. Nurse, W. Melling, W. Boyle, W. Unsworth, S. Thomas, J. Allcock, 11. Leyland and A. Swarbrick. During an interval in the concert a presentation from the club members to the President, for his valuable services during the past year was made by Mr. A. Cook,of the Anglers' Association, and it took the form of a gold-mounted umbrella. Songs were rendered by Messrs. W. White, C. Nurse, J. Gorst, T. Allcock, and J. Jones. Mr. J. Gorst was the accompanist. The whistling solos by Mr. Whittaker were much enjoyed. Theft from a Shop-door.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Court on Tuesday before Messrs. J. Rymer Young (in the chair) and C. Jackson Holmes, Edward Sauce,Rose and Crown-street,was charged with stealing from outside the shop of Messrs-Scales and Sons, 1 Bridge-street, a pair of men's boots, valued at Bs. 11d., the property of Messrs. Scales, on Monday night—The Chief Constable said that the prisoner was seen out- side the shop by a girl named Alice Raderoft who saw him cut piece of string which was attached to a pair of boots and run away. The manager,Benjamin Ge,)rge Little, was at once imformed and gave chase. The prisoner ran up Rose and Crown-street and then i dropped one of the boots and attempted to strike the manager with the other.—P.C. Bennett was informed and arrested the prisoner who when charged denied any knowledge of the theft.—Prisoner said he was drunk at the time. The Chief Constable said the prisoner was a lazy man and would not work and had been forty-one times previously convicted.—The Chairman said the prisoner would have to go to gaol for two months with hard labour.</p>
        <p>For Bronchial Coughs take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure. 1/13, 2/9.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 21, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 21, 1907.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S BIG MATCH AT WILDERSPOOL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Coming. Events.</p>
        <p>That highly popular play" Sunday" will be pro- duced at the Royal Court Theatre next week. There will be matinees on Boxing Day and Saturday. Mr. F. H. Rose (of the " Clarion " staff) will address a Socialist meeting in the Co-operative Hall to-morrow (Sunday) evening. For Chronic Chest Complaint, Woods' Great Peppermint Cure.—Advt. Workhouse Medical Officer. The Warrington Guardians are taking steps to fill the vacancy caused by the resignation of the resident medical officership by Dr. T. E. Flitcroft. In our advertising columns applications are invited for the position, the salary offered being £l5O per annum and vaccination fees, with apartments, rations, and other usual allowances.</p>
        <p>The Museum. An addition of an unusually interesting kind has ' just been made to the contents of the Art Gallery, Mr. Charles Holmes, editor of the" Studio," having lent for a short time part of his collection of modern medals and plaquettes. Most, if not all, of the principal French medallists of the last twenty years are represented. MORE LIGHT AT LESS COST.—" OSraM " Electric Lamps do this and more. Full details from the local agent.—J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Colonel Hall Walker injured. 1 While hunting with the Cheshire Hounds on Thurs- day at Hargreave Green in the Chester Vale, Colonel Hall Walker was thrown at a bad drain and his horse ! fell on him. He was removed to his residence at Sandy Brow, where medical examination revealed severe injuries to the ligaments. It was at first thought his leg was broken. 1 Wycliffe Young _People's Society. _ . _</p>
        <p>-The meeting on- Mond-ty evening w-as held in the ladies' parlour, Memorial Buildings, and took the form of a social evening. Various games vere indulged In songs also being given by the Misses Armstrong and Bouchier. Mr. W. Brown contributed a violin solo, and Miss Payton and Mr. T. F. Wallington a piano- forte duet. Refreshments were served during the evening.</p>
        <p>For Children's Hacking Coughs at night, Woods Great Peppermint Cure. 1/11 2/9.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Independent_ Labour Party.</p>
        <p>Councillor James Sexton, Of Liverpool, was the speaker at the Co-operative Hall on Sunday night and gave an address on" The true meaning of Socialism." There was a good attendance. The chair was occupied by Mr. Edward Horrocks who said that Mr. Sexton had helped them in the early days of their movement in Warrington. The Lecturer illustrated how the land values had increased and particularly instanced the case of Lord Derby whose wealth has been so greatly enhanced by the progress of the community. " The Messiah."</p>
        <p>We would remind our readers that the Musical Society will present Handers great oratorio - " The Messiah "at the Parr Hall on Thursday evening next (Boxing Day). The soloists will be :—Miss Esta D'Argo, soprano ; Miss Nellie Anderton, contralto; Mr. Charles Saunders, tenor ; Mr. Albert Garcia, bass. The plan of reserved seats is now open at Mr. It. H. Pearson's, Bridge-street. A special article entitled " How to listen to The Messiah ' " will be found on page 2.</p>
        <p>Machine Cleansed CURRANTS 3d &amp;-ld lb.at ASHLEY'S Advt. Juolor Liberal Atsociation.</p>
        <p>At the meetieg of this Association held at Gregory's Caf4 on Monday evening, the consideration of 'Socialism and Liberalism" was resumed. Coun- cillor Hawthorn presided. At the outset, Mr. R. Pierce Williams replied to some of the criticisms of the previous week, his contention being that his main proposition that the present system was economically unsound, and that Liberalism offered no alternative had not been met. An interesting discussion followed, in which the Chairman, Messrs. E. England, J. Darbyshire, J. Morton, and Kendrick took part, and Mr. Williams was thanked for his paper. Tea and Concert at Orford. The annual parochial tea and entertainment in con- nection with Orford Church took place on Thursday evening and proved a great success. At the concert a highly interesting programme was gone through, including pianoforte solos by Mr. Hubert J. Iddon and Mr. Monks, a trio by Messrs. W. and J. Povey and W. Richardson, songs by Mrs. Legge and Mr. E. Smethurst,violin solos by Mr. F. Richmond,a quartette by Messrs. W. and J. Povey, J. Gibson, and W. Richardson, humorous songs by Mr. Chandler, and a dialogue by Messrs. W. Knowles, R. Howarth, P. Higham, H. Williams, and J. Swinton. The concert was followed by a dance, Mr. J. Swinton acting as M.O.</p>
        <p>4 COAGULINE," KLINK,"TENASITINE.LCements for broken and other articles.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Warrington_ A.C. v: Sankey Harriers:</p>
        <p>The start from Bridge Foot for this local " Derby " was witnessed by a large number of spectators, the keenest interest being evinced by the enthusiasts who watched the various stages of the run from points of vantage in the country. Fifteen Sankeyites and twelve Warringtonians formed the pack which followed a trail (laid by J. Parsonage and J. Spence) over about seven miles of heavy country. The result: —Warrington: 1,2, 4,5, 6—total 18; Sankey: 3,8, 9, 10, 11—total 41: gave Warrington an easy victory by 23 points. Warrington have arranged to run the ir annual Christmas handicap on Christmas Day morning, starting at 10.30. The Warrington Football Club is generously providing dressing accom- modation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>CHRISTMASTIDE.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMASTIDE.</p>
        <p>Preparations for Local Festivities.</p>
        <p>Christmastide, the season of merry-making and good cheer, is once more at hand, but were it not for the lavishness of the shop window displays it would be difficult to realize the fact. The mildness of the weather during the past few days has left very little hope of an "old-fashioned Christmas," although one never knows what change may set in at the eleventh hour. The appear- of the streets, however, affords striking indications of the approach of the great festival. Tradespeople have . vied with one another in garnishing their shop windows and displaying their stocks of seasonable wares in the most alluring ways. There is no doubt that their enterprise will be well repaid by brisk business during the next few days. The celebration of Christmas Day, will, we believe, lack nothing in whole-heartedness in Warrington, and there will be few homes, however poor, to which the joys of the season will not penetrate in some degree. At the various public institutions every effort will be exerted to make the day a happy and memorable one for the inmates. Even those whose unfortunate lot it is to be in the Workhouse will realize something of the joyousness of the occasion. As usual the dining hall will be beautifully decorated with evergreens, mottoes, &amp;c., and a round of festivities has been arranged. After divine service in the morning, dinner will be served, consisting of the old English fare of roast beef and plum pudding. The inmates will be free to spend the afternoon pleasantly and pipes and tobacco will be distributed. A " special " tea will be served and the concluding event of the day will be an impromptu smoking concert. The wards of the Work- house Infirmary will be brightly decorated and every- thing possible will be done to cheer the patients. Through the kindness of the Mayor there will be the usual dis- tribution of hot-pot and mince pies to the deserving poor at the Drill Hall between 11 and 12 noon. Those persons requiring tickets should apply to their ministers. At the Infirmary, Padgate Cottage Homes, and Orford Barracks the oelebrations will be on much the same lines as in previous years. Christmastide brings no relaxation to the Postal officials;on the contrary it means a considerable increase of work. The authorities at the Warrington Post Office have made elaborate preparations to zops with the traffic. The advance posting arrangements expire to-day (Saturday) and the special box which has been placed near the general box will be used on the 23rd and 24th only for local correspondence and will be so labelled. The town letter boxes will be cleared finally at 6 p.m. for the night mail on the 24th inst. and the final collection at the Head Office will be at 10 p.m. It is hoped in view of the Christmas pressure, that on the 21st, 22nd, 23rd. and 24th, the public will post as early as possible for the night mails. The Gymnasium will be used for parcel post services from the 19th December to the 26th December inclusive, and the Conservative Club Assembly Rooms will be used for town postmen's deliveries on the 24th, 25th and 26th December. Extra staff will be employed both on the indoor work and on collection and delivery duties. There will be a delivery of parcels to-morrow (Sunday) both in the town and rural area, wherever a delivery of letters obtains on an ordinary Sunday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
