      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>Teams and Prospects.</head>
        <p>Teams and Prospects.</p>
        <p>By" ()</p>
        <p>The famous "All Blacks "—the pick of New Zealand Rugby Footballers—will arrive at the Central Station, I Warrington this morning at 10.50 in readiness for I their match with the Cup-holders at Wilderspoold Warrington is on the qui rive and ready to accord the tourists a hearty Lancashire welcome, which will first' of all be extended by the Mayor on behalf of the borough. A four-horse 'bus will convey the New Zealanders to the Town Hall, and after the brief procaedings there they will go to the Lion Hotel to prepare for what will undoubtedly be a severe struggle for supremacy. Warrington's one regret is that the All Blacks have not reached Warrington undefeated. Had they done so the meeting between them and the local team, the holders of the Northern Union Cup, would have been fraught with particular significence. It was too much to expect, however, that any team strange to Northern Union rules, and meeting professional foot- ballers for the first time, could play two matches a week for three months, and win every time. Though they have been beaten on seven occasions out of 21 games, the All Blacks have proved themselves a great side. Some of these defeats were not a fair criterion of the merits of the losers, far apart from the awful conditions which prevailed in several matches, the tourists did not play their strongest team, and were open to the charge of saving them- selves in certain mid-week games. Against the crack Northern Union teams they have given of their best, the defeat of such clubs as Broughton Rangers, Leeds, Hull, Swinton, Huddersfield, etc., being evidence of their capabilities. On Wednesday of this week, the All Blacks played the first of their fixtures against a representative County team, and favoured for once with fine weather, they beat Yorkshire in brilliant fashion by 23 points to 4. After successive defeats in the mud at Bradford and Halifax, the All Blacks must ' have given Yorkshiremen the idea that they were a spent force—an idea rudely dispelled at the expense of the pick of the County. Fine weather and firm footing ere essential to a team like the New Zealanders, who rely so much upon speedy running and quick hand-to-hand passing. It is the hope of everybody that the God of Rain will withhold his hand for the match to-day. Warrington have a fine ground and a fine team, and the struggle between them and their Colonial visitors should provide an exposition of the best that is in Northern Union football.</p>
        <p>It is an open secret that the All Blacks are keen on adding the scalp of the Cup-holders to their belt of trophies, and no one needs telling that the Warrington men mean to uphold the proud traditions of the Club by a victory to-day. Personally, I think the War- rington men will triumph. They have lately been showing form of the highest order, and at Wilderspoll they are as difficult a team to beat as any in the country. If the All Blacks win they will have reason to be proud of the achievement.</p>
        <p>Latest reports respecting the local team are very satisfactory. The men are in the pink of condition, and even the bulky Fish has got into something approaching his proper fighting weight. The " irre- pressible's " fame has reached New Zealand, and if Jack does not prove himself to-day the classiest of wing three-quarters, the All Blacks as well as the Warrington crowd will be greatly disappointed. The following is the Warrington team, with the heights and weights of the men :</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. Height. Weight. Full back : ft. in. at. lb. J. H. Tilley 5 B,A 11 7 Threequarters : J. Fish 5 6 12 8 G. Dickenson 5 10 11 10 T. Taylor 5 8 11 10 E. Brooks 5 7 11. 7 Half backs : . .7. Jenkins 5 6 10 6 S. Lees 5 5 10 6 Forwards: A. S. Boardman 5 101 14 4 F, Shugars 5 10- 14 0 J. Belton 6 0 13 IA G. Thomas 5 8 13 0 A. Naylor 5 8 12 10 W. Harmer 5 10 12 6</p>
        <p>The whole body of the New Zealanders-28 in all —will be the guests of the Club for the week-end, but the actual team for to-day's match will not be announced until this morning, when the offinial pro- gramme is issued. The list of players is as follows :</p>
        <p>BACK. - S. Turtill (International). THREE-QUARTERS. _ _ _</p>
        <p>G. Smith (International). E. Wrigley (International). D. M`Gregor (International). J. Lavery (North Island). H. Rowe (Auckland County). FIVE-EIGHTHS. . H. Messenger (International). R. Wynyard (Auckland County). W. Wynyard ( Nuckland County). L. Todd (Auckland County). HALVES. A. Kelly (Wellington County). J. Gleeson (Napier). W. Tyler (Auckland County). E. Tyne (South Irland). FORWARDS. W. Johnson (International). T. Cross (International). W. Mackrell (international). ' E. Watkins (International). C. Pearce (South Island representative). C. Bryne (Wellington County). D. Gilchrist (Wellington County). W. Trevarthen (Auckland County). C. Dunning (North Island). H. Wright (Wellington County). A. Lile (North Island). A. Callum (Wellington County). D. C 4. Fraser (Petone). _ . „. _</p>
        <p>H. Baskerville (Wellington County). In the foregoing list it is to be noted that all the players, who are defined as North Island or South Island representatives, are really internationals in the senbe that games between England and Scotland are spoken of as international. A great misfortune befell the All Blacks in the match with Yorkshire on Wednesday, for G. W. 1 Smith, the international threequarter, and the speediest and most talented player on the side, sustained a fractured rib. Smith has played in nearly every match and displayed form of the highest order whether on attack or defence, and his place will be difficult to fill. In all probability the team will be the same as against Yorkshire, with the exception of Smith. That team was composed as follows :</p>
        <p>THE ALL BLACKS. Height. Weight. Full back: ft. in. st. lb. H. Turtill. 5 9 11 5 Three-quarters: H. Messenger. 5 8 12 0 G. W. Smith. 5 7 12 2 H. Rowe. 5 9 12 2 Five Eighths. L. Todd. 5 7 10 0 E. Wrigley. 5 9 13 7 Half-back: R. Wynyard. 5 8 11 10 Forwards: H. Wright (captain). 5 11 13 8 A. Lile. 5 11 14 0 C. Pearce. 5 10 14 5 T. Gilchrist. 511 13 3 T. Cross. 6 0 14 7 W. Johnston. 6 1 13 8</p>
        <p>Probably Messenger will take Smith's place, and W. Wynyard or MacGregor fill the vacancy. It will be noted that the All Blacks adhere to their five- eighths formation., but on the field of play the differ- ence in the styles of the two teams will not be very marked. The New Zealanders' record to date is as follows':— For. Against. Opponents. G. T. PTS. G. T. PT'S:. Bromley Huddersfield Widnes 4 6 26 ... 1 3 11 Broughton Rangers Wakefield Trinity 1 1 5 ... 1 1 5 Leeds St. Helens Merthyr Tydvil 3 7 27 ... 0 3 9</p>
        <p>Keighley 0 3 9 ... 2 1 7 Wigan 1 2 8 ... 0 4 12 Barrow 0 1 3 ... 0 2 6 Hull Leigh Oldham 2 1 7 ... 1 2 8 Runcorn Dewsbury and Batley Swinton Rochdale Hornets 9 5 19 ... 0 0 0 Bradford Halifax 2 0 4 ... 0 3 9 Yorkshire</p>
        <p>51 61 285 ... 23 38 160</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>alist."</head>
        <p>alist."</p>
        <p>Played. Won. Lost. Drawn. 21 13 7 1</p>
        <p>The International and other representative games which the New Zealanders have yet to contest have now been definitely arranged as follows : Jan. I.—New Zealand v. Wales at Aberdare (first International). „ B.—New Zealand v. Cumberland, at Workington. „ 11.—New Zealand v. England, at Wigan (second International). „ 18.—New Zealand v. Lancashire, at Oldham. „ 25.—New Zealand v. Northern Union. at Leeds (first Test). Feb, B.—New Zealand v. Northern Union, at Chelsea (second Test). „ 15.—New Zealand v. Northern Union, at Chelten- ham (third Test). After to-day's match both teams will sit down to " high "tea at the Lion Hotel, to which 75 invitations have been issued. Amongst those who are expected to be present are Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., and Mr. A.H, Crosfield, M.P.,and opportunity will be taken by the committee to present each gentleman with a hand- somely-framed photo of the team who gained honours at Broughton last April. when they won the Northern Union Challenge Cup. The New Zealanders will witness the second per- formance at the Hippodrome on Saturday, by the kind invitation of Mr. Frank Macnaghten. on Sunday the Colonials will drive to Knutsford, and dine at the Royal George Hotel, whilst prior to returning to Manchester at noon a visit will be made to Messrs. Crosfield's works. The local executive have been very energetic in making all the necessary arrangements not only for the comfort of the Colonials, but also for what is fully expected to be a record crowd. Improvements as regards seeing capacity have been effected at the head- quarters at Wilderspool, whilst the stands have been subjected to a minute inspection for fear of accident. The rails which surround the playing arena have also been strengthened. Given fine weather there should be a record crowd for the ground and the receints ought to approach £5OO-70 per cent. of which, of course, goes to the New Zealanders.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>ALL BLACKS' WAR-CHANT.</head>
        <p>THE ALL BLACKS' WAR-CHANT.</p>
        <p>After the welcoming cheer from the Warrington team the MI Blacks will give their impressive war- chant, which is as follows :</p>
        <p>Ka mate; ka mate; ka ora ka ora ; ka mate; ka mate ; ka oro ka ora; Tenei to tangata; Puhuru huru; Nana e pike mai. Whaka whiti to ra ; Hnpanei Hupanei Hupanei Hupanei; Whiti to ra.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>TIZ tNSIATION.</head>
        <p>TIZ tNSIATION.</p>
        <p>There is going to be a fight between us, may it mean death to you and life to us. We will fight on until one side is vanquished; so long as the daylight lasts we are here to continue battle, to be either killed or to be victorious. -- THE MEMBER UNABLE TO COME.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pemberton, treasurer of the Football Club, has received a letter from Mr.A. H.Crosfield,H.P., Penzance, in which he says :—" Were I not pre-engaged on my honeymoon I should not have let anything else pre- vent my coming to Warrington for the great event." After referring to the prowess of the New Zealanders Mr. Crosfield adds: "I hope you will have a magni- ficent match, and may the best side win ; and if that side should chance to be our own stalwart Warring- onians as we are on our own native heath, I hope our cousins from New Zealand will not grudge us the victory."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>WHEN THE MA.ORIS WERE HERE.</head>
        <p>WHEN THE MA.ORIS WERE HERE.</p>
        <p>The last New Zealand team which visited Warring- ton was " All Black " in the sense that the players were native Maoris, whereas the present tourists are whites, though the two Wynyards are nephews to two Maori players of the same name. The Maori team visited Warrington on Thursday, January 17th, 1889, and defeated the Warrington Club at Wilderspool by 2 goals, 1 try, to 1 try, 2 minors. One of the Wynyards who played threequarter scored all three tries for the Maoris, who were a fine body of men and clever footballers. At the time of their visit to Warrington they had played 43 matches against Rugby Union Clubs, won 20, lost 13, and drawn 4. The team played three matches a week frequently. The Warrington match was witnessed by 5,000 people, the receipts being £l4B. The teams which did duty on that memorable occasion were : Maoris: W. Warbrick, full back ; McCausland, Wynyard, Madigan, threequarters ; Elliot, Keogh, and Gage, half-backs ; Taiarva, Williams, Rene, Stewart, Ellison, Lee, S. Wynyard and Kauraria, forwards. Warrington: J. W Evans, full back ;J. Jolley, T. Barnes, J. T. Massey, threequarters ; W. Antrobus and W. Speakman, half backs ; Frank Turner, W. Nevins, W. Povey, 14. Littlewood, Fair Turner, H. Ashton, C. Unsworth, W. Randles, and W. Dillon (captain), forwards.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S PERFORMANCE AT OLDHAM. When the All Blacks played the undefeated Oldham team they gave a fine display, and ran the home club to a single point, though the conditions were all against the style of football that the All Blacks favour. On Saturday last, Warrington appeared at Oldham, and made an equally gallant attempt to lower the home club's colours. After a great game, in which the teams were very equally matched, Oldham triumphed by 8 points to 5 points. The least luck would have turned the score the other way. Neither of Oldham's tries was worth a cheer, while the one Fish scored for Warrington brought down the house. Near the end, Brooks was thought to have scored, but the touch judge ruled that he had stepped in touch. Every man on the Warrington side played in tip-top form, though the forwards were beaten slightly in the loose. The matches with New Zealand and Warring- ton are among the severest Oldham have had at Watersheddings, and strong team that they are, they may account themselves lucky in coming out of the fray on top each time. The Northern League table up-to-date is as follows:— For. A gst. Per- P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. c't'-ge. Hunslet Oldham Wigan l2 9 2 1 28 41 179 16 13 71 79•16 Keighley l4 11 3 0 32 32 160 20 15 85 7858 Broughton Hull l4 9 5 0 32 33 163 20 16 88 6485 Sal ford Warrington Halifax Batley l5 9 6 038 41 199 18 28 126 60•00 Hull K. R. Bradford l4 8 6 0 21 30 132 16 30 122 5714 Barrow Wakefield 1' l2 6 5 1 27 32 150 20 22 10G 5416 Leigh Dewsbury l3 6 7 0 18 21 99 22 27 125 46'15 Runcorn Leeds Rochdale l2 4 8 0 15 19 87 26 27 133 33'33 Bramley York 'l4 4 10 0 23 29 133 46 40 212 2857 Huddersfield Ebbw Vale l4 3 10 1 10 14 62 23 47 187 25.00 St. Helens l4 3 10 1 15 19 87 24 60 228 ?.5•00 Swinton Widnes „ 14 210 210 23 89 23 44 178 2142 Merthyr</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>WARRINGTON NEEDLEWORK GUILD,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON NEEDLEWORK GUILD,</p>
        <p>This Guild has done exceedingly good work in supplying garments for distribution amongst the poor of the town. During the past year, n') fewer than 83 garments were sent in which, with the blankets and clogs, made a total of 681. These were distributed by the clergy of all denominations. On Thursday afternoon the annual display of garments in connection with the Guild was held in the Town Hall, and upwards of 600 articles were on view. During the afternoon the exhibition was visited by a large number of townspeople. Great praise is due to the following officials for the warm interest they had taken in the preparations for the display :—President, Lady Greenall ; vice-president, Mrs. Harrison ; executive committee, the Mayoress of Warrington, Mrs, Armitage, Miss Dutton, Mrs. E. J. Fox, Mrs. Ferguson, Mrs. Long, Miss Massy, Mrs. iVlonro, Mrs. Pickrnere, Miss Pierpoint, Mrs. Roberts, Mrs. Bolton, Mrs. J. L. Tunstall, Mrs. G. Venn, Mrs. Lyon Whittle, Mrs. J. E. Wright ; hon. secretary and treasurer, Mrs. Fairclough, Gatesgarth, Latchford.</p>
        <p>Just a little wetting, Just a little sneeze, Just a little tightness, Just a little wheeze. Just an undertaker, Stealthy, quiet, sure, Who got SO mad because they had Wood's Great Peppermint Cure.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
