      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>Rev. Sidney Pitt and Councillor Evans' statement.</head>
        <p>Rev. Sidney Pitt and Councillor Evans' statement.</p>
        <p>Sir —Assuming that I am the minister mentioned ' several times in the recent meeting of the Council the worthy Councillors who did me that honour can hardly have read my remarks to the end or they would surely have made some reference to the Huddersfield case which it was my main endeavour to lay before my hearers. May I therefore repeat that in this manufacturing town where women are employed much more generally than in Warrington the infantile death rate has been reduced from 138 to 85 and during the third quarter of the current year to 62. This has been accomplished by sympathetic co-operation between the Health Committee and a voluntary association by which it is assisted. The figures as to the twenty-seven manufacturing towns were not accessible to me but I apprehend none of us can find ' any satisfaction in the fact that there are twenty-seven towns in the country where things are as bad or worse. What we all want is to make them better here, and there seems to be a clear indication of the way in!</p>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publication must be written on one side only of the paper and sent in, if possible, not -later than Thursday.</p>
        <p>which this may be done. If we may gather from Councillor Evans' statement that it is intended to adopt the Notification of Births Act and to appoint a health visitor specially charged with this matter, these are important and critical steps for which the prompti- tude of the Council should be duly acknowledged. The next thing is the voluntary association. Whether this should be formed for the purpose or whether the work can be done by some extension of existing societies I cannot say. That something of the kind should be attempted is only too clear. I can if desired supply full details of the working of the Huddersfield Public Health Union as Alderman Broadbent, the main mover in the matter there, has been kind enough to send m the information. In the meantime may I ask you to publish the accompanying sketch map that the position may be clearly before the eyes of your readers. I am,dear Sir, very truly yours _</p>
        <p>SIDNEY PITT.</p>
        <p>follavv His advice will not lead us into "greater evils." "SPERO BIELIORA."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. C. E. Parker puts the question" Is interest robbery ? "iii your issue of December 14th and asks Mr. Grayson or some Warrington Socialists to make the question more plain. With your kind permission I will endeavour to do so.</p>
        <p>Mr. Parker's point is, if interest is robbery then Socialism will gain the day. Ifindividual capital can be proved to be the enemy of labour and not the friend the sooner we put it in State chains the better. Mr. Parker uses an illustration of a special investment of £lOO in a horse and cart which I will deal with later on in my letter. I know Mr. Parker wants an answer straight to the point so I don't propose to go round corners.</p>
        <p>As a Socialist I believe the present form of owner- ship of capital divides society into two great classes; one of these classes,the wage-earning, the proletariat is, obliged to pay to the other, the capitalist, a sort of tax, in order to be able to live at all, and exercise its faculties to any degree. Here is a multitude of human beings, they possess nothing only labour, they can live only by their work. But in order to work they need. an expensive equipment which they have not got. Another class owns the means of production, the land, the factories, the machines, the raw materials and accumulated capital in the form of money, and naturally, this capitalist and possessing class taking advantage of its power makes the working and non- owning class pay a large profit. It does not rest con- tent after it has been reimbursed for the advances it made,and has repaired the wear and tear of machinery; it levies every year and indefinitely a considerable tax. on the product of the workman and fume?, in the form of rent for farm, ground rent of land in the cities, taxes for the payment of public debt, industrial profit, commercial profit and interest on stocks and bonds. Therefore in our present society the work of the workers is not their exclusive property, and since in our society founded on intensive production, economic activity is an essential funotion of every worker, as work forms an Integral part of personality the proletarian does not own his own body absolutely, the proletarian alienates a part of his activity, that is a part of his being, for the profit of another class. The rights of men are incomplete and mutilated in him; he cannot perform a single act of his life without submitting to this restriction of his rights, this alienation of his individuality. The worker can neither work nor eat, clothe nor shelter himself without paying a sort of ransom to the capitalist and owning class.</p>
        <p>IThen, again, where does the liberty of those who pro- duce this capital come in? Are they consulted as to what part they shall play in the industrial system? No. They are totally under the caprice of the capitalist ; without their consent and often with- out their knowledge the capital which they have created undertakes or abandons this or that enterprise. In other words, labour is doubly enslaved, since it is directed towards ends which it has not willed by means which it has not chosen, and so the same capitalist system which exploits the labour of the workman restricts the liberty of the labourer, nor is this all. Capital is only warned of its mistakes through crises the terrible consequences of which often fall upon the proletariats so by the extreme of injustice the working classes are socially responsible for the carrying on of Production which they have no share in regulating. To have responsibilty without authority, to be punished without even being consulted—such is the paradoxical fate of the Proletariat under the capitalist disorder. The above analysis of the operation of capital proves to my mind that those who are subjected to the exploitation of capital are those who proeuce the interest and profit which they are alienated from to a marked degtee. The division of wealth proves the latter statement. This proves to my mind that rent interest and profit is robbery. Individual capital is the enemy of labour and until collectively owned and controlled will continue to be so. I understand Victor Grayson was speaking of what Socialists would do to bring about this collective con- trol. When he spoke of the .£lOO investment it was to point out that immediately you invest you do so for interest ; this interest must be produced by labour. But suppose I deal here with Mr. Parker's parable of the horse and cart. Mr. Parker says if the original possessor of the .£lOO exchanged it himself for a horse and cart he lets the horse and cart out to hire ; the hire he receives includes wear and tear and interest on his £lOO. My answer is as follows :—The real value of an article is its cost of production. The wear and tear are caused by the user of the horse and cart, and the cost of the loss on the part of the lender would be the legitimate price to charge the user of the horse and cart. Should any other interest be charged it would be put upon the cost of production and the consumer would be charged a price over the real value, and I suppose anyone who does not -,et value for money would say he had been robbed. I expect horses and carts used as instruments of production under Socialism would not be individually owned but collectively owned and used for the common good. If a man spent £lOO of his savings to possess a horse and cart he would do so for his private use. Neither would there be any need for him to sacrifice his private use of the horse and cart for the man with the barrow. If any sacrifice is to be made the whole community must make it.— Yours truly,</p>
        <p>_ GEORGE REEVES, 17. Gibon-strpet,Stockton Heath.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>Devonian Association.</head>
        <p>Devonian Association.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Being desirous of forming a local branch of the above association, I shall be greatly obliged if you will kindly insert this letter in your columns calling the attention of all West Countrymen in the district and if they will communicate with me I shall be glad to call a meeting at an early date, when we can discuss the matter in detail.—Yours truly, J. P. HIIXTABLE.</p>
        <p>9, Arpley-street, Warrington. December 17th 1907.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>Nursing Association—The Mayor's Offer.</head>
        <p>Nursing Association—The Mayor's Offer.</p>
        <p>Sir,—At the beginning of this month I had the honour of presidingibt a meeting called in support of the Warrington Nursing Association and my attention being called to the urgent want of funds to enable the committee to carry on the work of this most useful institution I made an offer of £2O towards the funds on condition that 14 other persons would contribute the like amount so as to raise the sum of £3OO, the amount required to relieve the Institution from debt. At the meeting Mr. Henry Roberts volunteered to become one of the 14 subscribers and since the meeting Mr. W. P. Rylands, Messrs. Greenall Whitley and Co., Limited and Mr. Alderman Forshaw have offered to become contributors.</p>
        <p>I am particularly anxious to find 10 other persons willing to join in raising the MOO. Now lam sure that I have many friends who will help me and I feel that I need only call their attention to the valuable and beneficent work done by the Warrington Nursing Association and which is such a boon to the poor of the town to have a ready response to my appeal for help.—l am, sir, your obedient servant, JAnEs SgETEIURST, Mayor. Town Hall, Warrington, 20th December 1907. Warricgton Children's Summer Camp.</p>
        <p>Sir,—At—this season of the year one section of the community stands out from all others in striking con- trast.—The little ones ! If you happen to hear fathers and mothers, and sympathising friends, talking of Christmas," the little ones" will occupy a big place in their consideration. \Vbat weask your readers to con- sider in this communication, thrown broadcast, is that they will think kindly of the little ones we are so anxious to help. During the pAst two seasons nearly 1,000 poor chil- dren (boys and girls) from Warrington, have been given a week's holiday at the seaside. At the close of the season 1900 the establishment expenses necessitated the committee incurring an over- draft at the Bank. Ten Warrington gentlemen have very kindly pledged their credit to the bank to secure this sum. A portion has been paid off during this year. We are anxious to clear the balance off before commencing the work of next season. Will you help us? If anyone should happen to send too much it will not matter ! The overplus will come in nicely to strengthen the funds at the committee's disposal for general purposes. There are no paid officials in con- nection with this work. The only persons who receive remuneration are the cook and caretaker,</p>
        <p>We would add that remittances may he sent to yours faithfully, JOHN A. RANSOME, Hon. Treasurer.</p>
        <p>Shirley, Birchdale, Warrington. W. H. J. JENKINS, Hon. Secretary. Bank Chambers, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>"Is Interest Robbery P"</head>
        <p>"Is Interest Robbery P"</p>
        <p>Sir,—On taking up the "Examiner -7' on Saturday I was surprised to had a letter from that well-known correspondent, Mr. C. E. Parker, which firmly convinces me as to the impossibility of cornering Mr. Parker, because he is to my mind one of those persons who I will not be cornered, and keeps drifting away from the main points at issue, as shown in the controversy on Christ and Socialism with John Workman. His letter in Saturday's issue is perhaps a sample of Liberalism as Mr. Parker knows it. Writing on "Is interest robbery ?" he strikes a very hard point by beginning his letter by gaying he has just returned from the Parr Hall Socialis- tic meeting, at which there was ample opportunity of him ascertaining from Mr. Grayson M.P. or some other Socialist the answer to the question he asks without leaving the meeting and rushing to press to get an anwser. I hop ,1 that the next time Mr. Parker goes to a Socialist meeting and wanti to floor the speaker ho will have the courtesy to do so while the speaker is there, and not when he has gone away.—l remain yours, "A WHITE SLAVE."</p>
        <p>Sir,—May T be permitted to say a few words in reply to Mr. C. E. Parker on this subject. Mr. C. E. Parker gives us an illustration of a man who buys a horse and cart, which he can either use himself or hire out for money or interest, and if a man hires it out, Mr. Parker says, " What of this bloated capitalist' so-called? Why should ho not have his hire or interest ? If he had his horse and cart at home he could use it in helping him to do his own work. He sacrifices (sic) this advantage and gets instead the hire or interest." - S«crifices ! that is the point ! the man sacrifices his privilege of toiling each day and merely accepts in return the money that the other man works for. . _</p>
        <p>An equation is not altered by multiplying each side by the same number, nor can the truth or otherwise of this argument be effected by the same process ; so we will say that the man has 100 horses and carts and sacrifices the (this time rather questionable) advantage of using 100 horses and carts himself and accepts as compensation the money that 100 men have worked for. No, I\ Fr. Parker, we Socialists cannot stand by and see generosity ! the sacrifice is too great ! But what of the man who hires the horse and cart ? .llr. Parker says, "It is clear if the hirer does nut con- sider it to his advantage to have the use of this horse and cart he would not hire it." My answer is this -- Ido not believe that it is right that I, as a worker, should have to hawk my labour on the market like a pot or a pan and sell it ta the most powerful bidder (to quote Grayson) but strange as it may seem to Mr. Parker, I would rather do so than starve, in other words I consider it to my advantage, under the present conditions, to work for the capitalist but that does not blind me to the evils of the system. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>In closing, I would like to ask Mr. Parker one ques- tion, as I have seen this gentleman in the chair at a great many Christian Meetings. If to receive interest is not wrong, why did Christ denounce it? (Luke VI. 34-5.) Surely He had some reason and surely trying to</p>
      </div>
