 the whole of the *H .. *Hull Kingston R. 4 523 York ..... 0 1 3 tower would be likely to fall down. Addressing the *Bradford .. 1 2 8 Wakefield Trin. 0 2 6 Volunteers, with regard to the new scheme Colonel *Salford Edelstensaid : You can take it from me—and I think *Oldham I can claim [have never deceived you—that it is all Broughton R. right. There is ; *Runcorn 0 4 12 Widnes .. 0 0 0 NO TRAP ' Hull in it. It will I.ftict, yo i vary little indeed. On the *Leigh other hand it will raise you in the ti iti)nal estimation, *Keighley 0 3 9 Batley for the scheme places your services to better account *Rochdale LI. and dismisses for ever the possibility of the onprobrious Merthyr Tydvil... 0 3. 9 *Ebb Vale 0 2 6 epithets which used to be showered on the Volunteers aad have died hard. You may say: "If there is to be Warrington Fail to Check Oldham. so little change, why all this fuss ? " Well, it is like this. l'he miehine is to be diffe.•ent. It is to be re-modelled, and it wfll be a more effective eegine of war. Bat the cemp meat parts—you are the component parts—will remain mulch the same cud the strain upon them will be aim ast identically the seals as it was in the old machine, especfelly if we get what we look for—.t little increased lubrication of these component parts with what shall n mt. call local spirit, for fear should be misandersteod—(l. augater) —bat beet emul- sion. (Applause). GENE t 4,14 120 r 3 coaes. Before distributing the prizes, Geaeral Sir C. J. Burnett delivered a saort address. He seid he knew the Warringtou Corps because he took the trouble to inspect it and, as it were, to take it on the hop. (Laughter). He did not think the limrps was very well pleased when he came down to see them at Llanberis the first day they were in camp. (Laughter). When he inspected troops he liked to take them when they least expected it and thee he knew for a certainty what their value was. (Applause). He inspeoted the War- ringtOu Corps and he would take the opportunity of saying publicly that all he saw of them pleased him very much indeed. (Applause). If all Volunteer Corps came up to the same standard, there woull be very little reason to grumble at the Volimteer force. (Applause.) Colonel Edelsten had told them that the new scheme was about to come into force and therefore the Volunteers were going to die. (L ughter). The Volunteers could never die—(applause) —and it must be so, if service in the new Imperial Army of the second line was to be voluntary. It was only a transition and the change was only in name. Woatever credit might be due to the framers of the scheme, the honour of making it possible rested with the present Volunteer force. (Applause.) Had it not been for the Volunteer force the scheme in its present aspect could never have seen the light of day. It was quite correct to say that for the success of the scheme much depended upon the large employers, because if they did not look wi:h a favourable eye upon Volunteering, then Volunteering became in all cases difficult and in most cases almost impossible. But there was another very i Inportant element—in his view it was the most important of all—which went to make up the success of the scheme, and that was the ladies. (Laughter.) Not lone ago he was in Japan, and he would -like the ladies present to understand that it was the ladies of Japan who made the patriotism of Japan. Patriotism and discip- line were taught in the cradle, and that was the reason why in such an incredible short time—unparalleled in the history of the world—,Japan' from a comparatively- speaking obscure position, had raised herself to the rank of a first class power. (Applause ) It was by patriotism alone that empires were founded, and it was by real practical patriotism that they could be main- tained. . He sincerely hoped the Volunteers of Lanca- shire were to a man going to join the new territorial force, and that they were going to carry with them the same zeal, energy, and patriotism, the same devotion to King and country which had characterised their career as Volunteers. If they did that there could be no doubt about the success of the scheme The speaker showed how under the present system the Volunteers were -hampered because their organization lacked cohesion; and they were without cavalry and engineers, and most of their guns were obsolete. In modern" warfare fair troops with a good organization would beat g mod troope without organization. (Applause.) When the new seheyne came into force they would have artillery. engineers, cavalry, and medical units all properly organized, and•if it were enthusiastically taken up he was sure the Volunteers would be able to give a good account of. themselve when called _upon. (Applause.) THE PRIZE LIST. General Sir C. J. Barnett then distributed the prizes as fellows : WINNER OF BATTALION SHIELD.—" D" or Captain H. Owen's Company; 2nd Best Company in Battalion, Winners of silver bowl, Mounted Infantry (Captain P. N. Avery's) Company. BEST SHOT OF STA.FF SERGEANTS AND SERGEANTS.— Sergeant Drummer J. W. Plinston, A. Company. ([ledge of crossed rifles and crown surrounded by a scroll of bay leaves).' BEST SHOT OF CORPORALS AND PRIVATES.—Private W. Payton, E. Company. (Badge of crossed rides and star surrounded by a scroll of bay leaves). SECTION COMMANDERS BEST SFIOOTING COMPANY.— Badge of 'crossed rifles and crown. Last year's winners. BEST SHOTS (each winner being awarded badge of crossed rifles and star).—A Company, Colour-Sergeant T. Jolley; B Company, Sergeant E. Howard; C Com- pany, Lance-Corporal W. Hill; D Company, Bugler W. Miller • E Company, Sergeant T. Whitfield; F. Com- pany, Sergeant J. Lloyd; 0 Company, Private J. Bate; fi Company, Sergeant o.Ridyard; I Company, Corporal .1. Barker: Mounted Infantry Company, Sergeant P. Magian; Cyclist Company, Lance-Corporal F. Allen. WINNERS OF COMPANY CUPS.—A Company Challenge Cup for general efficiency, Colour-Sergeant T. Jolley; B Company, general efficien